HT_DATA.load("detail", {"k2h2lvhzMDc":{"wy":"Repeatedly called one of the most useful talks on building organizations and managing creative teams.","q":{"x":"one of the most useful talks of all time for building organizations is by Ed Catmull (of Pixar)","i":13575700,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13575700,"author":"danielvf","month":"2017-02","story":"Ask HN: What are some 'must watch' talks?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Very useful talk on building organizations","quote":"one of the most useful talks of all time for building organizations is by Ed Catmull (of Pixar)","text":"Rich Hickey's talk on simplicity is a must watch. [LINK] And one of the most useful talks of all time for building organizations is by Ed Catmull (of Pixar) [VIDEO]"},{"id":4821293,"author":"pooriaazimi","month":"2012-11","story":"What they don't tell you about being a game developer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pixar learned from its successes; Toy Story 2 crunch story","quote":"It's a long talk by Ed Catmull, but I can't recommend it enough.","text":"It's \"The Pixar Story\"[1]. A great documentary, highly recommended. But Pixar wasn't all roses either - during the tight production of Toy Story 2 (because they changed almost all of the story and had to rush it for christmas), a couple (who were both animators) forgot that their 1-year old was in the car (and not in the day care!), and the girl was there for almost 10 hours... She lived, but still a pretty horrible story. The source for this unpleasant anecdote is [2]. It's a long talk by Ed Catmull, but I can't recommend it enough. It's really, really awesome . Success hides problems (Ed Catmull) Want to know why Pixar was awesome for soooo long? It wasn't luck, and it wasn't because they had great animators. It wasn't even (entirely) because they told terrific stories. It was because they constantly learned from their \"successes\"... Watch (or listen) to Catmull's talk. I guarantee that you won't regret the 55 minutes you spend on it... [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2798508,"author":"henryprecheur","month":"2011-07","story":"A conversation with Ed Catmull","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture with many gems from a developer turned manager","quote":"I highly recommend it, many gems from a brilliant developer turned brilliant manager.","text":"Keep Your Crises Small, [VIDEO] It's a lecture from Ed Catmull. I highly recommend it, many gems from a brilliant developer turned brilliant manager."},{"id":2421468,"author":"alexqgb","month":"2011-04","story":"Microsoft's office: Why insiders think top management has lost its way","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Surface problems early rather than ship issues","quote":"Here he is, giving a really good talk at Stanford Business School","text":"I believe Ed Catmull refers to this as 'keeping your crises small.' That is to say, it's much better to actively surface problems before they leave the shop than it is to ship with issues, and try to deal with implosions after the fact (ahem, Vista). Here he is, giving a really good talk at Stanford Business School, in which he covers this (among other good ideas). About an hour, but worth much more. [VIDEO] Interestingly, an ethos like this suggests that the fate of Microsoft is NOT inevitable. It's only becomes that way when you no longer care about shipping garbage. Exhibit B: Detroit in the 70's and 80's."},{"id":1377918,"author":"10ren","month":"2010-05","story":"Animating a Blockbuster: How Pixar Built Toy Story 3","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pixar co-founder's business talk at Stanford","quote":"fantastic business talk by Ed Catmull (pixar co-founder) at Stanford","text":"fantastic business talk by Ed Catmull (pixar co-founder) at Stanford [VIDEO] btw: I find it hard to believe that Toy Story 3 could be any good (because of the \"3\"). Is it?"},{"id":14805930,"author":"hodgesrm","month":"2017-07","story":"Things I’ve Learned from Reading IndieHackers","stance":"rec","why":"Good teams work through inevitable product problems","quote":"There's a great speech by Ed Catmull that makes this point well.","text":"It's worth thinking about the team in advance, especially to understand what things you do well and where you need complementary talents. Good teams also work through the problems that are inevitable in new products. There's a great speech by Ed Catmull that makes this point well. ( [VIDEO] ) Of course this is useless advice if you are doing a one-person business. :)"},{"id":12654260,"author":"bla2","month":"2016-10","story":"Google Canceled the Launch of a Robotic Arm","stance":"rec","why":"Catmull explains how success can hide problems","quote":"Ed Catmull presents it very well here","text":"Google seems to be doing ok to me. I like your point that success can hide problems though; Ed Catmull presents it very well here: [VIDEO] It looks like people were suggesting that Google takes this advice more to heart six years ago already: [LINK]"},{"id":5765795,"author":"6ren","month":"2013-05","story":"Inside Pixar's Leadership","stance":"rec","why":"Long talk on Pixar leadership","quote":"Long. Worth it.","text":"Ed Catmull, Pixar: Keep Your Crises Small , Stanford Graduate School of Business [VIDEO] (54:11) Long. Worth it."},{"id":2594149,"author":"panic","month":"2011-05","story":"Ideas Matter","stance":"rec","why":"Successful films come from thousands of ideas and good teams","quote":"The entire video is worth watching","text":"Ed Catmull, president of Pixar, has made the same comment about their films. The entire video is worth watching, but the quote in question is at [VIDEO] - The other thing to note is, there is a confusion, I think, that the people have and is — the books and press kind of work this way in that we think about \"an idea.\" When we think of ideas for movies, we think about ideas for products. And it's usually thought of as some singular thing. But the reality is, these successful movies — as with all successful products — have got thousands of ideas. It's just all sorts of things necessary to make it be successful. And you have to get most of them right to do it. And that's why you need a team that works well together."},{"id":1455618,"author":"10ren","month":"2010-06","story":"Pixar’s leaders want to make their creative powerhouse outlast them","stance":"rec","why":"Catmull on Pixar's creative review process","quote":"Great video by Catmull to Stanford business school on it","text":"Article is a little shallow. Great video by Catmull to Stanford business school on it: [VIDEO] (54 minutes) And here's an article by him: [LINK] e.g. Toy Story 2 was an impetus to creating the present review system; the reason for saying 5 good things is just to make the people feel better about the criticisms: the last thing you want to do after years of exhausting work is point out what sucked. Catmull is the main guy driving these standards; Lasseter is busy being a creative; BTW: I thought I'd discerned a hidden pattern of pixar having the writer voice the primary mentor role - Brad Bird voiced Edna Mode in The Incredibles ; Andrew Stanton voiced Crush in Finding Nemo - but... Brad Bird didn't voice Gusteau in Ratatouille"},{"id":6492133,"author":"Arjuna","month":"2013-10","story":"The Hacker News Generation (Afraid of Hard Work)","stance":"reference","why":"Catmull recounts Toy Story 2 crunch and its costs","quote":"Ed Catmull recounted this story about the production of Toy Story 2","text":"Phrases like, \"The myth of burn out\" and \"Burn out is just a rationalization for giving up early\" give me great pause. Burn out is real . It is dangerous, and even an overwhelming, driving passion for your work, your creation, can lead to disaster. Ed Catmull recounted this story about the production of Toy Story 2 [1]: \"So we came back, John [Lasseter] told the story crew to take a good rest over the holidays, and come back on January 2nd... we were re-boarding the movie. We had 8 months left. We then started this incredibly intense effort to get this movie out. It was boarded quickly, it was pitched to the company, it was an electrifying pitch. We had a lot of over-achieving people working for over-achieving managers to get the movie out. We worked brutal hours with this. When I say \"brutal\", we had a number of people that were injured with RSI [2], one of them permanently left the field. We had, actually, a married couple that worked there. This was in June, so it's summer, and the father was supposed to drop the baby off at daycare, but forgot; don't know why... but came and left the baby in the car, and came into work. Again, as the heat was rising, the mother asked... they realized and they rushed out: the baby was unconscious. The right thing was done, they put ice-water on the baby. The baby ended up being fine in the end, but it was one of those traumatic things, like, 'Why did this happen, are they working too hard?' So when I say it was intense, it really was inten"},{"id":3634637,"author":"evanlong","month":"2012-02","story":"My Google Interview Experience","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"My Google interview experience: Google Internship January 2008: Had a phone screen. First question as I heard it: “Given a binary tree tell me if it is a binary tree?” I was a bit confused and said I didn’t understand the question and requirements. Given A tell me if it’s A. Yes? The interviewers response to my confusion was: “Do you even know what a binary tree is?” Yes, I do. When he restated the question as “given a binary tree tell me if it’s a valid binary search tree” I understood the requirements efffed up something in the implementation though. Then was asked to do another problem. Something with number sequence and iteration. Got told “division is slow” so don’t use it. Couldn’t see a solution without division on the spot. Then got asked if I know about Java serialization. My response: “implements Serializable” and you can write out or read in objects but I have never really used it in any serious way before. I got the reject. Which I expected more or less. Kind of frustrating since I felt the interview was pretty much done in the first few minutes since I was asked: “do you even know what a binary tree is.” Kind of an uphill battle from my perspective. YouTube February 2010: Applied for YouTube because I saw an ad in gmail. Why not. 2 phone interviews. And 4 onsite. I thought I did ok solved everything to the best of my ability. Frustrated that I didn’t get it. Really enjoyed it. Seemed like a lot of fun. One interviewer told me my current job sounded boring. Not at"},{"id":2318925,"author":"zachbeane","month":"2011-03","story":"Ideas don’t make you rich. The correct execution of ideas does.","stance":"reference","why":"Catmull on good teams versus good ideas","quote":"If you give a mediocre idea to a good group, they'll fix it","text":"Reminds me of Ed Catmull's talk about Pixar management at [VIDEO] -- \"If you have a good idea and you give it to a mediocre group, they'll screw it up. If you give a mediocre idea to a good group, they'll fix it, or they'll throw it away and come up with something else.\""},{"id":1404603,"author":"zachbeane","month":"2010-06","story":"The Value of Ideas","stance":"reference","why":"Catmull on good teams versus good ideas","quote":"If you give a mediocre idea to a good group, they'll fix it","text":"Reminds me of Ed Catmull's talk about Pixar management at [VIDEO] -- \"If you have a good idea and you give it to a mediocre group, they'll screw it up. If you give a mediocre idea to a good group, they'll fix it, or they'll throw it away and come up with something else.\""}],"r":[["jo_B4LTHi3I",1],["6K8NkJpUei4",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["AxCo2bE9Gtk",1],["vYA0f6R5KAI",1]]},"2Op3QLzMgSY":{"wy":"Seen as a classic and often the moment programming 'clicks'; commenters praise Abelson's opening that computer science is not about computers.","q":{"x":"blew my mind and made me switch from being a UX designer dabbling in Rails to a full blown programmer.","i":14168188,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":27332623,"author":"dancemethis","month":"2021-05","story":"“Computer science is not about computers”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Legendary best version of the idea that CS is not about computers","quote":"the best variation of this idea by far","text":"Wow, what a MASSIVE missed opportunity to quote the best variation of this idea by far, done by Hal Abelson in the legendary \"6001-Introduction to Computer Science\" class on MIT in the 80s. You can check it here: [VIDEO] Abelson in the first minute crosses both computer AND science, and references the also legendary SICP with \"computer so-called science actually has a lot in common with magic\". Honestly, this alone already made the article that empty."},{"id":18199123,"author":"otras","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some old talks (10+ y) that are still relevant to watch today?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First SICP lecture on the naming of computer science","quote":"I highly recommend the first lecture in the SICP series","text":"The Mother Of All Demos is an absolute classic: [LINK] [LINK] I also very much enjoy Feynman’s talks. Here’s one on imagining physics: [LINK] For computer science in particular, I highly recommend the first lecture in the SICP series, especially on the naming of so called “computer science”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16501826,"author":"stuxnet79","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: What's an (article|book|vid) that changed the way you view programming?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First ten minutes changed how the commenter views computing","quote":"this is where it all clicked to me","text":"First 10 mins of the first Structure and Interpretation lecture (by Harold Abelson) [1] Famous first words ... \"I'd like to welcome you to this course on computer science. Actually, that's a terrible way to start. Computer science is a terrible name for this business.\" ... and this is where it all clicked to me .... \"Well, similarly, I think in the future people will look back and say, yes, those primitives in the 20th century were fiddling around with these gadgets called computers, but really what they were doing is starting to learn how to formalize intuitions about process \" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14168188,"author":"dmix","month":"2017-04","story":"Calculus Made Easy (1914) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Black box abstraction and Lisp blocks inspired a career switch to programming","quote":"blew my mind and made me switch from being a UX designer dabbling in Rails to a full blown programmer.","text":"Reminds me of the MIT SICP lecture videos from the 80s. The concepts of black box abstraction and the simplicity of using LISP like lego building blocks blew my mind and made me switch from being a UX designer dabbling in Rails to a full blown programmer. It was still entirely relevant to today even though it was a few decades old as the fundamentals of computer science are still fundamental. [VIDEO] Hearing that intro music still brings a smile to my face. I just happen to be relearning math right now as I dive deeper into data science and this is perfect timing. Going to watch this series once I get through my math proofs book (\"Book of Proof\" by Richard Hammack which I recommend to people getting into math [LINK] )."},{"id":8060309,"author":"r-s","month":"2014-07","story":"Interactive SICP","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Original SICP lectures, dated but great content","quote":"It would be quite difficult to beat these lectures, while they appear a bit dated, the content is great.","text":"One section of original lectures are freely available via MITs OpenCourseWare youtube channel: [VIDEO] It would be quite difficult to beat these lectures, while they appear a bit dated, the content is great. SICP is a great way to learn about programming, although I am very surprised they got through the whole book in an introductory course. Chapters 4 and 5 cover some topics I was barely aware of nearing the end of my formal CS education."},{"id":4882861,"author":"tinco","month":"2012-12","story":"Zuckerberg visits Stanford CS106A third year in a row","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fun, interesting intro to CS with an awesome lecturer teaching Scheme","quote":"the lecturer has an awesome way of explaining it","text":"Heh that's interesting, I just watched 106A yesterday to get an idea of how the programming introduction course is compared to my university (utwente.nl). I actually was a bit disappointed to see that it is very much like our introduction course, except that this teacher is a bit more enthousiastic, and perhaps the course starts at an even more basic level I think. I think for real CS enthousiasts 6.001 at MIT is much more interesting (and fun) to watch: [VIDEO] (it teaches scheme and the lecturer has an awesome way of explaining it)"},{"id":40243255,"author":"hickelpickle","month":"2024-05","story":"The world's loudest Lisp program to the rescue","stance":"rec","why":"SICP lectures helped the commenter appreciate Scheme","quote":"What made me appreciate scheme was watching some of the SICP lectures","text":"Little schemer is good, some people hate it some people love it. But it is a fairly light read the slowly teaches some syntax at a time, questions you about assumptions then revels the information as it goes on. It would be the least dry read. There is also sketchy scheme for a more thorough text, or even the rs7s standard, which are both pretty dry but short. What made me appreciate scheme was watching some of the SICP lectures ( [VIDEO] ) and the little schemer to learn more. I also read some of the SICP along with it, though I put it down due to not having the time to work through it. Scheme is interesting and toying with recursion is fun, but the path a mentioned above is only really enjoyable if you are looking to toy around with CS concepts and recursion. You can do a lot more in modern scheme as well, and you can build anything out of CL. But learning the basics of scheme/lisp is can be pretty dry if you are just looking to build something right away like you already can in a traditional imperative language. But it is interesting if you are interested in a different perspective. But even RS7S scheme is still far from the batteries included you get with CL. I personal found the most enjoyment using Kawa scheme, which is jvm based and using it for scripting with java programs as it has great interop. I used it some for a game back end in the event system to be able to emit events while developing and script behaviors, I've also used it for configurations as well with a g"},{"id":38080096,"author":"efortis","month":"2023-10","story":"Today's CS pedagogy is problematic","stance":"rec","why":"First SICP video listed among good parts of CS pedagogy","quote":"Also, at least, the first video of SICP","text":"Some Good Parts: - Professor Richard Buckland has CS playlists on youtube (UNSW eLearning). In one of his lectures he starts writing a sudoku solver (I think) starting with high level abstractions. - Also, at least, the first video of SICP [VIDEO]"},{"id":23373321,"author":"xyzwave","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: I'm an incoming freshman to college for a CS major, what should I know?","stance":"rec","why":"Source of the timeless quote","quote":"SICP is not a bad way to augment a CS degree :-)","text":"Such a timeless quote. I believe this is the source: [VIDEO] While there, SICP is not a bad way to augment a CS degree :-)"},{"id":18309895,"author":"lunchladydoris","month":"2018-10","story":"Not Lisp again (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Abelson and Sussman lectures conveying the magic of the course","quote":"I felt a similar sense of magic the first time I saw the derivative section.","text":"For anyone who wants to feel what this might have felt like (and you've yet to watch them), MIT posted videos of Abelson and Sussman presenting the course on YouTube [0]. I felt a similar sense of magic the first time I saw the derivative section. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14168114,"author":"unabst","month":"2017-04","story":"Why MIT Switched from Scheme to Python (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"The classic Scheme course lecture series","quote":"Here is the classic course","text":"I took 6.001 in 1999 (I think). Hal Abelson taught it along with a professor who took off his sweatshirt to reveal a Microsoft tee at his final lecture (he went to Microsoft). What was great about Scheme (Lisp) is that most programs are basically words from your vocabulary, parentheses, and cars and cdrs. With procedural languages, there is always a sense that the language provides all the tools, and the magic happens somewhere underneath the hood. But with Scheme it feels as if it's happening right before you. It makes you feel like a wizard, and not a monkey. And it requires knowing all the spells. It requires knowing how to make magic happen. Or not. Once you know none of it's magic. But that's the point! Python is arguably easier and more practical for both science and work. It's already popular on the web server, and is used everywhere else -- unlike Scheme. But the recent resurgence of functional programming is super exciting with Elixir and Elm and the like. I'm looking forward to building my next project using Elixir. Here is the classic course: [LINK] Original video lecture on YouTube (linked from above): [VIDEO]"},{"id":42385721,"author":"dailykoder","month":"2024-12","story":"What I learned covering computer science in 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture reinforcing that CS is about understanding problems, not computers","quote":"And I still remind myself of that.","text":">A pithy quote attributed to the pioneering researcher Edsger Dijkstra helps me explain: “Computer science is no more about computers than astronomy is about telescopes.” Reminds me of the MIT lecture \"Lecture 1A | MIT 6.001 Structure and Interpretation, 1986\"[1]. And I still remind myself of that. If you fundamentally understand your problem and solve it, then writing a few characters in your favorite text editor that do the task is easy - [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41091383,"author":"kurisufag","month":"2024-07","story":"The secret of Minecraft (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Opening line comparing computer science to magic","quote":"We'll actually see that Computer so-called-Science actually has a lot in common with magic.\"[0]","text":">You know, magic >So I became a programmer because I wanted to be a wizard that's a classic MIT AI lab idea. \"We'll actually see that Computer so-called-Science actually has a lot in common with magic.\"[0] [0] [VIDEO] 0:40, and a lot in the SICP textbook all your rblx info is still true, by the way. there's a lot more you can script with too, including internal and external API integration, inter-game info transfer, transactions, all kinds of stuff."},{"id":27332716,"author":"aniijbod","month":"2021-05","story":"“Computer science is not about computers”","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson's opening line that CS is not about science or computers","quote":"","text":"The very first thing that Hal Abelson says and puts up on the chalkboard in his very first SICP talk (Lecture 1A 6.001 'Structure and Interpretation') in 1986 is that \"Computer Science is not about either science, or computers'. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20471359,"author":"CaioAlonso","month":"2019-07","story":"Think in Math, Write in Code","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson's distinction between mathematics as truth and computing as process","quote":"Harold Abelson creates the distinction[1] between Mathematics being the study of truth and Computing being the study of process.","text":"\"The natural language which has been effectively used for thinking about computation, for thousands of years, is mathematics.\" I'm not sure if this is true. Harold Abelson creates the distinction[1] between Mathematics being the study of truth and Computing being the study of process. It seems to me that these really are different things and Mathematics isn't the \"natural language\" to discuss computations, but rather truth and patterns. But of course process (computing) can only happen within the boundaries of mathematical truths and patterns. [1] [VIDEO] the first few minutes"},{"id":14747687,"author":"Nadya","month":"2017-07","story":"Blogger discredits claim Amelia Earhart was taken prisoner by Japan","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson's opening on computer science not being about computers","quote":"The introduction is amusing and opens with Computer Science has nothing to do with Computers and little to do with Science.","text":"The quote is of Hal Abelson and one of his lectures at MIT disagrees with you [1]. The introduction is amusing and opens with Computer Science has nothing to do with Computers and little to do with Science. It's really a poorly named field. I believe it's a bit tongue in cheek and not defining science entirely by \"uses the scientific method\". [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14202586,"author":"shakna","month":"2017-04","story":"What programming languages do you know and which ones do you want to learn?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Interesting. Maybe we should compare notes sometimes. I have a bit of a story with finding alternative-type stuff. I really like the theory of programming, and am constantly looking for languages that are a) easier to write b) amazingly expressive Its always finding what's out there. > - I've been contemplating tackling Python, in spite of the 2.x/3.x thing. There's the perfect vacuum where versioning shouldn't be a problem, and the practical real world where knowing Python and the differences between the two will let me actually do interesting things. Thankfully, that's mostly gone now. Few enterprises still holding out, but most of the big libraries are 2/3 or 3 only. And 3 is a lot more thought-out. Most of the warts are gone. > - I completely forgot to mention Awk (!) mostly because I have no idea at what point I began to pick it up, but I did get a basic idea of how it works a while ago, and I've been factoring it into shell pipelines more frequently lately. It's great for when I need to do something that sed can't express well (and I just properly read about how match() a) returns the index and b) also works apropos to grep recently, which is great) Awk is different, but great. I had a boss at a previous job who'd used it for at least a decade for everything, so I had to learn the nitty-gritty. I ended up building a CGI application that basically ran the entire website in Awk. Big learning experience, but rather cool. > (As an aside, Lua is ~200K and awk is ~632K. Tha"},{"id":9986498,"author":"sepeth","month":"2015-08","story":"The 'No True Programmer' Fallacy","stance":"reference","why":"First SICP episode on what programming is","quote":"the first episode of SICP videos helped me to reach the same conclusion","text":"> Note that you can be a programmer without ever having touched a computer, people have been coming up with algorithms and integrating them into systems for ages. Slightly different, but the first episode of SICP videos helped me to reach the same conclusion: [VIDEO] We can even say Newton, or Euclid wrote programs. Maybe not by sitting in front of a computer, but they described a process to calculate something."},{"id":13920362,"author":"keithnz","month":"2017-03","story":"Beautiful Online SICP","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"you all with your fancy pants \"reading\". Nothing beats the movie, not only showing the height of computing power, but the awesome fashion of uber geeks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7920571,"author":"userbinator","month":"2014-06","story":"8088 Domination Post-Mortem, Part 1","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think the underlying cause of this over abstraction is largely a result of abstraction being excessively glorified (mostly) by academics and formal CS curricula. In some ways, it's similar to the OOP overuse that has thankfully decreased somewhat recently but was extremely prevalent throughout the 90s. In software engineering, we're constantly subjected to messages like: Abstraction is good. Abstraction is powerful. Abstraction is the way to solve problems. More abstraction is better. Even in the famously acclaimed SICP lecture series [1] there is this quote: \"So in that sense computer science is like an abstract form of engineering. It's the kind of engineering where you ignore the constraints that are imposed by reality.\" There is an implication that we should be building more complex software just because we can , since that is somehow \"better\". Efficiency is only thought of in strictly algorithmic terms, constants are ignored, and we're almost taught that thinking about efficiency should be discouraged unless absolutely necessary because it's \"premature optimisation\". The (rapidly coming to an end) exponential growth of hardware power made this attitude acceptable, and lower-level knowledge of hardware (or just simple things like binary/bit fields) is undervalued \"because we have these layers of abstraction\" - often leading to adding another layer on top just to reinvent things that could be easily accomplished at a lower level. The fact that many of those in the demosc"}],"r":[["3CWCqJl0BEs",1],["C6nAxiym9oc",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["YThelfB2fvg",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1],["O4Ko7NW2yQo",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["Zd_UcjMusUA",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1],["yJDv-zdhzMY",1],["1rZ-JorHJEY",1],["l70iRcSxqzo",1],["8bZh5LMaSmE",1],["7d9juPsv1QU",1]]},"Ja5Q75hf6QI":{"wy":"Recommended during Afghanistan withdrawal threads as a sobering look at why the transition to local forces was failing.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend the 2013 documentary","i":28189683,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34827914,"author":"pengaru","month":"2023-02","story":"Taliban fighters are sick of the 9 to 5 working desk jobs to run Afghanistan","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows war was a waste with no plausible path to success","quote":"If you haven't seen the VICE special This Is What Winning Looks Like [0], watch it.","text":"If you haven't seen the VICE special This Is What Winning Looks Like [0], watch it. As a sibling comment said, there was no plausible path to success. It was just a tremendous waste of time and resources, many of which just ended up in terrorist hands. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30180394,"author":"cf141q5325","month":"2022-02","story":"Vice Media organised $20M Saudi government festival","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Impressive embedded reporting by Ben Anderson","quote":"Ben Anderson whos Afghanistan and Iraq reporting was really impressive.","text":"Vice news has (or had?) some actual journalists. They hired Ben Anderson whos Afghanistan and Iraq reporting was really impressive. Especially \"This is what winning looks like\". [VIDEO] If i recall correctly he made the footage when embedded via the BBC and (re)published it on vice? Still incredibly impressed of the footage he managed to shoot as an embedded journalists. I would guess in no small parts due to the integrity of Major Bill Steuber. Unfortunately that seems to have cost him his military career. He is now a security guard in Manhattan. [LINK] Afterwards Anderson mentioned the required style of reporting at Vice negatively (the camera focusing on his reactions) but he stayed with them for a while. Not sure if he is still associated with them. edit: Fixed timestamp & He left vice last year [LINK]"},{"id":28189683,"author":"peterkelly","month":"2021-08","story":"Afghan president flees the country as Taliban move on Kabul","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating insight into failed transition to local security forces","quote":"I highly recommend the 2013 documentary","text":"For a fascinating insight into the disaster that was supposed to be the training of and transition to local security forces, I highly recommend the 2013 documentary \"This Is What Winning Looks Like\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":19057187,"author":"newnewpdro","month":"2019-02","story":"Vice Media to Reorganize, Lay Off 10 Percent of Staff","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Illuminating coverage of rebuilding failures; upsetting","quote":"I highly recommend giving this a watch though it's very upsetting","text":"Their youtube channel used to deliver incredibly raw and direct coverage of world events other US news sources seemed to be ignoring. Over the last few years though I've become increasingly annoyed with what content of theirs I saw, it's definitely changed. Their coverage of the rebuilding efforts and failures in Afghanistan for instance was very illuminating and well done, I highly recommend giving this a watch though it's very upsetting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35781705,"author":"pengaru","month":"2023-05","story":"Vice Is Said to Be Headed for Bankruptcy","stance":"rec","why":"Solid journalism on the US war in Afghanistan","quote":"seemed like pretty solid journalism","text":"I was never much of a VICE follower, but their This Is What Winning Looks Like [0] special on the US' war in Afghanistan seemed like pretty solid journalism. Wish they'd continue producing content like that... Hamilton's Pharmacopeia was fun as well. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35830198,"author":"reaperman","month":"2023-05","story":"Vice Media to be Acquired by George Soros and Fortress","stance":"rec","why":"Example of Vice's older, higher quality work","quote":"","text":"\"Old Vice\": [VIDEO] \"New Vice\": [LINK] Hopefully they can regain their conscience and quality."},{"id":35830211,"author":"reaperman","month":"2023-05","story":"‘Like Icarus – now everyone is burnt’: how Vice and BuzzFeed fell to earth","stance":"rec","why":"Example of Vice's older, higher quality work","quote":"","text":"\"Old Vice\": [VIDEO] \"New Vice\": [LINK] > A @VICEUK video producer once responded to me: \"I don't understand why this is so difficult, all I want to know is if you can do the job\" when I brought up the issue of my safety covering sensitive topics in China, and how I'll take the fall while her team leaves unscathed. > Naomi: Off limits stuff: I don't talk about my relationship status or my sexual orientation. China is China and it's a complex issue that is sometimes dealt with in pragmatic ways- and my focus is on other issues. It's just a lot of trouble here. > Vice: \"LOL. We outed you on an international stage and also went out of our way to get your Patreon banned, buh-bye!\" Hopefully they can regain their conscience and quality."},{"id":28277970,"author":"sureglymop","month":"2021-08","story":"Fighting Like Taliban","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how lost and helpless the situation always was","quote":"See this documentary (from 8 years ago) about how utterly lost and helpless this situation has always been","text":"The goal was never to \"win\". It was to build up an army (the afghan army) that could eventually stand by itself and defend the government in place. This was a largely failing effort even years ago, considering that most of the afghan army were made up of people from the north of Afghanistan and previous geopolitics (north/south) play a role. The people in the south would at times trust the taliban over sympathisers for the previous northern warlords which had influence over members of the afghan army. See this documentary (from 8 years ago) about how utterly lost and helpless this situation has always been: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27801748,"author":"pengaru","month":"2021-07","story":"Special Report: Afghan pilots assassinated by Taliban as U.S. withdraws","stance":"rec","why":"Illuminating look at waste and chaos in Afghanistan","quote":"was very illuminating for me, the situation there is a total clusterfuck and waste of resources","text":"The VICE documentary This Is What Winning Looks like [0] was very illuminating for me, the situation there is a total clusterfuck and waste of resources. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21916817,"author":"newnewpdro","month":"2019-12","story":"What were the creepiest declassified documents of the last decade?","stance":"rec","why":"Sobering and disturbing report on Afghanistan struggles","quote":"It's a very sobering and disturbing report, available on youtube.","text":"The VICE documentary This Is What Winning Looks Like exposes some of the same struggles with Afghanistan. It's a very sobering and disturbing report, available on youtube. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7895065,"author":"HenryMc","month":"2014-06","story":"The U.S. Military’s Campaign Against Media Freedom","stance":"rec","why":"Great reporting on the war in Afghanistan","quote":"has some great reporting on Afghanistan.","text":"The Vice documentary 'This Is What Winning Looks Like' has some great reporting on Afghanistan. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28187505,"author":"sorokod","month":"2021-08","story":"Taliban enter Kabul as Afghan government on brink of collapse","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in support of Afghans' unwillingness to defend themselves","quote":"","text":"To state the obvious, the Afghans in aggregate, were not willing to build an army and were not willing to defend themselves against the Taliban. This Is What Winning Looks Like (2013?): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JFaOYYSxSEA",2],["c2lgEyNaop4",1],["X29lF43mUlo",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["jK0BkTPUGQY",1],["tzr7hRXcwkw",1],["VFFlO7yBIBM",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1],["1Y1ya-yF35g",1],["sGG-A80Tl5g",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"QM1iUe6IofM":{"wy":"HN returns to it as the go-to critique of OOP, recommended for explaining the costs of abstraction better than most written arguments.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video by Brian Will who does a much better job explaining this problem than I could.","i":15735988,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":21815547,"author":"jmulho","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Books you plan to read in 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues design-pattern books are not worth years of effort; Brian's videos excellent.","quote":"Brian's other (not controversial) videos (dozens) are excellent for learning about programming.","text":"Looking at your top priority books I recalled a slide from one of Brian Will's videos on object oriented programming: \"Object Oriented Programming is Bad\" [VIDEO] . At around the 43:51 he pulls up a slide picturing several of these (and similar) books. Here is a rough quote: \"I can tell you from personal experience of having read these books that you don't need to read them. They don't have answers, they aren't going to square the circle, and you are going to waste productive years of your life trying to live up to their ideals.\" Now that is just one rather controversial opinion, but we are talking about potentially wasting productive years of your life so I wanted to let you know that opinion is out there. That being said, it would probably be hugely beneficial to read a few of those books and then watch a few of Brian's videos (he has three or four on the topic) and sort out your own opinion. Also, Brian's other (not controversial) videos (dozens) are excellent for learning about programming. Also, Steve Krug's Don't Make Me Think is a keeper. I thought of it last night when I was checking out at a store. I stuck my debit card into the thing, entered my PIN number, then clicked the big blue button that said \"Skip PIN\". The woman behind the counter said \"don't worry about it; everybody does that!\""},{"id":15735988,"author":"ryanmarsh","month":"2017-11","story":"When I use classes, when I don’t, what I do instead and why","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains problems with encapsulating data and behavior in classes.","quote":"I highly recommend this video by Brian Will who does a much better job explaining this problem than I could.","text":"Code should be written first and foremost for humans to understand, and incidentally for compilers to understand. [LINK] If we can all agree about that then we have to ask ourselves if encapsulating data and behavior at the granularity of classes makes the most sense for human comprehension. In naive OO examples (Animal -> Cat) it does. In practice however, it's debatable (at best). I highly recommend this video by Brian Will who does a much better job explaining this problem than I could. [VIDEO] Douglas Crockford also does a good job of explaining some of the trouble with classes. [LINK]"},{"id":42190248,"author":"cutler","month":"2024-11","story":"99 Bottles of OOP now available in Python","stance":"rec","why":"Argues OOP generates incidental complexity.","quote":"OOP is an industry of its own which generates a ton of incidental complexity.","text":"OOP is an industry of its own which generates a ton of incidental complexity. See \"Object-Oriented Programming is Bad\" by Brian Wills ( [VIDEO] ) and most of Rich Hickey's excellent videos, especially his keynote at Rails Conf 2012 where he basically told the Ruby crowd they're doing it wrong ( [LINK] )."},{"id":38732728,"author":"_dain_","month":"2023-12","story":"C Object Oriented Programming (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of how OOP's encapsulation and decoupling are mostly a mirage.","quote":"This video has a pretty good breakdown of how the \"encapsulation\" and \"decoupling\" promised by OOP is mostly a mirage","text":"This video has a pretty good breakdown of how the \"encapsulation\" and \"decoupling\" promised by OOP is mostly a mirage: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30656862,"author":"zamalek","month":"2022-03","story":".NET Myths Dispelled","stance":"rec","why":"Popular video touching on many contentions against OOP.","quote":"Here's a really popular video that touches on many of the contentions","text":"Here's a really popular video that touches on many of the contentions: [VIDEO] My personal tipping point was the realization that OOP forces you to first figure out how you solution maps to OOP, before figuring out (or iterating on) how it maps to code. It creates nothing more than accidental complexity: [LINK]"},{"id":30661383,"author":"DeathArrow","month":"2022-03","story":".NET Myths Dispelled","stance":"rec","why":"Offered among alternatives to OOP.","quote":"For those interested in something better than OOP, here's a few links","text":"I think this is a valid critique. But then, most of the development (C aside) has been a victim to OOP. I have begun to think that OOP creates more problems than it solves. Added complexity and performance loss being the biggest. It's insane if you peek into a big projects where many developers came and went and each of them read an article about what it seemed an interesting design pattern and tried to implement them. You can have some kind of procedural programming if you use classes just to store data and use static methods. But the frameworks are OOP and there is no way around that unless you write your own frameworks. I am a big fan of Mike Acton's Data Oriented Architecture speeches, but I didn't find a way to implement something similar in C#. For those interested in something better than OOP, here's a few links: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30760714,"author":"cloogshicer","month":"2022-03","story":"The Big TDD Misunderstanding","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes the costs of abstraction well.","quote":"A lot of my thoughts are summarized well by Brian Will in this video","text":"That's fair - I'm not against abstraction in general. I agree that we wouldn't be able to handle the essential complexity of software without it. I'm just saying that each abstraction also has a cost, and makes understanding the whole thing more difficult. Abstraction is, in my understanding, a necessary but costly tool. A lot of my thoughts are summarized well by Brian Will in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23510307,"author":"cxr","month":"2020-06","story":"Design Principles Behind Smalltalk (1981)","stance":"rec","why":"Thoughtful and thought-provoking video essay on OOP.","quote":"It's both thoughtful and thought-provoking, although I don't agree with the conclusion.","text":"At the risk of putting readers off by the title, there's a video essay on YouTube called \"Object-Oriented Programming is Bad\"[1]. It's both thoughtful and thought-provoking, although I don't agree with the conclusion. Steve Naroff (from Stepstone/NeXT/Apple) did a recent-ish set of interviews with the Computer History Museum[2][3] and referenced that video a bit (and extemporized about objects in small parts throughout). Side note: Naroff and Brad Cox are set to present a paper on Objective-C at this year's HOPL[4]. I wish it were as readily available as Allen Wirfs-Brock's JS paper for the same conference[5][6]. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK] 5. [LINK] 6. [LINK]"},{"id":21816270,"author":"crimsonalucard","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Books you plan to read in 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Criticism of OOP that applies to design patterns.","quote":"Take a look at the following video","text":"This isn't about OOP vs. FP. This is about OOP vs. everything else. Rust, Go and javascript are three languages that are moving away from the OOP paradigm. None of those three languages are classified as functional. >Is there some resource you can point to that says in the past when faced with situation X developers tended to use pattern Y, these days that has been superseded with way of doing things Z? No. A general trend is an anecdotal observation. Still a quick google search yielded: [LINK] Take a look at the following video: [VIDEO] While I don't agree with a lot of it, I think it sums up my opinion really clearly. It's not about DP's but because DP's focus around OOP and the video is a criticism of OOP therefore the arguments apply to DPs."},{"id":21287849,"author":"lazulicurio","month":"2019-10","story":"Why Isn't Functional Programming the Norm? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Pretty interesting videos on state in OOP.","quote":"The blog post and related youtube videos are pretty interesting as well[2][3][4].","text":"There was an article on state in OOP posted here a few days ago that I found very thought-provoking[1]. The blog post and related youtube videos are pretty interesting as well[2][3][4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":13790940,"author":"NegatioN","month":"2017-03","story":"How we secretly introduced Haskell and got away with it","stance":"rec","why":"Supports view that OOP magnifies design problems.","quote":"I liked Rich Hickeys talk \"Simple made Easy\" [1] and \"Brian Will: Why OOP is Bad\" [2]","text":"Prodcedural code can also be well compartmentalized and tested. I was also until recently quite convinced OOP was the only way to go, but I'm seeing signs everywhere that a lot of the design-problems I've met over the past few years are at least magnified by OOP. The abstraction promised by OOP is a good thing, however very few people are able to consistently make good, reusable and maintained abstractions. To the point where it becomes a weakness rather than a strength. I don't want a billion wrapper-objects that obfuscate my code, and makes the surface area of it bigger than it has to be. Often I struggle understanding code more because of how it was separated, than because of the complexity of what it actually does. I liked Rich Hickeys talk \"Simple made Easy\" [1] and \"Brian Will: Why OOP is Bad\" [2] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13891540,"author":"hackits","month":"2017-03","story":"Principles for C programming","stance":"rec","why":"Articulates the case against OOP well.","quote":"One of the video that can articulate the whole thing better than me is","text":"One of the video that can articulate the whole thing better than me is ( [VIDEO] ) It was a long winded written rant. I still use OOP but its more or less glorified name spaces with functions. Today I rather work on procedural code than OOP code. It may have bugs, it may have their own little quirks but its like a old rusted vehicle that still keeps going."},{"id":11342992,"author":"thunderbong","month":"2016-03","story":"Object-Oriented Programming is Garbage: 3800 SLOC example [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Resonates with commenter's experience of OOP's problems.","quote":"honestly, it rings too close to my experience. Need to think about this a bit more deeply!","text":"Previous videos Object-Oriented Programming is Bad [VIDEO] Object-Oriented Programming is Embarrassing: 4 Short Examples [LINK] I'm watching the 'is Bad' one, and honestly, it rings too close to my experience. Need to think about this a bit more deeply!"},{"id":43052039,"author":"muixoozie","month":"2025-02","story":"Do-nothing scripting: the key to gradual automation (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Series ranting about needless abstraction with simplification examples.","quote":"he has a series basically ranting about this.","text":"Lol Reminds me of Brain Will's [VIDEO] he has a series basically ranting about this. One of them he goes into examples simplifying needlessly abstract code."},{"id":32143701,"author":"imbnwa","month":"2022-07","story":"Why long-term plans don't work and how to fix them","stance":"reference","why":"Classic video recommending procedural over OOP, compared to Agile.","quote":"Brian Will's procedural programming recommendation contra OOP in his classic 'Object-Oriented Programming is Bad'[1]","text":"I find an uncanny correspondence between the recommendation given here, following as it does from an interpretation of the Agile manifesto, and Brian Will's procedural programming recommendation contra OOP in his classic 'Object-Oriented Programming is Bad'[1] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29331742,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2021-11","story":"Overengineering can kill a product","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Engineering within the wrong paradigm can kill your product. I see far more problems on a day-to-day basis with patterns and anti-patterns taken too far. For example, I never use the factory pattern, because it leads one down the Java road where everything ends up an object with mutable state. Which isn't scalable over some metric (like a million lines of code) because a human brain can't trace execution, even with a debugger. A far better pattern generally is to take a functional approach of accepting data, swizzling it, and returning the resulting data without mutability or side effects. Another really bad pattern is when execution suspends and resumes somewhere else (goto hell). Any project which uses queuing, eventual consistency, promises, nonblocking streams, even basic notifications or things as complex as monads will encounter this nondeterminism and inability to statically analyze code. Note that pretty much all web development suffers from some aspect of this due to its async and multi-signaling nature. So what I do now, which I don't see much these days, is solve problems abstractly in a spreadsheet (pure functional programming), in the shell (the Actor model) or as a flowchart (declarative and data-driven design), and then translate that to whatever crappy language/framework I have to use for the project. I find that today, roughly 90% of developer effort goes to discovery, refactoring and testing of ill-conceived code. Only 10% is \"actual work\" and that's probabl"},{"id":20396612,"author":"noobermin","month":"2019-07","story":"Data Still Dominates","stance":"reference","why":"Recently discovered talk that prompted rethinking OOP.","quote":"May be this is just in my head because I recently discovered this talk (ignore the incendiary title)","text":"I'm not a systems designer, so I am somewhat ignorant to make this statement, but this sounds almost like \"nouns-first\" is the right thing, but almost every single one of those people cited in the article are anti-oop people. Moreover, OOP has generally fallen by the way side and been blamed for unnecessary complexity in some legacy systems from the 90s/00s. The moment you conceptualize your system as the interaction of concrete data types, OOP starts to seem like a logical paradigm to choose. May be this is just in my head because I recently discovered this talk (ignore the incendiary title): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19357805,"author":"Rumperuu","month":"2019-03","story":"Object-Oriented Programming Is Good*","stance":"reference","why":"Brian Will's earlier anti-OOP video, cited as background for his follow-up.","quote":"Brian Will of “Object-Oriented Programming is Bad”[0] fame","text":"Brian Will of “Object-Oriented Programming is Bad”[0] fame presents ‘a positive case for an alternative’, or a ‘prescription for how code should be written, rather than how it shouldn't .’ [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18705113,"author":"fogetti","month":"2018-12","story":"Why Do React Hooks Rely on Call Order?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You are implying that the causation goes like this: redux good -> so the app will be good too. And to make a case in point you are trying to make an argument that because many apps are poorly designed, everyone should use redux. You really can't see how fundamentally broken is your argument? Really??? And you are not the only one who makes this HUGE fallacy. All the redux/react fanboys are like you. At this point I already consider those people who make these arguments that they are just following/using redux because it's a religion for them. Not to mention the fact that some people considers the whole uni-directional message flow a HUGE antipattern. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11102803,"author":"fenesiistvan","month":"2016-02","story":"Why I haven't given up on C++","stance":"reference","why":"Brian Will argues OOP is bad.","quote":"Brian Will says that that whole OOP concept is bad","text":"Brian Will says that that whole OOP concept is bad (not to say about C++ which is OOP in the ugly way): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rI8tNMsozo0",2],["IRTfhkiAqPw",1],["lnnsDi7Sxq0",1],["Rqfkuc5mawg",1],["MBRqu0YOH14",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["iGFqfTCL2fs",1],["Wm2h0cbvsw8",1],["d8RRE2rDw4k",1],["R2paSGQRwvo",1],["rX0ItVEVjHc",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["X1T3IQ4N-3g",1],["xP5-iIeKXE8",1]]},"p2LZLYcu_JY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a favourite tech talk full of good ideas from forgotten 1980s software, worth watching in full.","q":{"x":"The whole talk is well worth watching","i":17190521,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":37987670,"author":"theodpHN","month":"2023-10","story":"Alan Kay: Doing with Images Makes Symbols [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Smalltalk program written in 1975 by a 12-year-old girl","quote":"Dang, Alan Kay's old talks are so amazing.","text":"Dang, Alan Kay's old talks are so amazing. Check out this Smalltalk program written in 1975 by a 12-year-old girl. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17190521,"author":"scroot","month":"2018-05","story":"Media for Thinking the Unthinkable (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Complex ideas expressed via visual, kinesthetic and symbolic reasoning","quote":"The whole talk is well worth watching","text":"> Visualizations are great for shared intuition, but it should only be used at the end of a long personal struggle which should involve hours upon hours of undistracted effort, preferably involving something tactile like a pen and pencil. There is a classic Alan Kay talk that touches on this very issue, called \"Doing With Images Makes Symbols.\" It discusses what the true power of HCI really is: that complex ideas (or even \"unthinkable\" ones) can be expressed via HCI using some combination of visual, kinesthetic, and symbolic reasoning. The whole talk is well worth watching, but here is the part I feel is most relevant for Victor's point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15035957,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2017-08","story":"Concrete-free washing machines are lighter to transport, just as good in a spin","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demonstrates how good old forgotten software can be","quote":"enjoy this fully","text":"I'm already trying hard not to fall into full alankayism [1] and this only reinforces my view. Jobs said that many people in companies are clueless... [1] for people that never watched his talk where he demonstrates how pretty old and forgotten software can be, enjoy this fully [VIDEO]"},{"id":12638204,"author":"cconroy","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Summary of several 1980s-era software systems with many good ideas","quote":"It's really a summary of several software systems with good ideas abound.","text":"Doing with Images Makes Symbols, Alan Kay. [VIDEO] The title says it all. It's really a summary of several software systems with good ideas abound. I believe all the software is 80s or prior. Edit: I also forgot to mention some psychology and math."},{"id":25025497,"author":"artem247","month":"2020-11","story":"Programs are a prison: Rethinking the building blocks of computing interfaces","stance":"rec","why":"Smalltalk demo starting around 40:30","quote":"I love the demo of one of the Smalltalk programs in this talk by Alan Kay [0], starts at around 40:30","text":"> (see also Alan Kay's vision of software inspired by biological cells and systems – lots of talk, no non-trivial proofs of concept). I just wanted to say that actually Kay's vision was implemented in something very non-trivial, both on the hardware side - Alto, and the Smalltalk operating system. They were used by real people and exhibited lots of traits that this article talks about. And some of their ideas were hugely influential in mainstream computing. I love the demo of one of the Smalltalk programs in this talk by Alan Kay [0], starts at around 40:30 [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17990016,"author":"e12e","month":"2018-09","story":"Show HN: TechYaks – Best of 50k tech talks ranked by confidence intervals","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among classic talks missing from a ranking","quote":"Alan Kay \"doing with images makes symbols\"","text":"Maybe these are mostly too new, or you have different (more practical, hands on?) definition of tech-talk - but from a quick look the only speakers I expected - and found - were Sandi Mets and Rob Pike. If it's practical, I'm surprised not to see the js \"wat\" lightning talk (which I now can't seem to find...). If it's more general \"best of\", I'd expect something like Guy Steele \"growing a language\" : [LINK] Douglas Engelbart \"the mother of all demos\": [LINK] Alan Kay \"doing with images makes symbols\": [VIDEO] Or, if that's too long, the much more condensed ted talk: \"a powerful idea about teaching ideas\": [LINK] (Maybe that's not a \"tech talk\"?) Rich Hickey \"simple made easy\" : [LINK] To name a few of the top of my head."},{"id":16839378,"author":"jdale27","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as a favourite tech talk","quote":"here is Alan Kay's \"Doing with Images Makes Symbols\"","text":"I was going to mention Growing a Language, but since it's already been posted, here is Alan Kay's \"Doing with Images Makes Symbols\": [VIDEO] ."},{"id":15931809,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-12","story":"Communicating Advanced Mathematics to Kids","stance":"rec","why":"Kay on how calculus ideas develop from early ages","quote":"So many things I could say about this, but I\"ll let Alan Kay speak for me -","text":"So many things I could say about this, but I\"ll let Alan Kay speak for me - [VIDEO] Alan talks about how ideas in Calculus could be manifested from an early age and a richness in understanding built up as they aged up: - at very young ages, kids really respond well through \"doing\" / the enactive channel. When asked to draw a circle, kids in Papert's group would first emulate what a LOGO turtle would do by rotating their body in a circle (making tiny increments in x and y). - as they got a bit older, the visual / iconic channel was more developed and they could understand the abstraction of a circle on pencil/paper and how the concepts carried over there - closer to early teens, symbols were much easier to grasp and relate to, etc. With this context in mind, there have been some cool efforts to mix the second and third channels I just mentioned to communicate advanced math concepts. Vi Hart and Grant Sanderson's youtube channels come to mind. Here are my favorite videos by Grant: - \"What does it feel like to invent math\": [LINK] - On the visual intuition behind a hard problem on the Putnam exam - \"The hardest problem on the hardest test\": [LINK] - From vectors to matrices to vector spaces to higher-level ideas like the link between linear algebra and calculus: \"Essence of linear algebra\" series: [LINK] What I especially like about Grant's videos, is that he often walks through what a mathematician would do, the questions she would ask, etc."},{"id":37988019,"author":"darrickw","month":"2023-10","story":"Alan Kay shows the Rand tablet [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Higher quality version of the Rand tablet footage from 24:20","quote":"You can find a higher quality version of this beginning around the 24:20 mark","text":"You can find a higher quality version of this beginning around the 24:20 mark of the video at the link below. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29300231,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-11","story":"Alan Kay at UCLA (2016) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That was probably around 1982-1984, when Alan Kay was Chief Scientist at Atari, and set up Atari Cambridge Research Lab. Atari Cambridge Research- part 1 - Cynthia Solomon: [LINK] >This is a demonstration of some of the works in progress from July 1982 to April, 1984. From learning Logo, a look at our work at introducing people to computers, to music with some of our futuristic instruments. We'll take you on a tour of our lab. Director or Research, Cynthia Solomon. >I'm Cynthia Solomon. You're about to see some of the research that has been going on at the Atari Cambridge Research Laboratory. I've been its director since we started in 1982. The staff consisted of 22 full time research and support people, as well as 10 consultants. >Our research has been motivated by our past experiences in the Logo Group at the MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory, where we worked with Seymour Papert and Marvin Minsky. >We wanted to get Logo out of the laboratory and into the world. We wanted to share it, and share its potential as a powerful too for thinking. And so before we joined Atari, many of us were part of the development team at Logo Computer Systems, where we designed, implemented, and documented Apple Logo. >When we finished Apple Logo, I left Logo Computer Systems. I wanted to get back to doing research. I contacted Alan Kay, who was Chief Scientist at Atari, and it turned out he was interested in setting up a research laboratory in Cambridge, near MIT. >He was familiar with our"}],"r":[["lpu-3UF_b48",1],["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["2-oBNh0UkQc",1],["sCZJblyT_XM",1],["Mfsnlbd-4xQ",1],["NVEgyJCTee4",1],["vuIlsN32WaE",1],["1AjhFZVfB9c",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1],["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1]]},"pW-SOdj4Kkk":{"wy":"A perennial HN reference for arguments that software is getting worse and that hard-won knowledge is disappearing.","q":{"x":"Very much worth watching in its entirety and thinking about","i":27240081,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27240081,"author":"colllectorof","month":"2021-05","story":"Text editing hates you too (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full talk worth watching and thinking about","quote":"Very much worth watching in its entirety and thinking about","text":">Nostalgia for the simplicity of the past ends up having ugly cultural implications. It's easy to say \"let's go back to the time when things were simple\"; it's a lot harder to say \"folks in (e.g.) Israel shouldn't be able to type in their native language\". This is a ridiculous mischaracterization of what Jonathan Blow was talking about. Here is the full presentation. Very much worth watching in its entirety and thinking about: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22254524,"author":"spectramax","month":"2020-02","story":"Stop Perfecting, Start Shipping","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues for careful, quality software over shipping broken products","quote":"I recommend watching this incredible talk (it's about software)","text":"I am not so sure. This is a handwavy advise I always hear from contemporary business wizbangs, but I don't think it is objectively or universally true. Pedantic, careful and attention to detail, perfectionist products that just work without things breaking all the time are a pleasure to use from the standpoint of the customer. Figure out the implementation details, funding, motivation, etc. as an entrepreneur - shipping broken products to paying customers is something that I only see in the software industry. You don't buy a vacuum cleaner and expect to send half of the missing screws to the customer 6 months later. Hell, just 20 years ago when you bought your SEGA game cassette, it shipped as a final product. Not to be patched ever. Once its out to the factory, if you found a bug, tough luck. Yes, this analogy has its limits, but silicon valley mentality of this startup hustle is questionable. I recommend watching this incredible talk (it's about software) : [VIDEO]"},{"id":34112631,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Is software becoming less efficient?","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiring talk on declining software standards","quote":"Personally, I find this talk by Jonathan Blow to be rather inspiring","text":"> software actually becoming less efficient? I'd argue that software is becoming worse in some ways. It seems that the acceptable tolerance is _very_ low these days, with most people are just shrugging issues off. Personally, I find this talk by Jonathan Blow to be rather inspiring: [VIDEO] As for software becoming less efficient specifically, I'd say that it is, for example, relying on microservices for everything is probably not a good idea for most companies. Not everyone is AWS, nor do they need to be."},{"id":29631206,"author":"SQueeeeeL","month":"2021-12","story":"How are Rome's monuments still standing?","stance":"rec","why":"Great points on ancient people's complex achievements","quote":"Jonathan Blow makes a bunch of great points about our simplified view of ancient people","text":"Jonathan Blow makes a bunch of great points about our simplified view of ancient people, versus the reality of their complex achievements which could only be brought about by lots of ingenuity and iterations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28393460,"author":"city41","month":"2021-09","story":"The universe has already made almost all the stars it will ever make","stance":"rec","why":"Pessimistic view that we may lose progress and regress","quote":"Jonathan Blow has an interesting talk on the subject","text":"There's also the pessimistic view that we will actually lose progress and regress over time. Jonathan Blow has an interesting talk on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27069524,"author":"d_burfoot","month":"2021-05","story":"Crazy New Ideas","stance":"rec","why":"Blow deplores state of software engineering, web programming in particular","quote":"See his talk about preventing the end of civilization (!!)","text":"I follow Blow's work and opinions quite closely. I am quite confident he was NOT criticizing Mighty specifically. Instead he was deploring the state of software engineering in general and web programming in particular. He is saying something like \"I can't believe web engineering sucks so bad that a tool like Mighty actually makes sense\". See his talk about preventing the end of civilization (!!): [VIDEO]"},{"id":23584571,"author":"perfect5th","month":"2020-06","story":"An Intro to Compilers (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Knowledge lost generationally from black-box abstractions","quote":"There's a great talk from game developer Jonathan Blow [1] that describes how knowledge is lost \"generationally","text":"I took a couple of compiler courses in university, and I think for the most part it made me a better programmer, mainly because it enforced understanding of how specific programming concepts and constructs are generally implemented. That, and a more intense look at the \"heap of abstractions\" you're generally working with when you write a higher-level language. I've always advocated that, if you want to really understand the tools you are using, you need to understand at least one level below the \"surface abstraction\" that you are working if. Even better if you can understand two or three levels down. There's a great talk from game developer Jonathan Blow [1] that describes how knowledge is lost \"generationally\" due to our lack of understanding of the black boxes we build things on top of. Not sure I 100% agree with his thoughts, but it's an interesting take. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23671054,"author":"fagnerbrack","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: Why is development so expensive?","stance":"rec","why":"Programmers' declining cost-efficiency knowledge","quote":"Take a look at this","text":"It costs so much mostly because programmers don't know how to write software cost-efficiently. As everybody follows the same path, VCs start to believe the cost is increasing when in fact it's the programmer's knowledge of cost-efficiency software design that is decreasing. Take a look at this: [VIDEO] The costs to develop software are ridiculous and unrealistic. It turns out that if enough people act as ridiculous and unrealistic that becomes the new normal."},{"id":22220035,"author":"mrspeaker","month":"2020-02","story":"Amateur radio skills prove useful during bushfire emergencies","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting programming talk on losing knowledge","quote":"An interesting (programming) talk by Jonathan Blow on the subject of losing knowledge!","text":"Of course that's true - we probably don't need to start re-teaching the abacus... but I'm careful about getting too complacent. When everything has been going well for a long time it's tempting to think this will continue forever. But one, say, global pandemic can bring everything down - and some of our modern conveniences can be gone... possibly for centuries. An interesting (programming) talk by Jonathan Blow on the subject of losing knowledge! [VIDEO]"},{"id":21859126,"author":"beefman","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among best talks of 2019","quote":"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization, by Jonathan Blow","text":"The Soul of Erlang and Elixir, by Sasa Juric video: [LINK] HN discussion: [LINK] Preventing the Collapse of Civilization, by Jonathan Blow video: [VIDEO] HN discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":20032205,"author":"worldsayshi","month":"2019-05","story":"Docker is vulnerable to a symlink-race attack","stance":"rec","why":"Blow's talk on software and civilization collapse","quote":"If you'd like to venture further into this rabbit hole I can recommend","text":"If you'd like to venture further into this rabbit hole I can recommend \"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\": [VIDEO] And \"The Thirty Million Line Problem\": [LINK]"},{"id":37151770,"author":"gradschool","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: What are the “lost arts” of our field?","stance":"reference","why":"Blow argues software skills are lost as older generations retire.","quote":"Well-known game developer Jonathan Blow has a lot to say about software and technology more generally being in decline","text":"Well-known game developer Jonathan Blow has a lot to say about software and technology more generally being in decline due to skills being lost when older generations retire [1]. In my own experience, writing code with pointers in it used to be a skill expected of entry level programmers, and getting the hang of it was something of a rite of passage. Around the time Java started being used as a teaching language, it became best practice to rely on the language's standard libraries instead of rolling one's own linked list or whatever, and I instructed university students accordingly. As things have developed, the design effort put into Rust seems to imply that pointers are now regarded as a grand challenge problem. These days I don't tie my own neck ties any more but wear the clip-on kind because the knots have been developed and tested by experts. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31671350,"author":"rglover","month":"2022-06","story":"The End of Localhost","stance":"reference","why":"Understanding loss leading to industry collapse","quote":"Jonathan Blow nailed it on this idea a few years back","text":"No. This is the absolute wrong direction. In effect, you're giving a third-party absolute control over your code and your ability to work on it. You're also disconnecting the developer's mental model of how all of this stuff works (arguably why there's so much bad code floating around). Eventually, that will devolve to people not knowing how any of this works and the industry will collapse. It's an abstract line of argumentation related to this, but Jonathan Blow nailed it on this idea a few years back: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28194961,"author":"tuckerconnelly","month":"2021-08","story":"React Renderer for Three.js","stance":"reference","why":"Endless abstraction layers leading to civilization decline","quote":"Reminds me of Jonathan Blow's talk on the end of civilization via endless layers of abstraction[1].","text":"While not incompatible with ECS, the DOM and this renderer go all-in on the javascript event-loop. You would have to write your own run loop, which executes the systems on every frame (ideally creating a DAG and executing in parallel while possible), and leave the event loop behind, with all the niceties like `onClick`, to go full ECS. Otherwise you'll create some Frankenstein monster of part ECS, part event-loop, part declarative React. Additionally, you can throw OOP in that mix as well, because Three.js has it's own whole OOP-style framework, that you're strapping declarative React on top of with this renderer. Reminds me of Jonathan Blow's talk on the end of civilization via endless layers of abstraction[1]. I really think, when it's ready, a Bevy[2]-style system either native or compiled to WASM with WebGPU will be ideal. And while I'm airing opinions (forgive me), I think writing shaders now is like SQL 30 years ago. Developers left optimizing difficult--according to them--SQL to database administrators by abstracting it away into ORMs. If history is any indicator, I think we'll be having the same arguments on Hacker News 30 years from now about 3D frameworks vs writing shaders directly as we're having now about ORMs vs writing SQL directly. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24831630,"author":"sjy","month":"2020-10","story":"The Centralized Internet Is Inevitable","stance":"reference","why":"Popular 2019 talk on software complexity mentioning Burja","quote":"Jon Blow’s popular 2019 talk on software complexity","text":"The author of this article, Samo Burja, also gave a great lecture in 2018 called “Civilization: Institutions, Knowledge and the Future” ( [LINK] ) which was mentioned in Jon Blow’s popular 2019 talk on software complexity, “Preventing the Collapse of Civilization” ( [VIDEO] ). Burja’s talk is worth watching if you’re interested in his analysis of technology and history over an even longer period, and it provides some context for his remarks at the end of the article about intellectual institutions and the benefits of centralisation."},{"id":24471584,"author":"akkartik","month":"2020-09","story":"Bootstrapping a Forth in 40 lines of Lua code (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You've probably seen Jonathan Blow's talk on the subject","text":"You've probably seen Jonathan Blow's talk on the subject: [VIDEO] I'm super interested in the subject: [LINK] . In the framework of this thread I focus on 2 of the 3 layers: problem and software. Hardware is out of scope. For now. Recently I've started thinking about BIOS (inspired by [LINK] ). So perhaps it's only a matter of time."},{"id":24209566,"author":"ipi","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: What Are the Big Problems?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Quality Software : Yes, this is becoming more and more important and is overlooked at many levels. With more of our infrastructure, lives and the future of humanity in the hands of a bunch of software & hardware. We need to take things a tad bit more seriously. I believe this is not yet a big problem but it is becoming one. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23928948,"author":"chansiky","month":"2020-07","story":"AI writing code will make software engineers more valuable","stance":"reference","why":"Knowledge disappearing across generations of programmers","quote":"in which he discusses the disappearance of knowledge as generations pass","text":"I agree with the sentiment that they only augment our capabilities, but then we end up with another problem, which is that the people who write the software are not fully aware of how it all works. Relevant to my argument is Jonathan Blow's `Preventing The collapse of Civilization` talk in which he discusses the disappearance of knowledge as generations pass: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21090228,"author":"franky47","month":"2019-09","story":"How the invention of the book changed how people read","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'd expect the bar to preserve any specific content to be a lot lower than what we had in the past. At the moment, I agree. However, as older civilizations rose and fell, their knowledge lived on in physical support, directly accessible by anyone who was taught how to read the language, therefore the assumption was that \"as long as the language lives on, books are a good archiving support\". When our modern digital civilization falls [1], I'm not sure most of it would be transmittable through long-term electronic archiving supports that may be easily readable (as easily as a written book at least) by future civilizations, because not only the language has to live on, but the technology needed to access the content also has to function. I also agree that some contents will always be more relevant to \"backup\". As for everything that happened on Facebook (same can be said for most social platforms), it is likely to entirely disappear with the company. One could even see it as a parallel civilization, that has no means of self-preservation other than that of their ruling entity's business interest. [1] Jonathan Blow - Preventing the collapse of civilization - [VIDEO]"},{"id":20066045,"author":"karlmcguire","month":"2019-05","story":"\"DigitalOcean Killed Our Company\"","stance":"reference","why":"Blow argues software progress is mostly better hardware","quote":"he talks about how we've made very little progress in the field of software","text":"This reminds me of recent talk by Jonathan Blow [1], where he talks about how we've made very little progress in the field of software and anything that appears to be progress is just software leveraging better hardware. It's quite scary how low our standards have gotten. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["U6qZPx4uD0g",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["f0gMdGrVteI",1],["E3URhJx0NSw",1],["qS34oV-jv_A",1],["ZSRHeXYDLko",1],["PbmJkMhmrVI",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["aAlR3cezPJg",1],["iukBMY4apvI",1],["kZRE7HIO3vk",1],["fsi0aJ9yr2o",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["QQhVQ1UG6aM",1],["kc_Jq42Og7Q",1]]},"z1ROpIKZe-c":{"wy":"A favorite entry point to the weird history and privileges of the City of London.","q":{"x":"My favorite gateway drugs to learning more about the city of London are probably CGP Grey's videos","i":28809026,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28809026,"author":"mstade","month":"2021-10","story":"Ireland joins OECD International Tax agreement","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gateway to City of London history and traditions","quote":"My favorite gateway drugs to learning more about the city of London are probably CGP Grey's videos","text":"I used to live and work in the city and loved telling people about the dragons guarding the city borders and the often weird and funny traditions. As much as the city is a hub of corporations it's actually far from the soulless place many make it out to be, and there is lots of interesting history there if you care to look. My favorite gateway drugs to learning more about the city of London are probably CGP Grey's videos: The (Secret) City of London: History Edition: [LINK] The (Secret) City of London: Government Edition: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13866182,"author":"Hagelin","month":"2017-03","story":"London’s Big Dig Reveals Layers of History","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great videos on City of London versus London","quote":"are great videos on the topic of the City of London (vs. London)","text":"CGP Grey's \"The (Secret) City of London\" are great videos on the topic of the City of London (vs. London) Part 1: [LINK] Part 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35232294,"author":"rjmunro","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"rec","why":"Follow-up on how City elects its government","quote":"There's a great video by CGP Grey about it here","text":"There's a great video by CGP Grey about it here: [LINK] , and a follow up about how they elect their government: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33077337,"author":"Lio","month":"2022-10","story":"The City of London’s oldest document is currently on public display","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Lord Mayor election process well","quote":"Here's another video from CGP Grey that explains it rather well:-","text":"Well on the one hand you're saying it's not that strange, on the other there's the election process for who can become Lord Mayor of the City of London. ;) In most parts of the country the \"Worshipful Society of Apothecaries\" plays very little part in local governance... Here's another video from CGP Grey that explains it rather well:- [VIDEO] ...and when did all this start? No body knows. It's so old there are no records."},{"id":19180917,"author":"mstade","month":"2019-02","story":"London's billion-pound guilds","stance":"rec","why":"Fun intro to the City's weirdness","quote":"For anyone looking for a fun little intro to the weirdness of the city and it’s history, I recommend CGP Grey’s channel","text":"I don’t know whether this is true because I never looked into it really, but a colleague of mine in the ndustry told me I could join the banker’s guild. His words were that because I lived in the city[1] at the time, and also worked for a bank in the city, I’d be eligible for membership if the bank would nominate or sponsor me as a representative. I’m hazy on the details, it was years ago, and I don’t really know whether it’s true since as mentioned I never checked it out. I kind of regret not doing so now though, since moving out of the city I’ve learned much more of it’s (weird and interesting) history and it would’ve been fun to at least try to see some of this from the inside. It probably wouldn’t have worked anyway, but would’ve been fun to try. For anyone looking for a fun little intro to the weirdness of the city and it’s history, I recommend CGP Grey’s channel and particularly his videos on the City of London[2][3]. [1]: for the benefit of people who might not be aware – “the city” is a colloquialism for the City of London, which is a small somewhat autonomous part of London, situated east of what most people (who don’t know this) probably think of when you say the city of London. [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11693064,"author":"Malic","month":"2016-05","story":"Dark money: London’s dirty secret","stance":"rec","why":"History, privileges, and how to become mayor","quote":"CGP Grey did a nice two-parter video describing its history, its privileges","text":"If anyone cares to know more about the City of London, CGP Grey did a nice two-parter video describing its history, its privileges and how to become mayor if you care to. The (Secret) City of London, Part 1: History [LINK] The (Secret) City of London, Part 2: Government [VIDEO]"},{"id":10707533,"author":"yoshamano","month":"2015-12","story":"The fall of Jersey: how a tax haven goes bust","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of City of London governing structure","quote":"CGP Grey does a really good job giving an overview of the history of The City of London.","text":"CGP Grey does a really good job giving an overview of the history of The City of London. [LINK] Along with another video about the governing structure of The City of London. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11112343,"author":"jgroszko","month":"2016-02","story":"Under-35s in the UK face becoming permanent renters, warns thinktank","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the City of London's special status","quote":"","text":"You're probably thinking of the City of London, which is an area inside London that has it's own government and special privileges apart from the rest of what we think of as London. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10495230,"author":"Pyxl101","month":"2015-11","story":"Lessig Ends Presidential Campaign","stance":"reference","why":"Mayor of London selection process","quote":"","text":"It's an interesting point. How does one determine when one is past the point of being a spoiler and into being a serious candidate? It seems that there is a self-fulfilling prophesy here, wherein if one is concerned about being a spoiler, then one will begin drawing votes away from other viable candidates as soon as they enter politics, and thus spoil; but yet, in order to ever become a viable candidate in the first place, one must do that. Perhaps the counter-argument is that you could enter politics away from an election, to \"test the waters\" without drawing support away from other candidates. But that's not a serious way to enter politics. Or you could enter politics at a different level, a lower level. I don't know if that's a viable way for Lessig to accomplish his aim. The same type of problems he's talking about might exist in other political spheres, but they aren't the ones that seem to concern him primarily. I can buy those arguments, but they leave me uneasy for a reason I can't characterize. It reminds me somewhat of the process of becoming the mayor of the city of London [1], where you're only allowed to run once several groups of people approve of you. It seems like Lessig is being held to a different standard, almost, because he is entering politics with a specific policy objective in mind, rather than because he himself wants to become a leader. Most politicians enter politics simply to win and become leader, and so they can be judged by whether they win. Less"},{"id":10201662,"author":"fennecfoxen","month":"2015-09","story":"Travelling to work 'is work', European court rules","stance":"reference","why":"City of London's odd government","quote":"","text":"In programming terms: namespacing. You have a set of activities which people do. You have validations on those activities. To determine whether a working arrangement is legal, all validations must pass. The UK has one set of validations. The EU has another. Some of them check the same things. Both the EU and UK validations make use of abstractions. One of these abstractions they reference is a particular symbol named 'work'. However, the symbol for 'work' is not defined in a common namespace or dictionary, but is instead private to each ruleset. Therefore if the EU court rules that 'work' must be defined a certain way for the purposes of EU regulations, it does not affect the UK regulations. If you think that's at all confusing wait until you hear about the government of the city of London. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LrObZ_HZZUc",5],["ulCdoCfw-bY",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["fKy7ljRr0AA",1]]},"54l8_ewcOlY":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as a thorough, non-dogmatic look at meaning, spirituality and escaping nihilism.","q":{"x":"this series has it all.","i":28185182,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28185182,"author":"sophrocyne","month":"2021-08","story":"The uncanny absence of nihilism","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on philosophy, evolution of religion, and escaping nihilism","quote":"this series has it all.","text":"For anyone deeply interested in philosophy, the evolution of religion from a cognitive science perspective, or escaping a nihilistic perspective, this series has it all. I can’t recommend it enough. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33742000,"author":"Raminj95","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Why is Aristotle so respected?","stance":"rec","why":"Episodes on Aristotle's influence on our grammar and concepts","quote":"I think the best answer to this question can be found in this series","text":"I think the best answer to this question can be found in this series, i think episodes 5-7 is where he talks about the importance of Aristotle and the ways in which he shaped our grammar around so many concepts that are central to our day to day lives. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32901945,"author":"thenerdhead","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: Activity and reading to move through mid-life crisis?","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies feelings of disconnect that can lead to a life crisis","quote":"he has brought clarity to the feelings I had that might have resulted in one.","text":"Maybe check out John Vervaeke's Awakening from the Meaning Crisis videos on YouTube: [VIDEO] Perhaps you might relate to how he describes this disconnect. While I don't think I've gone through a life crisis yet, he has brought clarity to the feelings I had that might have resulted in one."},{"id":31969927,"author":"barrysteve","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: What exactly is a mindfulness meditation?","stance":"rec","why":"History of spirituality as psycho-technology, cognitive science breakdown","quote":"It is very good and leads into discussions with him and Curt Jaimungal","text":"> Other gurus teach that meditation is “doing nothing”. Okay, but then again there’s nothing special about it, we all do it from time to time. Eastern spirituality sees \"nothing\" or nothingness as a spiritual place to relax in and be. Western religions see \"nothing\" as something that can't exist. That nothing is impossible for Christ. One function of prayer and meditation is to organize your thoughts and let go of thoughts and aims that are not helpful, sometimes called Sin. You can let them go into nothingness (eastern), into the altar of fire (catholicism) or let them flow away (anglican/methodist). You can also use prayer to aim and think about things that are good for you and you do want. If you want real detail, John Vervaeke's Awakening from the Meaning Crisis series [0] does the history of spirituality as a psycho-technology, cog-sci breakdown. It is very good and leads into discussions with him and Curt Jaimungal [1] on the Theories of Everything channel, which has a slightly more Eastern and hyper-intellectual physics blend to it. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] The practice of prayer and meditation has been degraded by practicing psychologists and pop culture. Stay away from that stuff, it eats into the prayer life and meditation from the inside or bottom-up."},{"id":30222868,"author":"sophrocyne","month":"2022-02","story":"The Reality War – It’s just Gnostics all the way down","stance":"rec","why":"Series on the problem driving people to seek such communities","quote":"There was a series focusing on the underlying problem driving people to seek these types of communities","text":"This was a great read. There was a series focusing on the underlying problem driving people to seek these types of communities - [VIDEO] The series touched on Gnosticism, and how it developed into future philosophies - even evolving into the foundation of the Nazi belief system. We’re at a point where, due to a wholesale rejection of religion, we’ve thrown the baby (practical wisdom traditions) out with the bath water (improbable metaphysical axioms) The author also touches on the notion of a metaverse, and I’ve been writing on the subject as well. This vision of a “metaverse”, as the author alludes to, is one that is not divorced from reality but perpendicular to it. It could be a great thing, or a digital hell. We live in weird times, and collectively are in dire need of wisdom."},{"id":21940583,"author":"westoncb","month":"2020-01","story":"Philosophy as a public service","stance":"rec","why":"New lecture series on the crisis of meaning","quote":"he also paints a pretty interesting picture by the time he gets to that point","text":"Here's an interesting new series addressing exactly the \"crisis of meaning\" issue you mention: [VIDEO] I've only gotten through the first lecture—and it does take him ~20 minutes to lay out something like the full plan for the lecture series, but he also paints a pretty interesting picture by the time he gets to that point. David Chapman has also been writing on this subject, apparently from a somewhat related angle, for years: [LINK] The two of them try reconciling their approaches here: [LINK]"},{"id":21038831,"author":"crispinb","month":"2019-09","story":"The effect of meditation on brain structure (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture series on meaning and spiritual traditions without other-worldly metaphysics","quote":"John Vervaeke's fascinating 'Awakening from the Meaning Crisis' lecture series","text":"> bogus nonsensical ones that say you need to light candles and get into some funky position and whatnot. It usually helps when investigating something you're a novice in to at least attempt to clear your mind of preconceptions. Do you know posture makes no difference? Where did you get that information from if you're a complete novice? Do you know setting (candles, incense) makes no difference? Where did you get that information from? Untrained intuition? It's notable that 'spiritual'* traditions from many different places and times emphasise posture & setting. It wouldn't do any harm to at least consider whether there may be more to this than you're assuming. * see (amongst many possible sources) John Vervaeke's fascinating 'Awakening from the Meaning Crisis' lecture series for why this doesn't necessarily connote other-worldly metaphysics: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["D9H9qTdserM",1],["s8nFqwOho-s",1]]},"hx7BXih7zx8":{"wy":"HN repeatedly points to it as the best public technical overview of Tesla's FSD approach, used both to defend and to critique Tesla's progress.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching head of AI at Tesla Andrej Karpathy's talk on their self driving architecture.","i":25705486,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27989387,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-07","story":"Tesla auto-pilot keeps confusing moon with traffic light then slowing down","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good overview of Tesla's vision architecture and fleet edge-case collection.","quote":"As a robotics engineer interested in vision, their architecture is inspiring.","text":"You can learn about their systems by watching talks by Andrej Karpathy. As a robotics engineer interested in vision, their architecture is inspiring. This talk [1] is a good overview but each talk he gives is a little different so search for more if you want to know as much as possible. But the big thing is that their autonomy computer can be programmed to look for odd scenarios and send them back home. Tesla uses their fleet of hundreds of thousands of cars to collect edge cases like this, and then they have a kind of compartmentalized neural network system that breaks apart disparate tasks. With their collected examples they can create unit tests to ensure that the moon stops activating the stoplight detector. Once trained, the unit tests presumably help ensure they don't end up with future regressions. So basically every time you see a Tesla do a weird thing, there is a good chance it will stop doing it soon enough. At least if it's hitting hacker news. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25705486,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-01","story":"Tesla to produce $25K car as early as 2022","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Karpathy on self-driving architecture and fleet as data source.","quote":"I highly recommend watching head of AI at Tesla Andrej Karpathy's talk on their self driving architecture.","text":"Removing the cameras may be a good idea, but note that their extensive fleet of camera-equipped vehicles is an important part of their potentially VERY lucrative self driving software development. EDIT: I highly recommend watching head of AI at Tesla Andrej Karpathy's talk on their self driving architecture. \"The Fleet\" as a source of data is a vital functional part of their self driving software development. They may be able to calculate that including cameras and a computer on a car that will ship in high volumes would increase sales of their self driving option across all Tesla vehicles (including that low cost model) enough to justify the inclusion of those parts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23837632,"author":"maxharris","month":"2020-07","story":"German court bans Tesla ad statements related to autonomous driving","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Technical presentation by Tesla's head of AI on full self-driving.","quote":"have you watched this entire technical presentation made by Andrej Karpathy, Senior Director of AI at Tesla?","text":"If you actually believe that Tesla will fail to deliver full self-driving in the coming years, I have two questions. 1. have you watched this entire technical presentation made by Andrej Karpathy, Senior Director of AI at Tesla? [VIDEO] 2. if you understand what you've seen in that video, why do you think Tesla will fail?"},{"id":26033849,"author":"maxharris","month":"2021-02","story":"Trump left a traffic-safety mess for Biden.Item one:Tesla’s self-driving claims","stance":"rec","why":"High-level technical overview of Tesla's FSD efforts.","quote":"If you'd like a high-level technical overview of Tesla's FSD efforts, check out this talk by Andrej Karpathy from February 2020","text":"This is anti-Tesla astroturfing, so I flagged it. If you'd like a high-level technical overview of Tesla's FSD efforts, check out this talk by Andrej Karpathy from February 2020: [VIDEO] There are literally thousands of videos of people filming the FSD beta doing amazing things. They are making great progress very, very quickly: [LINK] As you can see from videos like the one above, real-world data is needed to make FSD practical. There is a very long tail of things to get right, so blocking this progress with regulations would effectively outlaw FSD, or delay its development by a very long time. Given the number of road accidents that human drivers cause (think of just sleep deprivation and drunk driving), delaying FSD through regulation would ultimately cause a great number of uncessary deaths."},{"id":24437739,"author":"fossuser","month":"2020-09","story":"Nikola: How to Parlay an Ocean of Lies into a Partnership with GM","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Tesla's approach and that the progress is real.","quote":"You can watch that talk and see the approach they're taking.","text":"It's not the same: [VIDEO] You can watch that talk and see the approach they're taking. Maybe you're more skeptical than they are about the near term possibility, but you can see that the work and progress is real. The AGI risk is real too. What Milton said doesn't make semantic sense, it's not a question of timelines. [LINK]"},{"id":24602302,"author":"maxharris","month":"2020-09","story":"Tesla’s Nemesis in China Is a Tiny $5k Electric Car from GM","stance":"rec","why":"Technical talk demonstrating Tesla's autonomy progress.","quote":"Here's a Tesla engineer that gave a great technical talk demonstrating their progress as of early this year","text":"Better yet, let's get rid of cars altogether from city streets. This is coming along very nicely... [LINK] Autonomous driving is just around the corner, too, which means that there is no need to waste space on parking structures, either. Here's a Tesla engineer that gave a great technical talk demonstrating their progress as of early this year: [VIDEO] EDIT: yes, there are clear reasons why having an three-dimensional underground structure will absolutely affect the social dynamics above-ground in a city, yet it remains true that it is possible to banish cars from downtown cores in the near future (i.e., say by 2030 or so), given the present shift to electrification and autonomous driving. These issues are worthy of debate, and I'm just trying to shift the conversation to what's current. I'm not trying to rain on anyone's parade! I love the goal of city streets for people, as exemplified by the beautiful laneways in Melbourne (11 minutes): [LINK]"},{"id":23162842,"author":"kjksf","month":"2020-05","story":"Waymo has raised $3B in the last two months","stance":"rec","why":"Karpathy explains fleet data gathering for training self-driving neural nets.","quote":"Here's the most recent talk by Karpathy about this topic","text":"Here's the most recent talk by Karpathy about this topic: [VIDEO] (there are more if you google for \"karpathy talk\") Here's the summary via 2 examples. 1. Example of gathering data that needs further labeling To implement neural network (NN) to recognize stop signs they program the cars to recognize things that look like stop signs and deploy that to the fleet of over 600 thousand cars on the road. The cars send those \"might or might not be a stop sign\" images back to tesla and they get manually labelled and added to a training set. They gather enough images and the \"recognize stop signs\" feature is done. Apply the same logic to other recognition tasks: recognize cars, pedestrians, animals, speed signs, traffic lights etc. Yes, labeling is expensive but everyone else (including Waymo) has the same cost. But Waymo has even bigger cost of driving around to collect those images. Tesla makes gross profit on every car they sell and they got 600 thousand people driving for them for free. Waymo has 600 cars, each of them reportedly costing over $200k and they have to pay drivers at least minimum wage for each hour of driving. That's why Google is reportedly is spending $1 billion a year on Waymo and why they need $3 billion of additional investment to keep going. 2. Example of gathering data that doesn't need labeling Consider implementing NN to recognize cut in i.e. other cars entering your lane in front of you. They deploy a first version trained on small sample, running in shadow"},{"id":33087434,"author":"joewadcan","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla Vision Update: Replacing Ultrasonic Sensors with Tesla Vision","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"They are reliable enough to use for FSD","text":"They are reliable enough to use for FSD: [VIDEO] so i think it's reliable enough for park assist, summon etc. Yeah you're def right about the moisture sealing... it's been hit or miss on production tolerances"},{"id":32401878,"author":"jsight","month":"2022-08","story":"Tesla’s self-driving technology fails to detect children in the road, tests find","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for broader safety analysis scenarios.","quote":"A comprehensive safety analysis has to also include these scenarios","text":"The actual report is here: [LINK] It failed to detect a stationary mannequin in one specific scenario. Does this generalize? Does this mean there's a major safety problem? These would be assumptions that this test can not prove. A comprehensive safety analysis has to also include these scenarios: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31708147,"author":"baryphonic","month":"2022-06","story":"US moves closer to recalling Tesla’s self-driving software","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy talks indicate a more deliberate development approach.","quote":"I've seen a few talks from Andrej Karpathy that indicate to me a more deliberate approach.[0]","text":"> All evidence from their own AI/AP team and presentations is that there is no real design and system validation going on over there. They're flying by the seat of their pants, introducing potentially lethal regressions in every update. What is this evidence? I've seen a few talks from Andrej Karpathy that indicate to me a more deliberate approach.[0] \"Software 2.0\" itself seems like an approach meant to systematize the development, validation & testing of AI systems, hardly a seat-of-your-pants approach to releases. I have my own criticisms of their approach, but it seems there is pretty deliberate care taken when developing models. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30165098,"author":"wcoenen","month":"2022-02","story":"Tesla to recall vehicles that may disobey stop signs","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy on neural nets gradually replacing traditional code.","quote":"Karpathy has commented about the neural nets gradually replacing the traditional code. See his presentation[2] around 18:45.","text":"There is a mix of a \"neural net planner\" and a \"explicit planning and control\" in traditional code[1]. The explicit planner has the last word, and uses input from both the \"vector space\" and the neural net planner. Karpathy has commented about the neural nets gradually replacing the traditional code. See his presentation[2] around 18:45. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27868541,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-07","story":"OpenAI disbands its robotics research team","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the work needed to make a robot drive safely.","quote":"You can see how much work Tesla has had to do to get a robot to safely drive on public roads.","text":"Not yet. I have a four wheel drive robot I designed with four 4k cameras feeding in to an Nvidia Jetson Xavier. [1] Just getting it to navigate itself using vision would mean building a complex system with a lot of pieces (beyond the most basic demo anyway). You need separate neural nets doing all kinds of different tasks and you need a massive training system for it all. You can see how much work Tesla has had to do to get a robot to safely drive on public roads. [2] From where I am sitting now, I think we are making good inroads on something like an \"Imagenet moment\" for robots. (Well, I should note that I am a robotics engineer but I mostly work on driver level software and hardware, not AI. Though I follow the research from the outside.) It seems like a combination of transformers plus scale plus cross domain reasoning like CLIP [3] could begin to build a system that could mimic humans. I guess as good as transformers are we still haven't solved how to get them to learn for themselves, and that's probably a hard requirement for really being useful in the real world. Good work in RL happening there though. Gosh, yeah, this is gonna take decades lol. Maybe we will have a spark that unites all this in one efficient system. Improving transformer efficiency and achieving big jumps in scale are a combo that will probably get interesting stuff solved. All the groundwork is a real slog. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":25555939,"author":"maxharris","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Are we in tech-stock bubble?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm sorry you missed out on one of the best investments of the decade, but there is a lot of room left for this to run. Tesla's coming FSD and robotaxis still haven't been priced in. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24437613,"author":"fossuser","month":"2020-09","story":"Nikola: How to Parlay an Ocean of Lies into a Partnership with GM","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Tesla's fleet-based training approach.","quote":"","text":"You set goals and work towards them. Becoming an inter-planetary species doesn't happen in a year. I want SpaceX to succeed and they have a track record of execution such that I now believe they really can. I was hopeful before (and if you listen to Musk talk about it he didn't think they'd be able to really pull it off early on either but figured they'd at least make progress towards it even if they failed), but now I think a mars colony is a real possible outcome. It's not a bold take to just state something is impossible until it happens, that's pretty much the default. The bold take is to look at what might be possible and execute goals in pursuit of that. For SpaceX this means reusable rocket technology to bring costs down (massive success here has them ahead of everyone else). Starlink as a revenue source is also a really good approach. For Tesla it's the 'master plan' of roadster -> model s -> model 3, reinvesting in infrastructure and battery technology with vertical integration to build out superchargers and drive costs down. This has been massively successful and their EVs (particularly the model3/y) have no equal at any price point EV or gas. The level 5 autonomy was really a bonus on top of that EV transition that they've added to, and if anyone can pull it off it will be Andrej Karpathy and the fleet of Tesla's they can train with ( [VIDEO] ). Bullshit and really big ideas can sound similar, but that doesn't mean they are - there's a lot of value in being able to"},{"id":23280178,"author":"KKKKkkkk1","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: What startup/technology is on your 'to watch' list?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of custom stop-sign detector used to critique scalability.","quote":"Andrej Karpathy's most recent presentation showed how his team trained a custom detector for stop signs","text":"LIDAR vs camera is a red herring. The fact that Elon and his fan club fixate on this shows you how little they understand about self driving. The fundamental problem is that there is no technology that can provide the level of reasoning that is necessary for self driving. Andrej Karpathy's most recent presentation showed how his team trained a custom detector for stop signs with an \"Except right turn\" text underneath them [0]. How are they going to scale that to a system that understands any text sign in any human language? The answer is that they're not even trying, which tells you that Tesla is not building a self-driving system. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22970810,"author":"maxharris","month":"2020-04","story":"American billionaires have gotten $280B richer since the start of the pandemic","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of Tesla's lead in autonomous driving.","quote":"LiDAR is a dead-end, and they're ahead of everyone else there, too","text":"It's not luck. I have a degree in molecular biology, and I took a lot of elective courses in immunology, oncology, virology. I worked in a research lab on HIV vaccine for two years after undergrad before I switched back to software engineering. So I was able to keep a cooler head about the virus than most, which enabled me to buy intelligently. I worked damn hard to earn that degree and in my time in the lab. I was a twitchy premed student that needed every grade to be perfect. NONE of the effort I poured into those six years of my life was luck. It was pure sweat. In the immediate future, I expect that the dip in advertising will cause Q1 earnings from Google and Facebook to be lower than expected. As the Fed continues to inflate stocks, this will cause a lot more buying of TSLA and AAPL. In the 2020s, Tesla will continue to soar because: 1) they have a huge lead in battery tech. they're about to announce that they've achieved the $100/kWh mark, which will let them sell electric cars that go as far as gas ones but don't cost more. 2) batteries are crucial for amazing tech like the emerging eVTOL scene. it's possible Tesla may enter that space and dominate it, because batteries determine everything there, too. in the meantime, I bought some shares of eHang (Chinese company, listed on the Nasdaq under EH) 3) they have a huge lead in autonomous driving. LiDAR is a dead-end, and they're ahead of everyone else there, too: [VIDEO] 4) demand is not a problem at all, especially in C"}],"r":[["KkpqA8yG9T4",2],["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["EpRrD58Ah3Q",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["-s5szfPYKY4",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1],["nqzpAbK9qFk",1],["7R4xhwy-1oI",1],["wUwZZaI5u0c",1],["uBh2LxTW0s0",1],["xsLJZyih3Ac",1],["Ucp0TTmvqOE",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["qEV9qoup2mQ",1]]},"HQYsFshbkYw":{"wy":"A favorite on Ask HN lists of programming YouTube channels, praised for mind-blowing systems and graphics programming and a watchable coding style.","q":{"x":"My favourite programming YouTuber by a long stretch.","i":19026585,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19026585,"author":"petercooper","month":"2019-01","story":"Let’s Create a Speech Synthesizer in C++17 [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic Bisqwit video building a Doom-style 3D engine.","quote":"My favourite programming YouTuber by a long stretch.","text":"Agreed. My favourite programming YouTuber by a long stretch. If anyone is unfamiliar with him and wants to watch some of the \"classics\" I'd recommend these: His \"cracking videogame passwords\" series: [LINK] Creating a Doom-style 3D engine in C: [VIDEO] Creating a Rogue-like game in C++11: [LINK] Creating a PIC16 emulator and NES music player: [LINK] Creating a NES emulator: [LINK] Creating a DOS VGA raytracer: [LINK]"},{"id":16224537,"author":"iwakura","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite YouTube channel for developers?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Systems and graphics programming that will blow your mind.","quote":"they will still blow your mind though.","text":"Bisqwit's video focusing on systems and graphics programming are total gems. [LINK] Not particularly useful for web developers which is what most programmers are nowdays, they will still blow your mind though. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12703000,"author":"petercooper","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite YouTube channel for developers?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bisqwit builds engines, emulators and more from scratch.","quote":"by far my favorite","text":"[LINK] is by far my favorite and I've spent many hours watching him. He does things like create a Doom-style engine from scratch: [VIDEO] .. create a NES emulator: [LINK] .. work back from a C++17 example to show why new C++ standards are needed: [LINK] .. and even building a Tetris clone in GW-BASIC: [LINK] .. Right now, he's doing a series on cracking 80s videogame passwords: [LINK] Sirajology - [LINK] - is another interesting one. He moves a bit too quickly for me, but does things like explain machine learning in 5 minutes or how to generate music with systems like Tensorflow."},{"id":10088971,"author":"petercooper","month":"2015-08","story":"The Strange Appeal of Watching Coders Code","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sped-up coding that follows the thought process well.","quote":"The typing effect is sped up and faked and done at just the right speed","text":"This is why I love bisqwit's videos on YouTube. He takes a middle ground between \"typing it in\" and pondered narration. The typing effect is sped up and faked and done at just the right speed to 'follow along' with his thought process. Example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15124375,"author":"rpeden","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask HN: Best coding related YouTube channels?","stance":"rec","why":"Builds a basic 3D engine in C, prototyped in Basic.","quote":"Mostly because he does fun things that are outside of the kind of programming I usually do, so it helps me broaden my horizons a bit.","text":"I'm a fan of Bisqwit: [LINK] Mostly because he does fun things that are outside of the kind of programming I usually do, so it helps me broaden my horizons a bit. One of my favourites is the video where he sets out to make a basic 3D game engine in C, but starts by prototyping it in Basic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18811918,"author":"TazeTSchnitzel","month":"2019-01","story":"Non-Euclidean Worlds Engine [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Simple demo engine showing non-Euclidean geometry.","quote":"a video about making a simple demo engine based around the concept","text":"As was Duke Nukem 3D. Here's a video about making a simple demo engine based around the concept, which shows off a little non-Euclidean geometry: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["N8elxpSu9pw",1],["y71lli8MS8s",1],["63vqob-MljQ",1],["eRkgK4jfi6M",1],["gPlpphT7n9s",1],["oLiheMQayNE",1],["PUv66718DII",1]]},"M6vpFV6Wkl4":{"wy":"Recommended in diet and weight-loss threads as the accessible case for the carbohydrate-insulin view of obesity.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend you watch this amazing lecture by Garry Taubes at Google","i":5248686,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5248686,"author":"TomGullen","month":"2013-02","story":"What Does 200 Calories Look Like?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture with case studies on fat, obesity and diet","quote":"Highly recommend you watch this amazing lecture by Garry Taubes at Google","text":"msluyter correctly points to the Inuits as a good case against what you've said. Their diet consists pretty much entirely fat and meat yet they have lower heart disease and rates of cancer. [LINK] Pretty strange how their not keeling over from heart disease! Perhaps the traditional view of fat is wrong. Highly recommend you watch this amazing lecture by Garry Taubes at Google: [VIDEO] He also brings a lot of other eye opening case studies to light such as obesity in Pima Indians during a famine."},{"id":3416606,"author":"nosequel","month":"2012-01","story":"Learning to Eat Well and Stay Healthy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Taubes argues calorie type matters more than calorie count","quote":"Please watch videos from Gary Taubes who wrote \"Good Calories Bad Calories\" and \"Why we get fat\". Here's his google talk","text":"Actually you are most likely wrong. There is really good evidence that the number of calories don't matter and that the type of calories are everything. People associate the number of calories as mattering because they change other things at the same time. Please watch videos from Gary Taubes who wrote \"Good Calories Bad Calories\" and \"Why we get fat\". Here's his google talk: [VIDEO] On a personal note, I lost 40 lbs and I eat far more calories now than I did before. Open you mind for a bit and really watch that video, you might be convinced that what you think about calories are all wrong. I certainly changed what I thought was true as fact."},{"id":32944515,"author":"allsunny","month":"2022-09","story":"Lose weight the slow and incredibly difficult way","stance":"rec","why":"Taubes questions calories-in-calories-out thinking before skeptical Googlers","quote":"reminds me of a good talk where some googlers got a little upset when gary taubes started going into the problems with CICO thinking.","text":"i get the joke but reminds me of a good talk where some googlers got a little upset when gary taubes started going into the problems with CICO thinking. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6578158,"author":"TomGullen","month":"2013-10","story":"Sweden becomes the first country to reject low-fat diet dogma","stance":"rec","why":"Taubes explains reasons carbs are harmful beyond satiety","quote":"Garry Taubes explains a lot of reasons very well","text":"> The only issue with sugars are the null effect on satiety and promotion of binge eating. > sugars make it easy to overeat Those are issues, for sure! And great reasons why when dieting, a good foundation is exclusion of carbs. But that's only scratching the surface. Let me give you another downside. Eating sugar causes arterial inflammation. When your arteries inflame, your body packs it with oxidized LDL which can lead to heart problems and strokes due to constriction in your arteries. Guess what causes arterial inflammation? There's more as well. We need to brush our teeth because of our high carb diets. It causes tooth decay and gum disease. Before high carb diets, our ancestors had healthier mouths than we do today. [LINK] > \"You're walking around with a permanent immune response, which is not a good thing,\" says Cooper. \"It causes problems all over the place.\" More as well! It turns out that calories is an oversimplified model of dieting. Here's a recent interesting study that paints it clearly: [LINK] But there's more! Garry Taubes explains a lot of reasons very well: [VIDEO] There are so many reasons carbs are bad for you, not just from a satiation point of view but also from long term health points of view. I'm confident this post has actually done a disservice to the question why should we eat less carbs, but hopefully it's something you might find interesting and read more about and maybe change your opinion on. All the information, studies and evidence is out the"},{"id":6002362,"author":"TelmoMenezes","month":"2013-07","story":"How I have lost over 100 pounds and don’t know how","stance":"rec","why":"Arguments for ketogenic diets backed by explanatory models","quote":"Check out this talk if you want to know more","text":"The problem here is that mainstream nutrition \"science\" is also a fad. It is also a dramatic example on how the Internet + an incentive can produce better Science than the traditional grant (and lobby system). Many of the traditional dogmas of nutrition have recently come intro question: the idea that eating cholesterol is what increases cholesterol in your arteries, the idea that salt is bad for you, the idea that fiber is good for you and many others. Regarding ketogenic diets, there are so many results and it seems to work so much better than what \"they\" have been telling us that the idea that this is a \"fad\" starts to sound a bit silly. Actually, some proponents of ketogenic diets are backing their claims with explanatory models, instead of simple epidemiological studies (which are, in my opinion, the lowest form of Science). Check out this talk if you want to know more: [VIDEO] edit: this talk provides some convincing arguments against your assumption #1."},{"id":6002792,"author":"crusso","month":"2013-07","story":"How I have lost over 100 pounds and don’t know how","stance":"rec","why":"Presents evidence on diet science that was long ignored","quote":"Here's tons of evidence and someone wondering why we've ignored that evidence for so long.","text":"Another user already posted this link. Here's tons of evidence and someone wondering why we've ignored that evidence for so long. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3416796,"author":"nosequel","month":"2012-01","story":"Learning to Eat Well and Stay Healthy","stance":"rec","why":"Taubes challenges calories in, calories out","quote":"Taubes google talk","text":"Please explain how countless cultures who are severely malnutritioned and live off of less than 600 calories a day are obese? There are no credible studies that are currently being done that can answer those questions with calories in / calories out. Because it is simply wrong. Taubes google talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":3400896,"author":"simsimsalabim","month":"2011-12","story":"The Fat Trap: Our Bodies Are Fighting Against Us","stance":"rec","why":"Taubes talk offered as counterpoint to a flawed PDF","quote":"but you should really decide for yourself","text":"The linked pdf makes a number of factual errors. it incorrectly suggests that ketogenic don't encourage eating lots of vegetables and low-sugar fruits. It also trys to twist \"not all calories are the same\" into \"you can eat as much as you want\". and of course, trans fats should always be avoided. but you should really decide for yourself: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["jgOTZe07eHA",1]]},"wGt4XwBbCvA":{"wy":"Recommended as a sober, physics-based account of how energy underlies the economy.","q":{"x":"this doc illustrates the enormous realities of energy challenges with sober clarity often exposed by Jancovici","i":23043159,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30299020,"author":"yamrzou","month":"2022-02","story":"France to Build Six New Nuclear Reactors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jancovici talks in English raised climate awareness.","quote":"Jancovici is a legend.","text":"Jancovici is a legend. Listenening to him and reading his latest comic book ( Le monde sans fin ) made me very aware of climate issues and directly influenced my behavior. He deserves to be better known by international audiences. Here are some of his talks in english: - [VIDEO] - [LINK]"},{"id":37490734,"author":"BoiledCabbage","month":"2023-09","story":"WTF Happened in 1971?","stance":"rec","why":"Frames economy as energy transformation.","quote":"it does accurately summarize our economy as one of energy transformation","text":"I haven't read anything that I've felt covered it fully. If you want something easy to consume on our economy and technological progress essentially being getting better at converting energy to productive work you can check out this somewhat decent link from Jancovici: [VIDEO] It meanders a bit for the first 5-7 mins, but does a decent job of making the argument. Min 40 also has another pretty good slide. It does go into covering recommendations which I'm not necessarily recommending, but it does accurately summarize our economy as one of energy transformation. The best analogy in it is this. Right now if we need to do construction and dig a 10ft x 10ft x 10ft hole we can pull out a backhoe and be done in a min. If we with all of our current knowledge had to do it without consuming energy we'd be digging by hand with a shovel, and no faster than someone pre-industrial would. When you start looking at every industry you come to the same conclusion. All of our technological know how boils down to just better ways to be more productive in converting energy. But fundamentally consuming energy to be productive is the modern economy."},{"id":29171024,"author":"nunja","month":"2021-11","story":"Macron says France will build new nuclear energy reactors","stance":"rec","why":"Content by a French nuclear-minded ecologist.","quote":"the content is amazing and he definitely have a point if you want to follow [0]","text":"Jean Marc Jancovici is one of the most vocal of we can call \"Nuclear ecologist\" around in France, the content is amazing and he definitely have a point if you want to follow [0]. He is a realist, but in this field we need to go beyond and to open big money for the research into small and decentralized nuclear power. Every citizen have to know the pros and the cons, and be educated to the risks. Energy provide good living standards, but we have to know the drawbacks and we have to account for externalities in every business model. This will be the only way to keep our standards of living and keep an habitable home. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23043159,"author":"artur_makly","month":"2020-05","story":"Climate experts call for 'dangerous' Michael Moore film to be taken down","stance":"rec","why":"Sober illustration of energy challenges.","quote":"this doc illustrates the enormous realities of energy challenges with sober clarity often exposed by Jancovici","text":"this doc illustrates the enormous realities of energy challenges with sober clarity often exposed by Jancovici [VIDEO]"},{"id":22659353,"author":"H8crilA","month":"2020-03","story":"Stagnation and Scientific Incentives","stance":"rec","why":"Links energy consumption to GDP stagnation.","quote":"You have to look at energy consumption (joules / year). The correlations are uncanny and seem to explain just about everything.","text":"There's a much much simpler explanation for the stagnation of real GDP. You have to look at energy consumption (joules / year). The correlations are uncanny and seem to explain just about everything. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] It's so simple and accurate that I have no idea why doesn't everybody know about this. It better than discovering central banking."},{"id":19278719,"author":"blencdr","month":"2019-03","story":"Cheap renewables won’t stop global warming (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Conference on energy, economics and physics.","quote":"Another really interesting conference [1] about energy, economics and physics.","text":"Another really interesting conference [1] about energy, economics and physics. Bill Gates is right and the path to reduce our greenhouse gas emission won't be easy. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5Tek9h3a5wQ",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["s254IPHXgVA",1],["S9JGmA5_unY",1],["nm1TxQj9IsQ",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["RzWB5jL5RX0",1],["2tdiKTSdE9Y",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1]]},"iGFqfTCL2fs":{"wy":"HN likes it as a fun, clear explainer of traffic waves and of the benefits of leaving gaps while driving.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite videos of all time on this topic: Traffic Waves","i":30907151,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30907151,"author":"wildrhythms","month":"2022-04","story":"A smartphone app that allows public to submit evidence of speeding drivers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fun explainer on traffic wave phenomena","quote":"One of my favorite videos of all time on this topic: Traffic Waves","text":"One of my favorite videos of all time on this topic: Traffic Waves [VIDEO] (Not new information, but a fun video, and it's something truckers have known about for decades)"},{"id":18638562,"author":"TheSmiddy","month":"2018-12","story":"After 48 hours we haven't seen any sign people are using Waymo's service","stance":"rec","why":"Describes advantages of leaving gaps while driving","quote":"I really like this video for describing some of the advantages of \"zen driving\"","text":"I leave massive gaps constantly now, it lets me see farther ahead so I can switch lanes when I see a problem coming and my average trip time is definitely shorter because of this. I really like this video for describing some of the advantages of \"zen driving\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":17964689,"author":"tobmlt","month":"2018-09","story":"How America lost its love for the stick shift","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates driving to dissolve traffic standing waves","quote":"Somewhere on the web there is a great video of a guy doing this pretty effectively.","text":"You beautiful zen traffic wave surfer you. This, a thousand times this. Somewhere on the web there is a great video of a guy doing this pretty effectively. I guess it's maybe five years old now but worth a watch. I think this wsj vid may contain excerpts from the original: [LINK] If everyone could just relax out there, some of those traffic standing waves would diffuse away to nothing. Ahhh... When I get in bad traffic and struggle against the urge to tailgate I play a game where I try and give enough space to where I just roll up to the car in front of me as it is starting to move again. This gives me a time-local goal to aim at instead of fixating on supposed \"bad actors\" I might want to judge instead. Edit: I found it! (from 2008 it appears) [VIDEO]"},{"id":11597355,"author":"xbryanx","month":"2016-04","story":"Can a single car break a traffic jam?","stance":"rec","why":"Enjoyable summary of traffic wave ideas","quote":"is an enjoyable summary of some of these ideas","text":"William J Beaty's video on Traffic Waves (linked in the article) is an enjoyable summary of some of these ideas: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9583782,"author":"thunderbong","month":"2015-05","story":"What Are Traffic Waves and Why Do They Happen So Much?","stance":"rec","why":"Helped explain how traffic waves form","quote":"It helped me understand traffic waves quite a bit","text":"I don't know if you've seen this video[0]. It helped me understand traffic waves quite a bit [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5926214,"author":"socillion","month":"2013-06","story":"The Physics Behind Traffic Jams","stance":"reference","why":"Video from the original traffic waves site","quote":"A video from there, omitted from OP","text":"Original copy at [LINK] A video from there, omitted from OP: [VIDEO] OP is a cut-down version of this site, which has been up for a decade or two. I'm curious if it's a legitimate copy."}],"r":[["iYJKd0rkKss",1],["MBRqu0YOH14",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["QM1iUe6IofM",1],["Wm2h0cbvsw8",1]]},"-iCIZQX9i1A":{"wy":"HN points people to it as an entertaining, thorough takedown of Hancock's pseudoarchaeology whenever his claims come up.","q":{"x":"lengthy, point-by-point takedown of the Ancient Apocalypse thing both entertaining and informative","i":39071480,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42588974,"author":"hoerensagen","month":"2025-01","story":"Laser mapping reveals oldest Amazonian cities, built 2500 years ago (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Good resource showing Hancock lacks evidence for his theory.","quote":"I think this video is good Ressource on the topic.","text":"I think this video is good Ressource on the topic. [VIDEO] Basically Hancock show lots of interesting and fascinating archaeological sites which is great especially since a lot of these are things that most viewers didn't know about before. But sadly he chooses to use these site only to present his theory for which he's doesn't have any actual evidence."},{"id":40380606,"author":"roywiggins","month":"2024-05","story":"Egypt's pyramids may have been built on a long-lost branch of the Nile","stance":"rec","why":"Movie-length debunking showing the evidence is not there.","quote":"I enjoyed this movie-length debunking of Ancient Apocalypse","text":"If you like video essays, I enjoyed this movie-length debunking of Ancient Apocalypse. tl;dr: The evidence is simply not there, and real archeology is much, much more interesting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39071480,"author":"roywiggins","month":"2024-01","story":"Challenges to the credibility of two recent Netflix documentaries","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining, informative point-by-point takedown of Ancient Apocalypse.","quote":"lengthy, point-by-point takedown of the Ancient Apocalypse thing both entertaining and informative","text":"I found this lengthy, point-by-point takedown of the Ancient Apocalypse thing both entertaining and informative. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38304457,"author":"eesmith","month":"2023-11","story":"Nature may have helped sculpt the Great Sphinx of Giza","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed multi-part critique of Ancient Apocalypse.","quote":"detailed critique (in 4 parts)","text":"See Miniminuteman's \"I watched ancient apocalypse so you don't have to\" detailed critique (in 4 parts) starting at [VIDEO] ."},{"id":35165894,"author":"4ggr0","month":"2023-03","story":"Lidar Reveals 650-Square-Mile Maya Site Hidden Beneath Guatemalan Rain Forest","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous debunking of Graham Hancock's Netflix series with guest interviews.","quote":"currently debunking Graham Hancocks Netflix Series with some humour and guest interviews sprinkled through it","text":"Miniminuteman is a YouTuber who's currently debunking Graham Hancocks Netflix Series with some humour and guest interviews sprinkled through it. It's the first time I heard about Graham Hancock but it was enough for me to not be able to really take him seriously. His whole shtick seems to be to smear others and portray himself as a victim of \"mainstream archeology\" and just saying things without evidence which could be true if lots of established facts were to simply be ignored. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42592435,"author":"AlotOfReading","month":"2025-01","story":"Laser mapping reveals oldest Amazonian cities, built 2500 years ago (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Popular-media debunking of Hancock.","quote":"You can find debunkings in popular media","text":"The moai are half buried because that's just what tends to happen in erosive environments. Sediment buries things. As for specifically debunking his LGM speculation, there isn't much because no one with public credentials wants to spend time and effort on it in the current academic environment. You can find debunkings in popular media ( [VIDEO] ) and a few scattered \"popular\" academic publications like the SAA's special edition on Hancock's pseudoarchaeology ( [LINK] ), but most of the information is buried in papers and books that don't discuss him at all. There's just flat out no actual evidence for (virtually?) all of his speculation and you can't prove a negative. Hancock's MO is to take 1-2 anomalous results and spin out media around \"these dates don't line up, so let's speculate about something entirely unrelated\". The rapa nui episodes in Ancient Apocalypse S2 talk to one actual archaeologist, who doesn't even support the dates discussed in the episodes. They were just anomalous banana phytoliths that were present from sediment intrusions. Dating is hard and this stuff happens regularly, especially in areas without much active research like Rapa Nui. The standard understanding is post-1200, as documented in papers like [LINK] and [LINK]"},{"id":38241552,"author":"AStrangeMorrow","month":"2023-11","story":"Oldest Pyramid Found Not in Egypt or Americas, but in Indonesia","stance":"reference","why":"Section debunking the claimed 10000 year old Indonesian pyramid.","quote":"his 4h or so debunking of Graham Hancock ancient civilization Netflix series","text":"Miniminuteman goes over \"10000 year old Indonesian pyramid\" too in his 4h or so debunking of Graham Hancock ancient civilization Netflix series (the usual \"big archeology doesn't want you to know about this prediluvian ancient Atlantean civilization\"). Link to the relevant section of the video of this debunking series: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40381397,"author":"tiffanyh","month":"2024-05","story":"Egypt's pyramids may have been built on a long-lost branch of the Nile","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"hard to take this debunking serious when the very 1st point he makes about Graham is incorrect","text":"I just started watched the YouTube. It’s hard to take this debunking serious when the very 1st point he makes about Graham is incorrect. He states that Graham claims to be a researcher. [VIDEO] In the Netflix series, Graham doesn’t not. He makes it clear he’s not a researcher nor archeologist. He clearly states he’s an investigative journalist. [LINK] —- And then the YouTube host roots his show on the scientific method with the question “How do you prove there was an ancient civilians”. This is the wrong root question. The root question should be “How did ancient civilians create these structures using the technology we believe they had at the time”. Or said differently, “how did civilians not use more advance technology than we believe they had” (Which is only bronze tools and no existence of the wheel)"}],"r":[["KDpNQQqdSh8",1],["FKXOucXB4a8",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["7ttG90raCNo",1],["b9_Vh9h3Ohw",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1]]},"vuIlsN32WaE":{"wy":"HN regularly shares it in weight loss threads as a clear, precise explanation of the physics of weight loss.","q":{"x":"The mathematics of weight loss is laid out clearly in 20 minutes here","i":28594602,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":40491391,"author":"palata","month":"2024-05","story":"Ask HN: Have you tried weight loss drugs?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting TEDx talk shared in weight loss discussions.","quote":"Whenever I have a discussion about weight loss, I like to share this TEDx video","text":"Whenever I have a discussion about weight loss, I like to share this TEDx video: [VIDEO] Just because I think it is quite interesting, not trying to make a point or anything :-)."},{"id":30384820,"author":"projektfu","month":"2022-02","story":"Scientist busts myths about how humans burn calories","stance":"rec","why":"Mr. Wizard-style TEDx explanation of fat and energy.","quote":"","text":"Fat stores are considered, metabolically, as stored energy as they expand and diminish based on the metabolizable energy of the diet, which may be different from the measured total energy. But you’re definitely right! There’s a great Mr. Wizard-style TEDx video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28594602,"author":"relueeuler","month":"2021-09","story":"Death of the Calorie (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Clear 20-minute account of the mathematics of weight loss.","quote":"The mathematics of weight loss is laid out clearly in 20 minutes here","text":"Your description above is not how the body works. To support a mass M must require a quantity of energy E obtained from food. The attempt to alter this model with “loose” ideas about “burn rates” therefore does not make sense. The mathematics of weight loss is laid out clearly in 20 minutes here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24667055,"author":"hkolk","month":"2020-10","story":"When we lose weight, where does it go?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains weight loss mathematically, countering diet myths.","quote":"There is a nice Ted talk about the same topic.","text":"There is a nice Ted talk about the same topic. Something I often link to people saying \"I can eat everything I want in this diet...\" The mathematics of weight loss | Ruben Meerman | TEDxQUT: [VIDEO] My personal weight-loss (and now sports) is largely based on understanding how the human body works with metabolism.. carbs, fat, protein.. nice and geeky"},{"id":24667264,"author":"greenbush","month":"2020-10","story":"When we lose weight, where does it go?","stance":"rec","why":"TEDx demonstration with lab experiments on weight loss.","quote":"here's a great TEDx demonstration with lab experiments","text":"For the science geeks, here's a great TEDx demonstration with lab experiments: \"The mathematics of weight loss \" [VIDEO]"},{"id":15790469,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2017-11","story":"When somebody loses weight, where does the fat go?","stance":"rec","why":"Precise explanation of where fat goes when losing weight.","quote":"Someone on youtube made a perfect video explaining this with enough precision","text":"Someone on youtube made a perfect video explaining this with enough precision: not sure it's this one [VIDEO] but close"},{"id":30625184,"author":"dools","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Any weird tips for weight loss?","stance":"reference","why":"Supports the claim that weight loss is energy in versus out.","quote":"The only reason ANYONE ever loses weight is because they consume less energy than they burn","text":"Here's how I lost weight: 1) Download the Lose It app 2) Buy an apple watch (or whatever matches with your phone) 3) Connect the watch up to the app 4) Fiddle about with the settings 5) Stay below the line Excercise is optional . I did this for my dad and he can barely move, and he lost like 12kg. The only reason ANYONE ever loses weight is because they consume less energy than they burn, there is no other way for energy you consume to leave your system: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30625752,"author":"projektfu","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Any weird tips for weight loss?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how weight loss occurs through breathing out CO2.","quote":"This video explains how weight loss occurs","text":"If you think about it, you breathe in 21% oxygen and breathe out 5% CO2. If you switch to 100% oxygen, you still breathe out 5% CO2. This is because the carbonic acid is tightly regulated in the body at a level that is in equilibrium with 5% CO2. When we produce excess, through exercise, we ventilate more to maintain the balance. But the partial pressure of CO2 remains the same. There is some oxygen removed but only as much as the hemoglobin can carry. Prolonged 100% oxygen has no effect on this and is also toxic over time. People on oxygen therapy are really breathing 40%, and 100% is only used for short stints during anesthetic procedures. This video explains how weight loss occurs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28470143,"author":"loonster","month":"2021-09","story":"Massive animal species discovered in half-billion-year-old Burgess Shale","stance":"reference","why":"Partially explains the body-as-tube concept.","quote":"This is a video that partially explains the concept.","text":"I agree that cooking is a form of digestion, but that is not what I was thinking when I made my comment. We are walking tubes. The cavity inside of the tube is still external to the tube itself. This is a video that partially explains the concept. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17391688,"author":"pottspotts","month":"2018-06","story":"Losing 100 pounds in 276 days","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the physics behind weight loss.","quote":"","text":"Really great data! Thank you for sharing. The hard part for me is mindset and willpower. Unfortunately, knowing the physics* behind weight loss doesn't make it any easier, and perhaps might make it more stressful. Why can't I do this simple thing that logically is as complex as 2+2. Our minds have their own prerogatives. I wonder then what did you tell yourself? How many times did you have a bad day? What was your mood like throughout? I'd love to know so much more about the psychology. Great article though and thanks again for sharing! * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lpu-3UF_b48",1],["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["2-oBNh0UkQc",1],["sCZJblyT_XM",1],["Mfsnlbd-4xQ",1],["NVEgyJCTee4",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["1AjhFZVfB9c",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1]]},"zVhQOhxb1Mc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining, clear explanation of nuclear weapons basics from a policy expert.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend the \"Nuclear 101: How Nuclear Bombs Work\" lectures by Matthew Bunn","i":41464016,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41464016,"author":"virgulino","month":"2024-09","story":"Did Sandia use a thermonuclear secondary in a product logo?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining lectures on nuclear weapons basics by Matthew Bunn","quote":"I highly recommend the \"Nuclear 101: How Nuclear Bombs Work\" lectures by Matthew Bunn","text":"For anyone interested in the basics of nuclear weapons, I highly recommend the \"Nuclear 101: How Nuclear Bombs Work\" lectures by Matthew Bunn, a man heavily involved in nuclear arms control. His lectures are always highly entertaining, a real pleasure to watch. This is a clip from his lecture explaining the basics of thermonuclear warheads: [LINK] And the full “Nuclear 101” lecture, in two parts: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":16638809,"author":"shaqbert","month":"2018-03","story":"No Longer a Secret: How Israel Destroyed Syria's Nuclear Reactor","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains nuclear bomb making and which parts are hard","quote":"here is a great video from the Belfer center on nuclear bomb making and what are the hard parts [0].","text":"In context, here is a great video from the Belfer center on nuclear bomb making and what are the hard parts [0]. Getting the fission material is the hard part, and in order to get plutonium nukes you need a enrichment plant, i.e. a reactor that is not meant to produce energy but breed plutonium. [0] [VIDEO] (at min 47, he starts talking about the hard parts)"},{"id":42188478,"author":"nordsieck","month":"2024-11","story":"El Capitan: New supercomputer is the fastest","stance":"rec","why":"Surprisingly detailed public information on weapon design","quote":"the information that is online is surprisingly detailed.","text":"> It's because of the way the weapons are designed, which requires a CNWDI clearance to know, so your curiosity is not likely to be sated. While that's true, the information that is online is surprisingly detailed. For example, this series \"Nuclear 101: How Nuclear Bombs Work\" [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":34567568,"author":"verdverm","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What happens if a nuclear missile is air-destroyed before detonation?","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of how nuclear weapons work","quote":"has a good explanation of how nuclear weapons work.","text":"[VIDEO] has a good explanation of how nuclear weapons work. It may be possible that a missel is disabled and when it hits the ground, produces a much lower yield explosion than designed for, in particular a plutonium device, if I recall the video and extrapolate with sufficient accuracy"},{"id":19459584,"author":"espeed","month":"2019-03","story":"Revolutionary Ideas in Science, Math, and Society [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Harvard Belfer lecture on bombs","quote":"","text":"On US soil you'd have fallout from the soil getting sucked up into the atmosphere. See... \"Why World War II Matters - Victor Davis Hanson\" [video] [LINK] Harvard Belfer Center: \"Nuclear 101: How Nuclear Bombs Work Part 1/2\" [video] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eGffUODWWSE",1],["hJP5GqnTrNo",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["WNWYk4DdT_E",1],["g0lrIi0ce5E",1],["ouAG4vXFORc",1],["fbhPIK-oBvA",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["2V1FtfBDsLU",1],["KAWA1DuvCnQ",1]]},"MBT1OK6VAIU":{"wy":"HN cites it for the story of how Pitfall's levels are generated procedurally from a seeded counter, within 4K of ROM.","q":{"x":"This is a great talk for those who love such things","i":45613098,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45613098,"author":"tdeck","month":"2025-10","story":"A 4k-Room Text Adventure Written by One Human in QBasic No AI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How Pitfall fit into 4k","quote":"This is a great talk for those who love such things","text":"Also reminds me of how they fit Pitfall into 4k. A lot simpler game but the platform was also much more limited. This is a great talk for those who love such things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34381990,"author":"mgkimsal","month":"2023-01","story":"Atari 2600 Hardware Design: Making Something Out of (Almost) Nothing","stance":"rec","why":"David Crane explains Pitfall in person with Q&A","quote":"this conference session with David Crane on Pitfall/Atari is pretty good.","text":"Can't remember if I read it here recently, or on reddit, but this conference session with David Crane on Pitfall/Atari is pretty good. He doesn't as technical as some folks may want, but there's some coolness in just hearing him explain this in person. Couple of interesting Q&A bits towards the end too. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34096984,"author":"russellbeattie","month":"2022-12","story":"“Pitfall” for the Atari describes its whole world in a single byte","stance":"rec","why":"Designer explains fitting many levels into tiny space","quote":"I remember him going over how he was able to create so many levels in such a tiny space.","text":"I think much of this is explained at a high level in this talk from the original game designer: Pitfall Classic Postmortem With David Crane Panel at GDC 2011 (Atari 2600). [VIDEO] I haven't seen it in a while (will rewatch now), but I remember him going over how he was able to create so many levels in such a tiny space. I love these sorts of examples of breakthrough tech products that were the result of a developer figuring out how to do something that seemed impossible. Much of Apple's early success, for example, could be attributed to this."},{"id":6996172,"author":"phoboslab","month":"2014-01","story":"Racing the Beam (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Pitfall postmortem by its designer","quote":"Truly fascinating stuff.","text":"I haven't read the book yet, but David Crane's Pitfall Post Mortem[1] also covers a lot of what you mentioned. Truly fascinating stuff. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24760953,"author":"EvanAnderson","month":"2020-10","story":"Model fits in a tweet and predicts all elections since 1960","stance":"reference","why":"Pitfall's polynomial counter generates the world","quote":"","text":"I am reminded of the Atari 2600 game Pitfall and its use of a polynomial counter to procedurally generate the game world[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7769014,"author":"wazoox","month":"2014-05","story":"Pokémon Crystal disassembled source code","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, Pitfall! II on the 2600 is 4 KB, and run in 128 bytes of RAM, and has 256 different levels. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":5937709,"author":"spc476","month":"2013-06","story":"Dirty Game Development Tricks","stance":"reference","why":"Pitfall world generated from a seeded random number generator","quote":"David Crane basically used a random number generator with a predefined seed to generate the world","text":"And in Pitfall, David Crane basically used a random number generator with a predefined seed to generate the world ( [VIDEO] starting at the 22:12 mark)."}],"r":[["uOJCS1W1uzg",1],["gUnxzVOs3rk",1],["uxykI30fS54",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["sg2j7mZ9-2Y",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1]]},"kfg_e6YG37U":{"wy":"Often the first thing suggested to HN programmers asking about posture and back pain.","q":{"x":"my first recommended steps for you would be: 1. Watch the \"Deskbound\" Google Talk by Kelly Starett","i":12653034,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12653034,"author":"threepipeproblm","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: Posture – what do you do during work to avoid back pain?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First step recommended for fixing posture","quote":"my first recommended steps for you would be: 1. Watch the \"Deskbound\" Google Talk by Kelly Starett","text":"As someone who has made huge progress on posture over a long period of time, my first recommended steps for you would be: 1. Watch the \"Deskbound\" Google Talk by Kelly Starett [VIDEO] 2. Read and do the exercises in Awareness Through Movement by Moshe Feldenkrais. 3. Get into the habit of wiggling and shifting your body throughout the day. And get into the habit of devoting regular time to being more conscious of where your muscles are and how you are using them. 4. Trigger Point Therapy is amazing for self-treating body pain, if you are willing to experience short bursts of pain during the treatment."},{"id":23667484,"author":"cesarosum","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fixed their own bruxism?","stance":"rec","why":"General posture advice from Starrett","quote":"He has good general advice in this video as well -","text":"I suffered from mild bruxism about 10 years ago and have had several bouts of TMJD where I couldn’t close one side of my jaw. For me, the key has been poor posture induced by stress; these bouts coincide with heavy laptop and mobile phone use (neck angle forward and down). What fixed it for me was switching to a good desktop set-up whenever possible and self-treatment via Kelly Starrett’s Becoming A Supple Leopard (there is whole section in the second edition on neck and jaw). He has good general advice in this video as well - [VIDEO] I haven’t used the mouthguard now in probably 5 or more years."},{"id":5720612,"author":"paydro","month":"2013-05","story":"Apparently standing desks can hurt too","stance":"rec","why":"Starrett answers a standing desk question near the end","quote":"Love Starrett! He also did a talk at Google","text":"Love Starrett! He also did a talk at Google ( [VIDEO] ) and near the end someone asks about stand up desks. The problem the author has might not be due to the standing desk but more with his posture. The standing desk probably exacerbated the problem."},{"id":4993732,"author":"chhunsguns","month":"2013-01","story":"Fixing the computer guy posture [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"How to sit, with mobility and stretching guidance","quote":"Here's another useful resource regarding posture and sitting","text":"Here's another useful resource regarding posture and sitting [1] by Kelly Starrett, who's probably well-noted in strength training circles for his MobilityWOD project. He explains how to sit and provides guidance on several related topics such as shoulder/hip mobility and hydration. He also illustrates some massages and stretches you can do to take care of your junk through hours of prolonged sit-and-keyboarding. For what its worth, I've been following along with his MobilityWOD project [2] for some time and it has immensely helped with my strength training pursuits. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29198058,"author":"6r0k","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: Fitness while doing knowledge work?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've been a software engineer for over 15 years and so have been working at a desk for just as long. For the first few years I never thought twice about it, just sat all day long, no big deal. Eventually that started to catch up with me and my health began to noticeably decline. I eventually made a conscious effort to combat the sedentary nature of knowledge work and have since seen significant improvements in my health, both physically and mentally. I now have this dichotomous relationship with computers: on one hand I love programming but on the other I can't stand being sedentary and I don't like that the default mode for computer work is sitting at a desk completely stationary. So I'm always looking for the most effective way to maintain my well-being while also working at a desk all day and I've been writing about it on my blog. It's rough at the moment but am actively working to improve the content and quality; but I think this article in particular may be of interest: [LINK] . I think there are the obvious answers like: go for walks, go to the gym, do active things, etc. I'm 100% for all of those, but for the most part you can't really do any of them while working. But movement is the key and so I think the most important thing you can do at your workspace is to create a movement-rich environment where you are constantly able to shift positions and fidget. Ideally get a standing desk; this sets the foundation... you'll have a difficult time being sedentary while you're"},{"id":28187979,"author":"6r0k","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: How to deal with coccyx pain while sitting?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I realize you're interested in hearing from others dealing with the same problem; I, myself, am not, however, I am extremely interested in our general health as deskbound hackers and would like to offer some thoughts that may prove helpful: 1. Reduce optional sitting. Sitting for extended periods of time compromises our musculoskeletal system and in particular our spinal health. When we sit, the muscles in our lower body basically turn off leaving only our core to support our spine which is not sustainable. We eventually collapse into poor posture with our shoulders rounding forward and hips rounding backward putting the body into a c-shape. This position adds a ton of extra pressure on our vertebrae which can lead to various amounts of pain. Plus, the more we sit the less we move; and we need to move to keep muscles engaged and blood circulating. 2. When you do sit, choose the right chair, and sit properly. The ideal chair is one with a wide square seat made of wood or metal. It may sound uncomfortable, but that's the point; the goal is to encourage movement, a chair shouldn't encourage you to sit all day but to simply give you a place to temporarily rest. The square shape allows you to sit along the edge without sliding off which makes it easier to maintain a neutral upright position. And the hard surface allows for great mechanical feedback; you get uncomfortable which reminds you to move. Before you even sit, engage your core to stabilize your spine, then sit without coll"}],"r":[["Sw_Ze9zIafM",1]]},"0UjpmT5noto":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a clear account of how minor infractions punish the poor.","q":{"x":"Even more relevant is his piece on municipal violations and how they can unfairly steamroll the poor with escalating fees.","i":10977593,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":23670175,"author":"SuoDuanDao","month":"2020-06","story":"My family saw a police car hit a kid, then I learned how NYPD impunity works","stance":"rec","why":"Rant on minor infractions punishing the poor.","quote":"has a great rant about a similar situation","text":"Well, I can give you a simple example. Where I grew up, you'd hear a gunshot every so often. No one paid it any mind - we knew it was the neighbors hunting out of season. Technically a crime. But we all knew they and their children needed the meat. If everyone did it, the deer population would collapse and no one could hunt effectively even in-season. If someone had called a wildlife officer they could have certainly made the trek down. But no one would think to call the wildlife officers on them, because we knew it would lead to their children going hungry. I think the thing people from rich neighbourhoods miss about that sort of thing is that when they hear stories of that nature [1], people's children going hungry over a minor infraction is seen as a failure of empathy on the constabulary's part. It's not. It's a failure of the law, and the constabulary's job is to uphold the law, not play social worker to people who break it. Hence our community's collective judgement that it was in everyone's best interest if the wildlife officer never found out about our neighbor's poaching activities. [1] Joe Oliver has a great rant about a similar situation, lest readers think this only works in rural settings: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13761011,"author":"Clubber","month":"2017-03","story":"A majority of millennials now reject capitalism, poll shows","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of how municipalities punish people who can't pay tickets.","quote":"John Oliver did a good breakdown of how municipalities do pretty nasty things when you can't pay your tickets.","text":">How much can the government be stealing from you if you have no money? John Oliver did a good breakdown of how municipalities do pretty nasty things when you can't pay your tickets. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10977593,"author":"kevin_thibedeau","month":"2016-01","story":"“No Cost” License Plate Readers Are Turning Texas Police into Debt Collectors","stance":"rec","why":"How municipal violations steamroll the poor with escalating fees.","quote":"Even more relevant is his piece on municipal violations and how they can unfairly steamroll the poor with escalating fees.","text":"Even more relevant is his piece on municipal violations and how they can unfairly steamroll the poor with escalating fees. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10439543,"author":"bsimpson","month":"2015-10","story":"A debtors’ prison in Mississippi","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver's take on the same story.","quote":"Here's the John Oliver version of the same story","text":"Here's the John Oliver version of the same story: [VIDEO] (Also, incognito mode is your friend when cookies conspire against you.)"},{"id":10440564,"author":"utefan001","month":"2015-10","story":"A debtors’ prison in Mississippi","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver's take on the same story.","quote":"Here's the John Oliver version of the same story","text":"This is so sad. This has got to stop. (repost from below) bsimpson 2 hours ago Here's the John Oliver version of the same story: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16209281,"author":"colanderman","month":"2018-01","story":"“Get Out of Jail Free” Cards in New York","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"What disingenuous hyperbole. The infractions for which you can use these cards result in fines, not jail time; failure to pay these fines typically results in wage garnishment, not jail time: [LINK] (Jailing people for inability to pay fines/be garnished is a moral issue, about which there's been lots of rumbling lately: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":15790356,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2017-11","story":"Stanford CS007: Personal Finance For Engineers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your comment can be summed up as \"Just have money.\" If you can't afford health insurance, you can't make sure you're covered. The only option is to not have it and to go to the ER whenever there are problems. Cities find ways of ticketing you. They make $20M/yr from parking violations. If that revenue drops, they'll change the rules until it goes up again. [VIDEO] For example, there are tow traps that don't look anything like tow zones. I've seen at least five cars fall into it, myself included. The city won't change it, and you can't fight it. Another example: Street cleaning. It's a euphemism for \"Move your car or we'll ticket you.\" No one shows up to clean the streets, ever, but they're there at 8AM to write you a ticket. When you're forced to park on the street, you can't get away from this. And the notifications for street cleaning often appear between midnight at 8AM, meaning you can end up late to work or with a $150 ticket if you don't pay rapt attention every day."},{"id":10429971,"author":"themartorana","month":"2015-10","story":"Police Leaders Join Call to Cut Prison Rosters","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"At this point, anyone \"joining the call\" are simply attempting to hop onto the right side of history. This problem is so egregious that taking any other position is simply absurd. Or just f-ing evil. [0] [0] [VIDEO] Edit: what, did I piss off some people that like imprisoning an unbelievably disproportionate percentage of citizens, especially minorities, for crimes as mundane as an unpaid parking ticket? Well, you do you, I guess..."},{"id":10440183,"author":"justifier","month":"2015-10","story":"People Want Fairness, Not Economic Equality","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"fairness is a horrendous choice of words, but the one this article chose to use there are many unfair side effects of current economics look at taxes.. recently i read a major corporate entity paid 4k pounds in taxes in 2014(o). The top comment says: TIL our small UK startup pays 10-20x more corp tax .. Doing things honestly and by the book is expensive in both money terms and time terms (paying accountants) Big corps .. make me feel like a fool. though these corps are seen as equals in buraecratic lines, the expectations and avenues of utilising those regulations seems unfairly balanced or look at fines.. if i get a parking ticket(i) for 100$, i may be unable to pay it right away with my zero savings and my 12k/yr income, someone making 80k+ a year will just pay the 100$ grievance and be done with it i will have to start budgeting myself to make enough to pay the 100$ fine, but it will take time with bimonthly paychecks and in that time i will be punished with late fees only increasing the financial burden and time needed to accrue the funds even if trying to do the right thing people punished further simply for economic circumstance so though the two violators are seen as equal, the expectations and avenues of how to rectify the violations seems unfairly balanced people making less end up paying more for the same violation due to the bureaucracy maintaining the violations or i could use a payday loan(ii), another damning option these are only two examples of areas where a c"},{"id":9296654,"author":"cgearhart","month":"2015-03","story":"Economic Inequality: It’s Far Worse Than You Think","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Even municipal fines -- as emphasized by John Oliver recently -- contribute to the problem.","text":"That's the problem though -- there is not equality is possibilities. Analysis [1] of a study from the Pew Research Center on economic mobility [2] found that you are 10x more likely to be in the top 20% if you're born there than if you're born in the bottom 20%. Someone born in the top 20% who doesn't go to college is still 2.5x more likely to have an income in the top 20% as an adult than someone from the bottom 20% who does go to college. Moreover, it's worsened because life is more expensive in subtle ways as you move down the income bracket. [3] Even municipal fines -- as emphasized by John Oliver recently -- contribute to the problem. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2PCb9mnrU1g",1],["ue-1JoJQaEg",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["b5P3QDm61go",1],["MkJkyMuBm3g",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["1fZTOjd_bOQ",1],["ez773teNFYA",1]]},"449j7oKQVkc":{"wy":"Recommended as the case for blocking code and virtual threads over monads and async/await.","q":{"x":"one of my recent favorite videos generally on this topic has been from Curry On conference","i":10541575,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":42134831,"author":"fzeindl","month":"2024-11","story":"Async await: the worst thing to happen to programming?","stance":"rec","why":"Arguments against async/await from virtual threads advocate","quote":"Here are more arguments against async/await","text":"Here are more arguments against async/await: [VIDEO] Ron Pressler always was an advocate for blocking code and even joined oracle to add virtual threads to the JVM, thus invalidating the performance argument of the async/await/non-blocking crowd. I really wish young developers would be taught about the actor model and communicating sequential processes before falling for the false promises of async/await-land. And I wish JavaScript runtimes had a way of expressing continuations /blocking threads on their eventloop."},{"id":40982625,"author":"fzeindl","month":"2024-07","story":"Java Virtual Threads: A Case Study","stance":"rec","why":"Case for simple blocking code over non-blocking IO styles","quote":"Writing single-threaded blocking code is far easier for most people","text":"Does it shake out to any real advantage? To put it shortly: Writing single-threaded blocking code is far easier for most people and has many other benefits, like more understandable and readable programs: [VIDEO] The main reason why non-blocking IO with it's style of intertwining concurrency and algorithms came along is that starting a thread for every request was too expensive. With virtual threads that problem is eliminated so we can go back to writing blocking code."},{"id":20309894,"author":"apta","month":"2019-06","story":"Go proposal: Leave “if err != nil” alone?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on not over-relying on mathematical purity for correctness","quote":"I actually like what talks like these have to say","text":"> Or Java? Yes - that was implied in the same sentence where I wrote \"(e.g. exceptions or actual compiler enforced error handling like Rust).\" :-) > As far as I know, race conditions happen and leak out into production with Scala and Java. They do. But there are more techniques in those languages (such as immutable collections, as their type systems are actually able to model and implement them) to mitigate the issue. > and really the lesson of programming in the large for the past 30 years, is that it's not enough to have mathematical or methodological power in guaranteeing correctness in a program. I agree there. I actually don't abide by the ideas that using a language like Haskell or Idris or what have you will automagically grant you superior or error-free software. I actually like what talks like these have to say: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] However, that does not mean we disregard established practices, only to try to reinvent them in a bad way as we see golang trying to do (no generics - even though many other languages implemented them \"correctly\", bad error handling, no sum types, null pointers, and so on). They purposely disregarded these works without good arguments at all, and now they're struggling to find workarounds, or continue to dismiss them as non-issues, even though they clearly are. The way I see it, the moment Java gets fibers and value types implemented, and GraalVM's native compilation is mature enough for use, golang's appeal as a devops langauge where it en"},{"id":10541575,"author":"elcritch","month":"2015-11","story":"TensorFlow: open-source library for machine intelligence","stance":"rec","why":"Practical differences between monads and imperative control flow","quote":"one of my recent favorite videos generally on this topic has been from Curry On conference","text":"That's a fair point overall. It's basically the entirety of the debate between functional languages and imperative languages. Nonetheless, it is still pretty good question as far as someone trying to understand the differences and why it might be useful. @hmehta, one of my recent favorite videos generally on this topic has been from Curry On conference [1]. It's not strictly data-flow vs control flow, but it does discuss some of the more practical differences in between using monads for structuring data flows vs imperative control. My take is that monads can be roughly considered as wrapping up imperative control flow along with the data. E.g. wrapping up the if/else choices into a data structure. Currently I actually found studying up on actual category theory easier than all the \"easy introduction\" things regarding monads. As in, the ideas when presented straightforwardly and technically correct are simpler than we give credence to. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"4hZxo96x48A":{"wy":"Cited as a funny, informative teardown of bad interface design, and for the story that MuseScore hired him as head of design afterward.","q":{"x":"I find it very entertaining yet still informative","i":23525953,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":33993124,"author":"radicality","month":"2022-12","story":"MuseScore 4","stance":"rec","why":"Funny review of MuseScore 3 UI","quote":"Obligatory funny video review of MuseScore 3 UI","text":"Obligatory funny video review of MuseScore 3 UI: [VIDEO] Same author also has a funny video on the UI of another music notation program, Sibelius: [LINK]"},{"id":27006545,"author":"meowface","month":"2021-05","story":"Audacity Aquired by Muse Group","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining Tantacrul videos on MuseScore","quote":"here are Tantacrul's entertaining videos on MuseScore","text":"If anyone's curious, here are Tantacrul's entertaining videos on MuseScore and designing MuseScore's new font, and the Audacity video from yesterday: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23525953,"author":"hexmiles","month":"2020-06","story":"MuseScore 4: Moving from notation software to composition software","stance":"rec","why":"UI/UX critique of MuseScore, entertaining and informative","quote":"I find it very entertaining yet still informative","text":"they actually hired the person behind this video (tantacrul): [VIDEO] It criticize the user interface and experience of various music application. I find it very entertaining yet still informative"},{"id":21046655,"author":"xmprt","month":"2019-09","story":"The Distribution of Users’ Computer Skills: Worse Than You Think (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"UI critique of MuseScore versus a competitor","quote":"There's an interesting pair of videos (if you have some time) that goes over the UI of two competing music scoring software","text":"The solution isn't to put things in preference windows either because even smart users will find that cumbersome. There's an interesting pair of videos (if you have some time) that goes over the UI of two competing music scoring software and why one is bad and the other good. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40218762,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-05","story":"Printing Music with CSS Grid","stance":"reference","why":"Tantacrul's earlier critique of MuseScore","quote":"","text":"That's very much it. You could also make an analogy to the arts of typography and typesetting. It's of the same importance to a composer or musician that the notation is legible and beautiful, as it is to a graphic designer or reader that type and layout are legible and beautiful. If the GP is interested in the topic, there are great videos by Tantacrul on his experience redesigning the notation font for MuseScore [0] and his overview of MuseScore 4.0's engraving improvements [1] - in particular this second video, concerning mainly layout, shows just how far this CSS approach would have to go to have a truly legible, reflowable layout in the face of beams/slurs/ties. (I don't want to take away from anyone on the Sibelius team, they're likely even better experts on the subject than Tantacrul is, but as far as I know, they didn't make long-form Youtube videos about the topic! He also likely got the job of leading the MuseScore redesign thanks to his previous videos that critiqued and reimagined the interfaces of music composition software... including older versions of Sibelius [2] and MuseScore [3]) [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30629234,"author":"tasty_freeze","month":"2022-03","story":"“Compiling” Music","stance":"reference","why":"Funny UI critique that changed MuseScore's direction","quote":"The guy who created it is a trifecta: a composer, a typographer, and UI expert.","text":"Musescore is usable but isn't great. I haven't used lilypond, but I'm sure lilypond is miles better. The focus of musescore has changed over the past year or two, all triggered by a funny youtube sh*tpost criticizing its UI (it was one series of such videos taking shots at all the big notation programs). [VIDEO] The guy who created it is a trifecta: a composer, a typographer, and UI expert. The team at musescore took the criticism in good humor, and hired the creator of the video (Tantacrul, aka Martin Keary) to help them change it. The first phase was getting rid of some low-hanging fruit in the UI department. That work is ongoing. The main focus of the next phase (musescore 4.0) is the quality of the layout. Some of those improvements already landed in musescore 3.6."},{"id":26398209,"author":"NickHoff","month":"2021-03","story":"Music Software and Bad Interface Design: Avid’s Sibelius (2018) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Acerbic MuseScore critique that led to real changes","quote":"They actually took his criticisms and started making changes.","text":"This guy also made a similarly acerbic video about MuseScore, a good open source scorewriter [1]. They actually took his criticisms and started making changes. The two ended up working together and I think he is now the design lead for MuseScore. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24996959,"author":"ohazi","month":"2020-11","story":"Update on MuseScore 3.6 and 4.0","stance":"reference","why":"Scathing UI/UX review of MuseScore","quote":"some hilariously scathing UI/UX video reviews for some popular composing software tools","text":"For those out of the loop: Tantacrul is a composer who put together some hilariously scathing UI/UX video reviews for some popular composing software tools out there, including MuseScore [1] [2] [3]. The team developing MuseScore then brought him on as head of design. [1] Sibelius: [LINK] [2] MuseScore: [VIDEO] [3] Dorico: [LINK]"},{"id":23795899,"author":"karlding","month":"2020-07","story":"“LibreOffice is at serious risk”","stance":"reference","why":"Critique of MuseScore showing UX problems obvious in retrospect","quote":"in Tantacrul's critique of MuseScore [0], there's issues highlighted that seem obvious in retrospect","text":"I agree. GUIs are hard! On the other hand, it seems like Open Source has a lack of designers that are willing to contribute. For example, in Tantacrul's critique of MuseScore [0], there's issues highlighted that seem obvious in retrospect, but in Open Source projects, unless there's someone who drives the user experience, it's easy to miss all these small things that add up to signal (whether rightly or wrongly is besides the point) a lack of polish. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23525955,"author":"dry_soup","month":"2020-06","story":"MuseScore 4: Moving from notation software to composition software","stance":"reference","why":"Roast of MuseScore UI that led to the author being hired","quote":"meticulously roasted MuseScore's UI on YouTube, and the MuseScore team ended up hiring him","text":"Exciting news! This feels like a Blender 2.8-style project. What's funny about this is that a composer/music YouTuber/UX designer called Tantacrul meticulously roasted MuseScore's UI on YouTube, and the MuseScore team ended up hiring him, leading to this project. The video in question: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23348490,"author":"Nullabillity","month":"2020-05","story":"Linux Sucks 2020","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tantacrul did one on MuseScore[0] (a music composing program), and ended up getting hired as their head of design[1].","text":"Tantacrul did one on MuseScore[0] (a music composing program), and ended up getting hired as their head of design[1]. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: Mentioned in [LINK] , but don't have the exact timestamp"}],"r":[["dKx1wnXClcI",2],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["JLb5hZLw44s",1],["F6FR08VylO4",1],["1YbcB6b4A2U",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["UANN2Eu6ZnM",1],["RMWNvwLiXIQ",1],["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["qTBAW-Eh0tM",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["lvTqbM5Dq4Q",1],["ett_8wLJ87U",1]]},"8ORLN_KwAgs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a quick introduction to the quantum eraser and delayed choice weirdness, though one commenter notes later corrections to its framing.","q":{"x":"a quick video from space time for those being first introduced","i":21363600,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":22372991,"author":"mbell","month":"2020-02","story":"Double Slits with Single Atoms","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of the delayed choice quantum eraser experiment","quote":"See the Delayed Choice Quantum Eraser experiment for even more weirdness, a good video on it","text":"If you place a detector in front of (or behind!) the slits the wave function collapses and there is no interference pattern. This is all part of the mystery of it, as soon as you 'observe' a particle, in any way, it stops acting like a wave. See the Delayed Choice Quantum Eraser experiment for even more weirdness, a good video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21363600,"author":"mitchty","month":"2019-10","story":"Double Slit Experiment No Mystery","stance":"rec","why":"Quick introduction to the quantum eraser for newcomers","quote":"a quick video from space time for those being first introduced","text":"Indeed, a quick video from space time for those being first introduced. It’ll likely make you even more confused to the double slit experiment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19405179,"author":"comboy","month":"2019-03","story":"Experimental rejection of observer-independence in the quantum world","stance":"rec","why":"Mind-blowing video about quantum weirdness.","quote":"Also not directly related video if you like your mind being blown","text":"Here's a dumbed down version: [LINK] Also not directly related video if you like your mind being blown: [VIDEO] Especially given how our understanding of the world changed during the last 200 years, I would say we are still very much clueless. I find that exciting. Btw, no idea why did researchers get \"freedom of choice\" involved."},{"id":13982430,"author":"chakalakasp","month":"2017-03","story":"NIST Physicists Show Ion Pairs Perform Enhanced &#039;Spooky Action&#039;","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the quantum eraser experiment and its implications.","quote":"if you'd like to know more about this experiment and why it has ridiculous implications, check this PBS clip","text":"One of the more head exploding experiments I've seen regarding entanglement is this one: [LINK] By all apparences, it seems to violate causality. It's one of those things that make you suddenly stop and bemusedly wonder if maybe we've finally found a kludge in the Universe's code. edit BTW, if you'd like to know more about this experiment and why it has ridiculous implications, check this PBS clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39850275,"author":"elcritch","month":"2024-03","story":"Why quantum entanglement doesn't allow faster-than-light communication (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the best example of spooky action at a distance","quote":"The quantum erasure experiment is to my mind the best example of the spooky action at a distance.","text":"> One hypothesis is that there is no spooky action. One particle was 'always' going to resolve one way, likewise with the other. Like inspecting 'heads' on one side of a coin 'forces' 'tails' onto the other side. > I accept that this coin-hypothesis has been disproved by people who actually know what they're talking about. The quantum erasure experiment is to my mind the best example of the spooky action at a distance. The outcome of the particle pairs is measured after the entanglement occurs implying not just \"spooky action at a distance\" but also in time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21171092,"author":"Schoolmeister","month":"2019-10","story":"Learning Haskell is no harder than learning any other programming language","stance":"reference","why":"Quantum eraser cited as showing one thing after another is abstraction.","quote":"as demonstrated by e.g. the quantum eraser experiment [1][2].","text":"> Exactly. I don't know why Haskell fanboys insist on abstracting us so far from the machine. I believe it is because abstractions are the way we have always made progress. Is the C code that's so close to the machine not just an abstraction of the underlying assembly, which is an abstraction of the micro operations of your particular processor, which in turn is an abstraction of the gate operations? The abstractions allow us to offload a significant part of mental task. Imagine trying to write an HTML web page in C. Sure it's doable with a lot of effort, but is it as simple as writing it using abstractions such as the DOM? > We live in a mutable physical universe that corresponds to a procedural program. One thing happens after another according to the laws of physics (which are the procedural program for our universe). You just proved why abstractions are useful. \"One thing happens after another\" is simply our abstraction of what actually happens, as demonstrated by e.g. the quantum eraser experiment [1][2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21069426,"author":"comboy","month":"2019-09","story":"Exotic Physics Phenomenon Involving Time Reversal Observed for First Time","stance":"reference","why":"Shows we still have much to learn about time and causality.","quote":"we still seem to have a lot to learn about how time and causality works","text":"Not sure if it's the first time, we still seem to have a lot to learn about how time and causality works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43197087,"author":"calny","month":"2025-02","story":"Time Warp: Delayed-choice quantum erasure","stance":"critical","why":"Video's rewriting-the-past claim was later debunked and retracted by host","quote":"PBS Spacetime did an interesting video on DCQE, but it tripped me up trying to fully understand what was happening","text":"PBS Spacetime did an interesting video on DCQE, but it tripped me up trying to fully understand what was happening: [VIDEO] ... Later Sabine Hossenfelder did a video debunking the proposition that DCQE somehow showed that the past was being rewritten. [LINK] And Matt from PBS Spacetime acknowledged she was right in this respectful comment: > Sabine, this is amazing. You are, as usual, 100% right. The delayed choice quantum eraser is a prime example of over-mystification of quantum mechanics, even WITHIN the field of quantum mechanics! I (Matt) was guilty of embracing the quantum woo in that episode 5 years ago. Since then I've obsessed over this family of experiments and my thinking shifted quite a bit."}],"r":[["YJrOuBkYCMQ",1],["obS6TUVSZds",1],["AFVDZeg4RVY",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["XheAMrS8Q1c",1],["31D94QOo2gY",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["ZhuHCtR3xq8",1],["4zgYG-_ha28",1]]},"B2482h_TNwg":{"wy":"HN sends people here as the most straightforward and detailed starting point for understanding EUV lithography.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching this video about lithography and the machine that makes it all possible","i":45667857,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45667857,"author":"mrcsharp","month":"2025-10","story":"Die shots of as many CPUs and other interesting chips as possible","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains lithography and the machine that makes it possible.","quote":"I highly recommend watching this video about lithography and the machine that makes it all possible","text":"I'm always fascinated by how brilliant us humans can be. So much so that we can put billions of transistors in very small spaces and in complex structures while also mass producing it. I highly recommend watching this video about lithography and the machine that makes it all possible [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45352902,"author":"itopaloglu83","month":"2025-09","story":"Apple A19 SoC die shot","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed explanation of ASML EUV machines.","quote":"Here’s an incredible, very detailed video about it","text":"It’s a mind blowing achievement, nothing below sorcery if you think about it. ASML machines are hitting tin droplets with 25kW laser 50,000 times a second to turn them into plasma to create the necessary extreme ultraviolet light, and despite generating 500W of EUV, only a small fraction can reach the wafer, due to loses along the way. I believe it was like 10%. Here’s an incredible, very detailed video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47932480,"author":"jasode","month":"2026-04","story":"The World's Most Complex Machine","stance":"rec","why":"Straightforward overview of the whole EUV machine.","quote":"The more straightforward video of ASML EUV is from Branch Education","text":"The Veritasium video is good but their \"newscast\" style with constant back-and-forth cuts to talking heads can make the presentation a bit disjointed. The more straightforward video of ASML EUV is from Branch Education: [VIDEO] Because that vid gives an overview of the whole machine, it gives context to what each scientist is talking about in the Veritasium interviews."},{"id":46153388,"author":"danparsonson","month":"2025-12","story":"The RAM shortage comes for us all","stance":"reference","why":"Starting point for understanding chip manufacturing.","quote":"This will get you started","text":"Sure - once you have dozens of engineers and 5 years under your belt you'll be good to go! This will get you started: [VIDEO] Keep in mind that every wafer makes multiple trips around the fab, and on each trip it visits multiple machines. Broadly, one trip lays down one layer, and you may need 80-100 layers (although I guess DRAM will be fewer). Each layer must be aligned to nanometer precision with previous layers, otherwise the wafer is junk. Then as others have said, once you finish the wafer, you still need to slice it, test the dies, and then package them. Plus all the other stuff.... You'll need billions in investment, not millions - good luck!"}],"r":[["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["gDfNRWsMRsU",1],["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["qs2QcycggWU",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["069y1MpOkQY",1],["hBpetDxIEMU",1],["dX9CGRZwD-w",1]]},"HSxSgbNQi-g":{"wy":"HN recommends it whenever threads touch twin-engine ocean flights, diversions, or why the 747 and A380 are being retired.","q":{"x":"did a fanatastic video about this, really recommend it","i":15479810,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16052173,"author":"crikli","month":"2018-01","story":"A personal ode to the Boeing 747","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why the 747 and A380 are being phased out.","quote":"from my new favorite YouTube channel, Wendover Productions","text":"I was rolling my eyes at the subtitle. The article devolved from there. Some great (sometimes indirect) information on why the 747 and to a lesser extent the A380 are being phased out, from my new favorite YouTube channel, Wendover Productions: The Economics of Long-Haul Flights: [LINK] ETOPS: Explained [VIDEO] Politics of Aviation / 5 Freedoms of Aviation: [LINK]"},{"id":15479810,"author":"filereaper","month":"2017-10","story":"As the 747 Begins Its Final Approach, a Pilot Takes a Flight Down Memory Lane","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Economics of airlines and why twinjets win.","quote":"did a fanatastic video about this, really recommend it","text":"Yup, Wendover Productions (massive aeroplane nerd) did a fanatastic video about this, really recommend it if anyone's interested in the economics of airlines. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21953360,"author":"CaliforniaKarl","month":"2020-01","story":"Getting a 787 back in the air after a diversion","stance":"rec","why":"ETOPS explainer covering the diversion incident.","quote":"of course there is a Wendover video on this topic, and indeed covering this specific incident!","text":"As this is aviation-related, of course there is a Wendover video on this topic, and indeed covering this specific incident! Small Planes Over Big Oceans (ETOPS Explained) (2017) [video] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15377155,"author":"jasode","month":"2017-10","story":"Air France A380 over Greenland on Sep 30th: Fan and engine inlet separated","stance":"rec","why":"Short explainer on ETOPS twinjet ocean flights.","quote":"affected the financial bets that Boeing made on 787 (point-to-point) vs Airbus with the A380 (hub-and-spoke)","text":"What amazes me more is that the newest twinjets are now considered reliable enough to fly six hours away from the nearest landing strip Yes, there's also a short video about that: [VIDEO] It's very interesting since ETOPS[1] affected the financial bets that Boeing made on 787 (point-to-point) vs Airbus with the A380 (hub-and-spoke). [1] [LINK]"},{"id":24526534,"author":"redis_mlc","month":"2020-09","story":"It was more efficient to blow up a real 747 than to use miniatures or CGI","stance":"reference","why":"ETOPS rules and why diversions matter.","quote":"You should study the ETOPS requirements to see how nervous the regulators are.","text":"FYI: Actually several have diverted. You should study the ETOPS requirements to see how nervous the regulators are. Each ETOPS flight has special requirements for alternates that have lodging, food and reasonably nearby (timewise) maintenance. (Cold Bay is not a fun place to be stranded.) It's unlikely that an airliner can maintain altitude on one engine, so the risk is high that once a problem occurs, things can go bad fast. Ditching 300 passengers in the open ocean guarantees fatalities. I understand that non-pilots think \"it's handled\", but that's not really the case. That's why the old expression \"it's a 4-engine ocean\" came about. \"Small Planes Over Big Oceans (ETOPS Explained)\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":16945367,"author":"nordsieck","month":"2018-04","story":"Tesla's Simultaneous Brilliance and Incompetence Revealed in Teardown of Model 3","stance":"reference","why":"Twin-engine aircraft safely flying over oceans.","quote":"Boeing and Airbus have just gotten so good at engineering safe 2 engine aircraft","text":"4 engine aircraft are technically more safe than 2 engine aircraft. Boeing and Airbus have just gotten so good at engineering safe 2 engine aircraft that companies can run them over oceans without too much trouble. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NlIdzF1_b5M",2],["B2482h_TNwg",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["gDfNRWsMRsU",1],["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["qs2QcycggWU",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["069y1MpOkQY",1],["hBpetDxIEMU",1],["thqbjA2DC-E",1],["vtWp45Eewtw",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["wROQUZDCIMI",1],["1bO8zEaSuWg",1],["wfm2xCKOCNk",1],["2y5Pv4yN0b0",1],["Hqw57GJSrac",1],["b5P3QDm61go",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1]]},"WTYet-qf1jo":{"wy":"Linked in Windows 8 threads as a funny but accurate summary of its usability problems.","q":{"x":"I think this video explains most of the problems with Windows 8","i":4993106,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":9521739,"author":"bad_user","month":"2015-05","story":"Microsoft to stop producing Windows versions","stance":"rec","why":"Funny video on Windows 8 unifying PC and tablet","quote":"see this funny video on the subject","text":"I'm an Ubuntu Linux and OS X user and have been trying to like Windows 8.1 in the last weeks. The problems I have in no particular order ... - network management in Windows 7 was usable, whereas in 8.1 it is totally fucked - Microsoft accounts are on their way to become mandatory, as creating a regular account is not user friendly and there's a big and scary warning when you go that route; they are basically trying to resurrect Windows Live ID - Microsoft has started to force people on using their products - I could not figure out a way to turn OneDrive off completely, or to use anything other than Bing as the search engine in their \"Search Everywhere\" (this also happens on Windows Phone) - I haven't seen a \"modern Windows\" app that I like, they are all pieces of crap, including stuff from Microsoft; modern Skype for example is lacking crucial functionality; their app store is filled with trademark-violating malware and I've never seen such a low quality app store - from a usability perspective Windows 8 is a failure - there's something inherently different about a PC with 21 inch monitors, keyboard and mouse, versus a tablet, yet Windows tries to unify both worlds, becoming hostile in the process - see this funny video on the subject - [VIDEO] - of course, you can totally ignore the \"modern Windows\" side of the coin for now, except that's where Windows is headed - I bought the standard edition, then I discovered that I needed the Pro version to get disk encryption or to conn"},{"id":6389669,"author":"Karunamon","month":"2013-09","story":"Windows 8.1’s user-hostile backup story","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth 20-minute critique of Windows 8 UX","quote":"Someone goes into a great deal of depth on the 8 UX","text":">unattractive, resource-intensive, usability damaging Subjective, compared to what, subjective > simple, efficient, usable flat design is in. subjective, subjective, subjective. I don't know where this utterly moronic \"flatten ALL THE THINGS\" design meme comes from, but I wish it would die in a fire. Flat UI, at least Microsoft's implementation of it, has a great deal of fail about it. It's hard to tell what's clickable and what's not, things are not organized in a logical or easy to reach manner, and on and on. Have a look at this if you have about 20 minutes to spare. Someone goes into a great deal of depth on the 8 UX: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4993106,"author":"mtgx","month":"2013-01","story":"Windows 8 proving less popular than Vista","stance":"rec","why":"Explains most problems with Windows 8's touch-first interface","quote":"I think this video explains most of the problems with Windows 8","text":"Microsoft made a mistake trying to force a made-for-touch interface on PC users, and in the end the strategy is not that different than forcing a PC interface on a touch device. It may actually be worse, because at least in that case you would be familiar with the interface, so at least it would have that advantage. I think this video explains most of the problems with Windows 8: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4980598,"author":"mtgx","month":"2012-12","story":"Fujitsu Not Pleased With Windows 8 Demand, Either","stance":"rec","why":"Animated critique of Windows 8 usability problems","quote":"This guy described a lot of the problems with Windows 8 and he is pretty spot on","text":"No, you feel that way because that's actually how it is. This guy described a lot of the problems with Windows 8 and he is pretty spot on (although he does exaggerate a bit in some cases): [VIDEO]"},{"id":9523301,"author":"hvidgaard","month":"2015-05","story":"Microsoft to stop producing Windows versions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm an Arch, OS X, and Windows user. I'm a developer in my day job, on the .net platform. I prefer Arch any day of the week, but I really like windows as well. > network management in Windows 7 was usable, whereas in 8.1 it is totally fucked I have zero problems with network management. It has not changed how you do it from the CLI, and the GUI is just different. If your definition of \"totally fucked\" includes \"new GUI\", then I'm not sure what to say. I agree that it's a step back from Windows 7, but saying it's totally fucked is downright wrong. > Microsoft accounts are on their way to become mandatory, as creating a regular account is not user friendly and there's a big and scary warning when you go that route; they are basically trying to resurrect Windows Live ID I was throughly against the Microsoft ID to start with, but for other reasons I had to get one and since I use multiple PCs it has actually been a positive experience for me. I see that as a good thing, and if you don't like it you can still create a local account. The warning is fair, because a lot of the new functionality depend on the MSID. > Microsoft has started to force people on using their products - I could not figure out a way to turn OneDrive off completely Nobody is forcing you to use OneDrive, it's a feature and if you don't like it don't use it. Simple as that. You either do not use a MSID as your login or disable it as this ( [LINK] ) MS article instructs. Just because you cannot remove an integral"}],"r":[["oyLBGkS5ICk",1],["uqH_Y1TupoQ",1],["J44svaQc5WY",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["1gEz__sMVaY",1],["amJ-ev8Ial8",1]]},"ax7f3JZJHSw":{"wy":"HN uses it to illustrate learned literacy in game and software UX, and how unfamiliar conventions confuse newcomers.","q":{"x":"The video has a bunch of other interesting observations.","i":27556331,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37430537,"author":"zjp","month":"2023-09","story":"Linux becoming a Windows / OS X clone","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how unfamiliar conventions confuse newcomers.","quote":"is a pretty good video about a similar thing","text":"I agree, for most people the system is a bootloader for Chrome. But this article is about one guy's disappointment he can't hold the whole system in his head anymore, and I'm just saying the user never cared about that in the first place. But also, if you've only used one operating system, there hasn't been any need to generalize what you've learned from that system interaction wise. We had to learn a bunch of computing conventions. Eventually you learn things like \"most command line tools have -v,--verbose\". Grandpa doesn't think computers work this way, he thinks Windows works this way and how should he know how Linux works. 'What games are like for someone who doesn't play games' is a pretty good video about a similar thing. It's easy for you, you know computing vernacular. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31894977,"author":"newswasboring","month":"2022-06","story":"Life is not short","stance":"rec","why":"Explains difficulty returning to modern games.","quote":"This video explains my feelings better than me","text":"I don't know man, I downloaded Cup Head because it looked cute and it has been kicking my ass since like a month. I know it is one of those notoriously hard games but I think the point stands. I think games have moved away from precision challenges to more abstract ideas. Like Doki Doki Literature Club, I have no f'ing clue whatever that is all about but I am experiencing it. Challenge for me comes from the fact that it relies on peoples knowledge of tropes to enable different story progressions. I don't have that because last game I played was in like 2009. Another game I am having trouble with is watch dogs 2. I know, AAA game, made for less than pro gamers. But again, they are relying on my knowledge of AAA games for conveyance. I regularly have moments where I am thinking, what do I do? where do I go? Now I have played GTA vice city, so open world is not an alien concept. But the scale of it all, its just magnificently complex. Edit: I occurs to me all this can just be the fact that I suck at video games. But I used to be good at them, that's the point. Edit 2: This video explains my feelings better than me [VIDEO]"},{"id":27556331,"author":"vinkelhake","month":"2021-06","story":"The UX of video game tutorials","stance":"rec","why":"Non-gamer wife plays games; reveals assumed base knowledge.","quote":"The video has a bunch of other interesting observations.","text":"This guy[1] put his non game playing wife in front of a bunch of games, one of them being SMB. He noted that she never learned how to sprint, and that the game doesn't tell you how to do it either. If you can't figure it out on your own, you have to be told about it, or read the manual. NES games (and other games of that era) were in general pretty bad at tutorials. Players back then were expected to figure things out for themselves, mostly by trial and error. The video has a bunch of other interesting observations. A common theme is that games often assume that the player has some base understanding of game play that might not always be there. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":22752412,"author":"gundmc","month":"2020-04","story":"An analysis of unclear lessons in video games (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Substantive look at unclear lessons in games.","quote":"very well done and much more substantive than this summary","text":"I really recommend clicking through and watching the referenced embedded video that forms the basis for this article. It's very well done and much more substantive than this summary. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31023165,"author":"rilezg","month":"2022-04","story":"Video games are kinda boring","stance":"reference","why":"Section on thinking games would be cooler.","quote":"","text":"This reminded me of the youtube video 'What Games Are Like For Someone Who Doesn't Play Games', specifically the section 'Thinking Games Would Be Cooler' (begins at 14:43): [VIDEO] I think games both appeal and do not appeal (to different people, or the same person at different times in life, respectively) because of the limitations of what the game allows you to do. It can be empowering to feel like you understand the world of the game you are playing, or disempowering to have to follow the arbitrary rules created by the developer."},{"id":30850574,"author":"rgoulter","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Best video games to expand teenage minds?","stance":"reference","why":"Wife lacked game literacy for basic interactions.","quote":"","text":"> And what does scene construction, story arc and so on mean when you can do anything ? I'm reminded of this video, where a game-playing husband discusses his non-gaming wife's attempts to play games. [VIDEO] Part of the problem his wife ran into was that she lacked the 'literacy' for games that her husband (who had played games growing up) had, and so wasn't able to perform basic interactions which he had experience with. I think deep discussions about a topic require familiarity with the basics. - What kind of discussion can you hope to have or appreciate if you don't share fundamental understanding? You can probably ask what they found fun, or what's cool about it. > what does story arc mean when you can do anything? You might be able to appreciate Liana Kerzner's Lady Bits series. e.g. in this video, she discusses what 'agency' means in terms of games. She considers that there's the player's agency, the writer's agency, and the (diagetec?) agency of the characters in the story. [LINK]"},{"id":24240720,"author":"throw149102","month":"2020-08","story":"Over three billion people worldwide now play video games, study reports","stance":"reference","why":"Non-gamer struggles to move camera and player together.","quote":"Interesting video talking about what games are like for a non-gamer","text":"Adding onto this, imagine how you would feel if someone said \"There are 3 billion Spanish speakers worldwide\" But then you learned that only 500 million were really fluent, and the rest ranged from broken Spanish that was enough to get by in a Spanish speaking country to people who were forced to take a Spanish class in highschool and never used it again. I think this concept of \"fluency\" in games is probably a more useful metric. The question is, if I dump you in front of a game that you've never played before, but it conforms to the language of video game design, how quickly can you beat it? I would imagine that someone who only played Candy Crush would not understand how to move their camera around in a 3D environment, for example.[1] You could extend the metaphor further, for example Spanish is spoken in many different ways around the globe, and therefore some people who speak one dialect of Spanish can't understand another dialect of Spanish. The same could be true for games, and we would need to classify and understand these dialects. [1] [VIDEO] Interesting video talking about what games are like for a non-gamer, shows an example of not understanding how to move the camera and the player at the same time."},{"id":21293340,"author":"savanaly","month":"2019-10","story":"Machines Beat Humans on a Reading Test","stance":"reference","why":"Games are hard without learned domain knowledge.","quote":"","text":"The funny thing is that even the easiest games are surprisingly difficult, even insurmountable, for otherwise capable humans who have not been \"trained\" on what to expect and learned domain knowledge through trial and error. See [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["x8TO-nrUtSI",1],["6avJHaC3C2U",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["eLqC3FNNOaI",1]]},"35Idb_lCU4o":{"wy":"HN recommended it in late 2020 as a detailed, science-based explanation of how the different vaccines work and how they were tested.","q":{"x":"This is the best science based informative video I have seen on how these different Covid vaccines actually work.","i":25344193,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25344193,"author":"tibbydudeza","month":"2020-12","story":"First person receives Pfizer Covid-19 vaccine in UK","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how the different Covid vaccines work","quote":"This is the best science based informative video I have seen on how these different Covid vaccines actually work.","text":"[VIDEO] by Ninja Nerd Lectures This is the best science based informative video I have seen on how these different Covid vaccines actually work."},{"id":27466825,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2021-06","story":"Heart inflammation cases in young men higher than expected after mRNA vaccines","stance":"rec","why":"Greater detail on the different COVID-19 vaccines","quote":"COVID-19 Vaccines goes into greater detail.","text":"Your insights are not entirely correct. There are more than two types of covid vaccines. Coronavirus Vaccines - An Introduction by JAMA is a good starting point. [LINK] COVID-19 Vaccines goes into greater detail. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26885992,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2021-04","story":"mRNA vaccines – a new era in vaccinology (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on the vaccine clinical trial process","quote":"I recommend listening to this lecture about the process","text":"Biology is messy and you can not say for certain that there will never be a group of individuals with an autoimmune issue. That is why vaccines go through several clinical trial phases. I recommend listening to this lecture about the process: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25382392,"author":"wenc","month":"2020-12","story":"FDA approves Pfizer vaccine – UC Santa Cruz scientists explain how it works","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at mRNA vaccines and clinical trials, biochem from 12:30","quote":"Here's a longer 43 minute video explaining them and the Moderna/Pfizer/BioNTech/Astrazeneca clinical trial processes in more detail.","text":"Here's a 5 minute video explaining mRNA vaccines in laymen's terms (Cliff-Notes style) [LINK] Here's a longer 43 minute video explaining them and the Moderna/Pfizer/BioNTech/Astrazeneca clinical trial processes in more detail. The biochem part starts at 12:30. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25403775,"author":"wenc","month":"2020-12","story":"Understanding mRNA Covid-19 vaccines","stance":"rec","why":"Technical explanation of vaccines and their clinical trials","quote":"Here's a longer, more technical, 43 minute video explaining them and the Moderna/Pfizer/BioNTech/Astrazeneca clinical trial processes in more detail.","text":"Reposting this: these two videos answers a bunch of questions that are in the comments. Here's a 5 minute video explaining mRNA vaccines in laymen's terms (Cliff-Notes style) [LINK] Here's a longer, more technical, 43 minute video explaining them and the Moderna/Pfizer/BioNTech/Astrazeneca clinical trial processes in more detail. The biochem part starts at 12:30. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25468955,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2020-12","story":"It took two days to develop Moderna’s vaccine","stance":"rec","why":"Walks through the whole biochemistry of mRNA vaccine response","quote":"He goes through the entire biochem process.","text":"It's not just proteins. The cells infected with the mRNA present those proteins on the outside of the cell to be recognized by other cells which start a chain process. Skip to around 12:30 in here: [VIDEO] He goes through the entire biochem process. Even the cells infected with the mRNA will need to be removed by the immune system using cytotoxic T-cells, so they need to get the chain process going. The reason we have \"memory\" for viruses is because a ton of random stuff in our immune systems evolved to multiply exponentially when they come in contact with viruses or proteins that indicate certain infections. It's a lot of stuff and you can't just inject that memory in; you have to stimulate the immune system to create them at a very large scale. It's not just antibodies. There are memory T-cells, parts of the complement system that get activated; a whole host of things. My issue is that our immune systems are incredibly complex, and there are a lot of components to this \"memory\" that are not entirely understood. Every other vaccine is an inactivaed virus (via heat treatment) or attenuated (virus run through other hosts to make it less dangerous to humans). The first vaccine was just a close relative: cow/horse pox instead of smallpox. The former are not dangerous in humans, but seem to produce the same immune memory. The mRNA vaccines are at attempt to say, \"Let's strip out all that other stuff and find just the thing we need.\" But our immune systems are incredibly complicat"}],"r":[["OmJ-4B-mS-Y",1],["S9JGmA5_unY",1],["vBiIDwBOqQA",1],["dW8Cy6WrO94",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["cTsEzmFamZ8",1],["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["ybcvlxivscw",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["zaXKQ70q4KQ",1],["2S0k12uZR14",1]]},"97t7Xj_iBv0":{"wy":"HN commenters like it as a high-quality, accessible look at an under-appreciated engineering idea and bring it up in snap-fit and hinge discussions.","q":{"x":"Veritasium videos are pretty darn high quality.","i":24044879,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24044879,"author":"mehrdadn","month":"2020-08","story":"Astronomers solve the missing baryon problem","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite Veritasium video on compliant mechanisms.","quote":"Veritasium videos are pretty darn high quality.","text":"Veritasium videos are pretty darn high quality. Fun to watch and learn from, sometimes mindblowingly. Definitely recommend subscribing. One of my favorites is Derek's video on compliant mechanisms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25329643,"author":"sitkack","month":"2020-12","story":"From LEGOs to Ziploc: The Science of the Snap Fit","stance":"rec","why":"Related to snap fits and compliant mechanisms.","quote":"Folks that like this might like CNC joinery and compliant mechanisms.","text":"Folks that like this might like CNC joinery and compliant mechanisms. [LINK] Why Machines That Bend Are Better [VIDEO]"},{"id":21593673,"author":"missosoup","month":"2019-11","story":"SpaceX's Starship Mk1 just blew off its bulkhead in a pressurization accident","stance":"rec","why":"Engineering involved in warhead design and safety.","quote":"Cool video about some of the engineering involved","text":"There wouldn't be much to 'leak'. The nuclear material in weapons is relatively safe to handle as long as you don't ingest it. It's the post-explosion fission products that are nasty. Also with nuclear weapons, as much engineering has gone into safety as into weapon design itself. It's astronomically improbable for a modern nuclear weapon to detonate prior to arming even if the explosive lens is set off. The reentry vehicle is designed to come down at circa mach 14 so a little bit of heat and jostling isn't going to bother it. Cool video about some of the engineering involved: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28126525,"author":"seg_lol","month":"2021-08","story":"Powering the Lunar Base","stance":"reference","why":"Compliant hinges proposed to handle lunar dust issues.","quote":"One could use compliant hinges [1] to help mitigate moon dust issues","text":"One could use compliant hinges [1] to help mitigate moon dust issues, those will have to be dealt with anyway. Found the link [LINK] > Light Bender is a novel concept for the generation and distribution of power on the lunar surface within the context of the Artemis mission and the “Long-Term Human Lunar Surface Presence” that will follow. The innovative concept is based on a heliostat that utilizes Cassegrain telescope optics as the primary means to capture, concentrate and focus the sun's light. A second key innovation is the use of a Fresnel lens to collimate this light for distribution to multiple end users at distances of a kilometer or more away without substantial losses. The redirected and concentrated solar energy is then converted to electricity at the end user’s location using small (2m-4m diameter) photovoltaic arrays that can be mounted on habitats, cryo-coolers, or mobile assets such as rovers or ISRU elements. This concept is superior to alternatives such as highly inefficient Laser Power Beaming, as it only converts light to electricity once, and to traditional power distribution architectures that rely on mass intensive cables. [1] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] This is a great recent example of a joystick adapter using compliant mechanisms, [LINK]"}],"r":[["rHIkrotSwcc",1],["0rCUF-V1esM",1],["QyQvfaW54NU",1],["PpG0z-npFIY",1],["EL7Au1tzNxE",1],["1OEu9C51K2A",1],["gzgyksS5pX8",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["RMWNvwLiXIQ",1]]},"S9JGmA5_unY":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to illustration of just how large 2^256 is.","q":{"x":"To comprehend how large a search space this is watch 3Blue1Brown's explanation","i":24780497,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":27428708,"author":"yamrzou","month":"2021-06","story":"DOJ seizes $2.3M in cryptocurrency paid to the ransomware extortionists Darkside","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Answers how secure 256-bit security really is","quote":"I went looking for answers and found this excellent video by 3Blue1Brown","text":"I wondered the same thing, so I went looking for answers and found this excellent video by 3Blue1Brown: How secure is 256 bit security? : [VIDEO]"},{"id":24780497,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2020-10","story":"Room-Temperature Superconductivity Achieved for the First Time","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how large a 256-bit search space is","quote":"To comprehend how large a search space this is watch 3Blue1Brown's explanation","text":"Large number comparisons are difficult for humans to comprehend. If you simplify life to a DNA strand 256 nucleotide long (for the sake of math comparison) - then the search space is 4^256. To comprehend how large a search space this is watch 3Blue1Brown's explanation [VIDEO]"},{"id":21996410,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2020-01","story":"The most important scientific problems have yet to be solved (1897)","stance":"rec","why":"Illustration of how enormous 2^256 is","quote":"3Blue1Browns wonderful illustration of how large 2^256 is","text":"We know that life is evolvable because life exists and we know the biochemical mechanisms involved (DNA + cellular biochemistry). Evolution implies a relatively smooth path through \"DNA space\" from, say for example, an early single cell eukaryote to a mushroom. However the search space is enormous. Even if we account for billions of years of evolution and a trillions of evolutionary experiments each year, a simple random walk with selection through DNA space should go nowhere because of the numbers involved. The curse of dimensionality[0] means there has to be some other principle of nature to make the search space yield a path from one viable life form to another. The search space of life would have to be 'smooth' in some sense. That 'smoothness' is something we don't understand. If DNA space is just 256 bits (as a dramatic simplification), then 2^256 is a very very big space to search just by chance [1]. Now imagine a space orders of magnitude bigger. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] (3Blue1Browns wonderful illustration of how large 2^256 is)"},{"id":35210184,"author":"ChancyChance","month":"2023-03","story":"SETI@home Is in Hibernation","stance":"reference","why":"Frames the scale of compute needed to crack 256-bit hashes","quote":"finding a SHA-256 collision would take so much compute power we don't have words to describe it","text":"What was the RSA crack effort you mention? It doesn't seem feasible, considering finding a SHA-256 collision would take so much compute power we don't have words to describe it, and even coming up with thoughts to frame the problem is hard ( [VIDEO] ). I can't imagine cracking even an RSA2048 key with everyone working on it."},{"id":32670815,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2022-09","story":"US Government Bans Export of Nvidia A100 and H100 GPUs to China and Russia","stance":"reference","why":"Absurd compute still has tiny chance of cracking 256 bits","quote":"it would still have a 1 in 32 billion chance of cracking.","text":"Even if you had your GPU-packed Kilo-Google per person multiplanetary Gigagalactic computer guessing numbers for 37 times the age of the universe, it would still have a 1 in 32 billion chance of cracking. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OmJ-4B-mS-Y",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["vBiIDwBOqQA",1],["dW8Cy6WrO94",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["cTsEzmFamZ8",1],["s254IPHXgVA",1],["nm1TxQj9IsQ",1],["wGt4XwBbCvA",1]]},"_fHfgU8oMSo":{"wy":"HN cites it as a vivid example of media consolidation and coordinated messaging.","q":{"x":"the Deadspin video of dozens of local news anchors reading the same editorial content handed down by Sinclair Broadcast Group is striking","i":34820265,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":25386329,"author":"EGreg","month":"2020-12","story":"We can have democracy or we can have Facebook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sinclair stations reading identical scripts","quote":"No really, watch it. It’s black mirror level","text":"It’s more than facebook. It’s CAPITALISM. People have to always preface any criticism of it by saying “there is nothing at all wrong with capitalism, but...” But no, the actual ECONOMIC SYSTEM and the incentives and constraints it exerts on institutions and organizations is responsible for what is happening. It selects for the current types of organizations and outcomes by its very nature. You see, both our news and our social networks are driven by a profit motive and face market competition . Thus they are forced to adapt to changing circumstancss in certain ways: Act 1. Journalism was disrupted by the Internet, and survived by adopting the FOX News model of locking in an audience and being biased, showing one part of the story. MSNBC explicitly did it, while CNN jumped the shark after the Malaysian Airlines flight, realizing it can lock in audience. One sided clickbait outrage articles are the norm. There is some attempt to have journalistic integrity and policy focus at Vox and NowThis, but then you also have MotherJones, OccupyDemocrats, DailyKos and more on the left. You have all kinds of independent podcasts like TYT network, Kyle Kulinski, David Pakman, Majority Report, etc. And similarly for the conservatives, libertarians, etc. We are divided also because giant corporations like Sinclair Television bought up all the local stations and outlets, resulting in this: [VIDEO] No really, watch it. It’s black mirror level, and you can see the capitalism at work here. Top do"},{"id":34820265,"author":"justin66","month":"2023-02","story":"Half of Americans now believe that news organizations deliberately mislead them","stance":"rec","why":"Shows dozens of local anchors reading identical Sinclair-supplied script.","quote":"the Deadspin video of dozens of local news anchors reading the same editorial content handed down by Sinclair Broadcast Group is striking","text":"> In one small consolation, in both cases Americans had more trust in local news. This trust in \"local\" news is often misplaced given how many local news outlets - television stations or newspapers - have been subsumed by larger interests. For anyone who hasn't seen it, the Deadspin video of dozens of local news anchors reading the same editorial content handed down by Sinclair Broadcast Group is striking: [VIDEO] [LINK] There is an awful lot of really, really good stuff put out by local newspapers and TV stations but people ought to be thoughtful about their use of any media."},{"id":30632652,"author":"Minor49er","month":"2022-03","story":"DuckDuckGo “down-rank sites associated with Russian disinformation”","stance":"rec","why":"Local stations reading identical scripts","quote":"you might find this video either amusing or alarming","text":"It doesn't need to be a secret. If you have a megaphone that everyone hears all the time, you will simply have more influence than people who whisper quietly. 6 companies dominate the media [1] which together make up at least 90% of all media [2]. Note that this isn't limited to just news. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] Also, you might find this video either amusing or alarming [VIDEO]"},{"id":41726703,"author":"trod123","month":"2024-10","story":"Uber terms mean couple can't sue after 'life-changing' crash","stance":"reference","why":"Sinclair anchors reading identical script.","quote":"","text":"Voting - First-pass-the-post fails mathematically once two majority parties come to power, no third party can win, and if both parties are subverted (into one hidden party with a hidden agenda) democracy then fails. We are seeing a perfect example of this with regards to Harris/Trump. Trump closely aligns with Fascism, Harris policies closely align with Marxism/Communism. You only have a choice between the two, is that even a choice? Protest - Has always relied on the inherent threat of violence and leaders being responsive to the groups involved. It has been shown in the last 20 years that the threat of violence no longer works, and leaders have learned to be no longer responsive at all once they have been elected so long as they don't commit any crimes or scandals that would force removal of them from their positions. Additionally, when protests occur, surveillance of any protest guarantees government retaliation moving forward for all individuals involved. Surveillance is never by itself, there is always government harassment associated with it that are often impossible to prove. Any protester has likely seen the telltale signs of this, and it never ends. (i.e. mail goes missing, both sending and receiving, communications/cell phone calls become unreliable in ways ou can't know a message was sent, internet services may throw weird errors on a continual basis, email/submissions dropped silently (i.e. job applications/resumes, or other high importance items like taxes). Crim"},{"id":36089806,"author":"bombastry","month":"2023-05","story":"Former Deadspin writer raided by FBI for Fox News hack","stance":"reference","why":"Compilation of Sinclair anchors reading the same script, by Timothy Burke.","quote":"","text":"To provide a bit of context, this article is about Timothy Burke. His most famous work is the compilation video of news anchors on Sinclair-owned local news stations reading the same script about how media bias is \"extremely dangerous to our democracy\".[1] He is also known for breaking the infamous Manti Te'o fake girlfriend story.[2] Additionally, he is noted for having a complicated computing setup to monitor, archive, and disseminate videos from dozens of live sources simultaneously. Some of these details appeared in a profile by the New York Times [3] and also appear in this article. Burke often shares short clips of offbeat moments from sports and news channels. The article states that he has recently shared behind-the-scenes clips of Tucker Carlson on his former Fox show; it appears likely that this raid was related to determining the origin of those clips. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":35042057,"author":"specialist","month":"2023-03","story":"Chinese disinformation targeting U.S. features fake people on fake news programs","stance":"reference","why":"Sinclair anchors reading same script, cited as automation precedent.","quote":"","text":"Max Headroom goes mainstream. This tech will save the Murdochs, Sinclairs, every purveyor of infotainment, so much money . No more salaries for anchors. No more huge payouts to settle (sexual) harassment and descrimination lawsuits. Agitprop like this will be automated, turnkey, ubiquitous: Sinclair's Local News Anchors all Read Same Script [VIDEO] So much potential. These are exciting times for trolls and grifters."},{"id":35046927,"author":"colinwilyb","month":"2023-03","story":"Chinese disinformation targeting U.S. features fake people on fake news programs","stance":"reference","why":"Anchors reading identical scripts, cited as already-perfected format.","quote":"The format is already perfected.","text":"How much computing power is required to duplicate current news anchors and feed them a script? The format is already perfected. [VIDEO] [Sinclair Broadcast controversy]"},{"id":31078280,"author":"tomatowurst","month":"2022-04","story":"Six companies control 90% of what you read, watch, and hear","stance":"reference","why":"Outlets repeating the exact same message","quote":"Reminds me of this viral video of different US media outlets saying the exact same thing.","text":"Reminds me of this viral video of different US media outlets saying the exact same thing. It made me think whether the claim that US intelligence agencies placing an insider was true or not. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29636310,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2021-12","story":"For Third Year, Committee to Protect Journalists Excludes Assange","stance":"reference","why":"Corporate interests over people","quote":"corporate interests over those of people","text":"I think that most systems are flawed and, given enough time, will be corrupted and ruined. That has happened with (to my knowledge) every communist nation out there, from USSR to Cuba. Similarly, that has already happened with the capitalist US: corporate interests over those of people, and the interests of the rich over everyone else; at times, it's something like the plot right out of a Deus Ex game: [VIDEO] . Honestly, the same is also probably happening with the more socialist leaning EU, at least based on how many stories about corruption and bribes one hears on the news in my country, not even thinking about the same on a grander scale. I don't really have any solutions to offer or ideologies to push and i'm not sure whether individuals being politically active is (always) even that effective, just look at Russia and how many people there are in prisons, or how polarized everything is in US with identity politics. All i can say for sure is that humans and human nature are the common denominator in every single one of those systems, in which the reality eventually differs from the theory behind them."},{"id":29147648,"author":"indrora","month":"2021-11","story":"Students pay price for UM diversity goals (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Sinclair pushing must-run content to local outlets","quote":"Must-Run content","text":"Conjecture, but backed up by the experiences I had with Journalism classes in college, part of my major: Such pieces are typically written by a high-ranking individual within the publication, but they wish to deflect the backlash that can come from writing such a piece, so the \"staff\" name is used to shield the writer from criticism. It's mostly Must-Run content: Content that a funding source or otherwise powerful position within the organization has deemed must be printed. Must-Run content is commonly used to burn or otherwise redirect vitriol, or more often establish a common message among many publications. Such things are also used to push local news outlets to [tow a line]( [VIDEO] ) by their parent organizations (such as Sinclair Media group) In the case of staff revolting, it's used to shield the individuals. In the case of a single high ranking individual, it's used to shield the individual by forcing the group to take the hit. The practice is common enough it is [regularly a topic of discussion in citation styles]( [LINK] )."},{"id":44213430,"author":"KennyBlanken","month":"2025-06","story":"Musk-Trump dispute includes threats to SpaceX contracts","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"First off, Trump skyrocketed to political fame with his nonsense claims about Obama's citizenship. The slide started in the 80's when Reagan killed off the 'fairness doctrine' which meant news outlets could present completely one-sided coverage of an issue. Couple that with massive consolidation of newspapers and TV news stations where all the programming is heavily coordinated and groups like Sinclair started pushing identically worded \"false news\" narratives across all their stations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42953507,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2025-02","story":"Payments crisis of 2025: Not “read only” access anymore","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your \"local\" news is regularly contractually obligrated to run segments written by a right wing company: [VIDEO] \"This is dangerous to democracy\""},{"id":43196815,"author":"genewitch","month":"2025-02","story":"The Deep Research problem","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"a sincere question; how is >A \"review website\" which is all that, but with fake reviews that promote one brand over the others… a chain of such websites that link to each other to boost PageRank scores… which are then cross-linked with a huge number of social media bots…\" functionally different than a majority of news outlets in the US parroting the exact same story, verbatim? This is extremely dangerous to our democracy. [VIDEO] * please note, i am glibly linking that youtube video as a completely transparent example of what i am talking about. There are many (many) more examples. don't read into the content of the video so much, and just think about the implications."},{"id":42127487,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2024-11","story":"Ask HN: What Is Wrong with the News?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"What do you consider \"the news\" Skimming the Infotainment dopamine sites for fun but not for news. There has never been a news site and probably won't ever be in my opinion. Not newspapers, radio, television, scrawling on cave walls. Everyone has multiple agendas, hidden agendas and some more obvious. Somewhat off topic from your question I enjoy when people make a mashup of all the \"news\" sites repeating the exact narratives they were given verbatim whilst playing circus music. Example [1] [1] - [VIDEO] [video][2 mins][warning: does not contain circus music]"},{"id":42226565,"author":"t-writescode","month":"2024-11","story":"Anti-scale: a response to AI in journalism","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm not sure you can trust local news stations, either, especially when they're all owned by the same company.","text":"I'm not sure you can trust local news stations, either, especially when they're all owned by the same company. For example, [VIDEO]"},{"id":39918661,"author":"mmh0000","month":"2024-04","story":"No joke: FTC boss goes on the Daily Show and is told Apple tried to block her","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"More alarming, some media companies publish the same fake stories without checking facts first\"[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39834201,"author":"wvenable","month":"2024-03","story":"Florida's DeSantis signs law restricting social media for people under 16","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"The point is his fetish is in the show's content! Although, still, a few exceptions aside doesn't invalidate your point. TV being a single group effort is much better than thousands of individual voices, what could possibly go wrong with that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32076314,"author":"yomkippur","month":"2022-07","story":"News engagement plummets as Americans tune out","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"people no longer trust the mainstream news outlets","text":"It's almost like people no longer trust the mainstream news outlets and its not tough to see why: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32270446,"author":"cies","month":"2022-07","story":"Yes, social media is undermining democracy","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Which democracy? The US is barely democratic. To be truly democratic in my book, the people need to rule. Voting on one of 2 evils every few years while having \"your representatives\" raped by lobbyists 24/7 is not people ruling: it's a facade for a system in which money rules. Pieces like this merely serve as a pretext for your fake-democracy to get more powers to sensor the, in this case, \"social\" media. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27374387,"author":"cryptoz","month":"2021-06","story":"King County, WA bans facial recognition software","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They are probably the worst media conglomerate in America honestly.","text":"The linked source is a Sinclair News outlet. They are probably the worst media conglomerate in America honestly. Their news is constant fear and I view anything by them as poisonous information because it is just as likely meant to mislead as it is to inform. Sure, KOMO publishes some real news. They also publish lies and wildly misleading stuff. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["ZMByI4s-D-Y",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["qOP2V_np2c0",1],["8q71hrwUcu0",1],["xoVJKj8lcNQ",1],["ipRvjS7q1DI",1],["FegjLbCnoBw",1]]},"c2J91UG6Fn8":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear explanation of why old headlights looked as they did, and as a case study in mandating requirements versus solutions.","q":{"x":"has, as so often the case, a great video about why old car headlights were the shapes they were","i":35211611,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35211611,"author":"jameshart","month":"2023-03","story":"A Short History of the VW Mark 1 Rabbit/Golf","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great explanation of why old headlights had their shapes","quote":"has, as so often the case, a great video about why old car headlights were the shapes they were","text":"Tech Connections has, as so often the case, a great video about why old car headlights were the shapes they were - and why in later models they often changed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36563914,"author":"dharmab","month":"2023-07","story":"Tesla Gives New UK Owners a ‘Reacher’ Stick to Deal with Left-Hand Drive Cars","stance":"rec","why":"Why driving LHD cars in RHD countries is unsafe at night","quote":"Technology Connections did a great video about this.","text":"There's two major safety issues: You can't see around cars to be able to pass. Okay, so you often don't pass. But much more importantly, car headlights aren't symmetrical. The one pointing towards the oncoming lane has a \"cut\" beam to avoid blinding other drivers while the other has a taller beam to improve visibility of anything entering the road. When you drive LHD in a RHD country, or vice versa, you are blinding other drivers while having low visibility of pedestrians and animals. Technology Connections did a great video about this. His solution was to stop driving his RHD car in low light. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33230205,"author":"xani_","month":"2022-10","story":"The US will finally allow adaptive beam headlights on new cars","stance":"rec","why":"Why the US was stuck with the same headlight design","quote":"Also interesting piece about why US was stuck in decades of same light design","text":"Also interesting piece about why US was stuck in decades of same light design because of more stupid regulations [VIDEO]"},{"id":33355444,"author":"NeoTar","month":"2022-10","story":"Where did all the “reject” buttons come from?","stance":"rec","why":"Example of mandating a solution versus mandating requirements","quote":"illustrated a good example of the difference between mandating a solution, and mandating requirements.","text":"There is a YouTube channel called Technology Connections which has a video about American car headlights: [VIDEO] which I think illustrated a good example of the difference between mandating a solution, and mandating requirements. Considering car headlights. Obviously there are some requirements we should make - they need to be bright enough to safely light-up the road. They should not blind oncoming drivers. They need to be reasonably safe in the event of a collision. Between the sixties and 1984, the government of the USA mandated a solution. Basically one model of headlights was permitted (with a few minor variations introduced over time). If you look at all car models from this time they have a similar appearance, and the same headlights are still in use in some commercial vehicles today. In most European nations (and other parts of the world) instead a requirement, or series of requirements were made. If you met these standards for lighting level, glare, etc. your headlight was permitted. This led to European and Japanese cars have very varied headlight models, while most American cars were forced to stick to the same old style. This meant that American drivers missed out on innovations in this space. Mandates can very easily become out-dates. Whereas well written requirements are much more timeless."},{"id":33868064,"author":"adql","month":"2022-12","story":"Cops Can Extract Data from 10k Different Car Models’ Infotainment Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Example of regulation leaving US with ineffective headlights","quote":"","text":"Right but in this case the actions taken were wrong, were not fixed, and still are not fixed. Like the fact only in near future it will be legal for US cars to have adaptive lights. Or how for years [1] near every car light was same ineffective design. Over-regulation is as dangerous as under-regulation. If car manufacturers have to squeeze fleet averages (which is on its own retarded idea, just tax every model separately ffs) for normal cars. Or how apparently 20MPG sportscar is \"gas guzzler\" but 15MPG SUV is not * [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33959191,"author":"cogman10","month":"2022-12","story":"Musk, the Anti-Censorship Crusader, Allegedly Shadowbanned ElonJet Account","stance":"reference","why":"Argument for regulation standardizing designs","quote":"I think for it to work, you pretty much need federal regulation to step in and standardize.","text":"I think for it to work, you pretty much need federal regulation to step in and standardize. I have this video in mind [1] If we can have EV packs sizes, structural capabilities, voltages, and max currents defined then we can start having swapping services and maybe even better recycling. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["meHYBhcpdvQ",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["DlTq8DbRs4k",1],["N2jZryt607U",1],["usSfgHGEGxQ",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["smStEPSRKBs",1],["1wAaI_6b9JE",1],["VdqhHKjepiE",1],["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1]]},"rimtaSgGz_4":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on big tech, monopolies and adversarial interoperability as the clearest statement of his argument.","q":{"x":"I really recommend Cory's talk at DEF CON","i":37823123,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37823123,"author":"parski","month":"2023-10","story":"To Fight Big Tech, We Must Seize the Means of Computation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Doctorow's DEF CON talk on fighting big tech","quote":"I really recommend Cory's talk at DEF CON","text":"I really recommend Cory's talk at DEF CON and he was a guest on the Srsly Wrong podcast. DEF CON: [VIDEO] Srsly Wrong: [LINK] It's a great podcast. I recommend the episodes on degrowth and half earth socialism in particular."},{"id":39059546,"author":"smashah","month":"2024-01","story":"Haier hits Home Assistant plugin dev with takedown notice","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on adversarial interoperability","quote":"This is a good one","text":"You can see \"Adversarial Interoperability\" on Youtube to see talks on this. This is a good one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37668933,"author":"neycoda","month":"2023-09","story":"FTC Sues Amazon for Illegally Maintaining Monopoly Power","stance":"rec","why":"Doctorow on halting enshittification and antitrust","quote":"","text":"Finally. This is the kind of thing that needs to happen to all of these giant corporations and utilities fighting for monopolies. Incidentally I heard today how many laws against anti-competitive behavior were stripped with Carter and Reagan onward, and it's become a trend in our hyper-corporatist world now that's hurting workers and income classes below the highest. Watch \"DEF CON 31 - An Audacious Plan to Halt the Internet's Ensh*ttification - Cory Doctorow\" on YouTube [VIDEO]"},{"id":37679822,"author":"takeda","month":"2023-09","story":"Google's 25th Birthday","stance":"rec","why":"Doctorow argues for fixing antitrust law","quote":"What is needed is what Cory Doctorow is saying.","text":"What is needed is what Cory Doctorow is saying. [VIDEO] We need to push politicians to fix the antitrust law. It isn't just Google that is benefiting from this now toothless law."},{"id":37830948,"author":"flipgimble","month":"2023-10","story":"John Riccitiello steps down as CEO of Unity after pricing battle","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow popularizing enshittification, applied to Unity","quote":"This is another example enshittification that Doctorow is popularizing","text":"It strikes me that this Penny Arcade cartoon is more prescient that I originally gave it credit for: [LINK] On the surface the cartoon is silly and extreme but speaks a deeper truth. Consider that SilverLake is the entity that has proven itself to care only about extracting profit and nothing about games as cultural expression of the community and welfare of those who make them. Riccitiello was incompetent in how he made the exploitation of game developers obvious but given Unity's funding structure they will keep doing the same. Maybe now they're scared that they are losing a valuable resource of developer trust, but they consider it only as far as it is a resource for profit extraction. This is another example enshittification that Doctorow is popularizing ( [VIDEO] ) and is so well illustrated in the cartoon above."},{"id":37611573,"author":"dudefeliciano","month":"2023-09","story":"Amazon Prime Video content to start including ads next year","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow talk on enshittification relevant to ads in Prime Video","quote":"An Audacious Plan to Halt the Internet's Ensh*ttification","text":"this was also my first thought after watching the DEF CON talk: An Audacious Plan to Halt the Internet's Ensh*ttification[1] 1: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aXflBZXAucQ",1]]},"sI1C9DyIi_8":{"wy":"HN repeatedly quotes its thesis that humanity's greatest shortcoming is failing to understand the exponential function.","q":{"x":"it shows a common blindspot in a way that is both clear, enlightening and frightening.","i":12639698,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":36821392,"author":"theossuary","month":"2023-07","story":"There’s a heatwave in the sea and scientists are worried","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter's favorite guest lecture ever","quote":"Your point reminds me of my favorite guest lecture I've ever seen","text":"Your point reminds me of my favorite guest lecture I've ever seen: [VIDEO] About 2/3rds through makes the same point you do."},{"id":12639698,"author":"shahar2k","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, enlightening and frightening talk on exponential blindspot","quote":"it shows a common blindspot in a way that is both clear, enlightening and frightening.","text":"[VIDEO] \"the greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function\" not a high tech talk, or particularly technically complex, but it shows a common blindspot in a way that is both clear, enlightening and frightening."},{"id":36841198,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"How, Mathematically, We’re Fucked: Exponential growth on a finite planet","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among sobering talks on overshoot and collapse","quote":"I recommend few videos for you.","text":"> Burn it all down and start over We won't be the ones doing that. > We're going to have to figure this sustainability thing out though It may be already late. I wish it wasn't so. I think we could have chance at repairing it ... if only we would do what's necessary. But we're not doing what's necessary, we never started even discussing it, and I'm starting to doubt that we'll ever do. We're talking only about climate change, but that's only one of the symptoms of the overshoot. Even people @ hacker news mostly don't have a clue. > we can't just ignore the problems We've been ignoring them for 50-70 years. The limits to growth / club of Rome was in 1972? So far ... we did nothing but ignored the problems. We may cross the 1.5C threshold in 5 years. Not in 2050 as previously expected, but in 5 years. And we're still pursuing economic growth. Have you noticed any evidence to the contrary lately? > it's going to be a big challenge, and things may get ugly I recommend few videos for you. I must warn you ... it may hamper your ability to enjoy parties. Collapse: The Only Realistic Scenario - Arthur Keller [LINK] How to Enjoy the End of the World - Dr. B. Sidney Smith [LINK] Arithmetic, Population and Energy - Dr. Albert A. Bartlett [VIDEO] Why Societies Collapse And What it Means for Us - Joseph A. Tainter [LINK] Revealing the Naked Emperor – Paris, 2° & Carbon Budgets - Kevin Anderson [LINK]"},{"id":41691696,"author":"carapace","month":"2024-09","story":"Solving climate change by abusing thermodynamic scaling laws","stance":"reference","why":"Full-length Bartlett lecture on sustainability","quote":"See \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy: Sustainability 101\", Al Bartlett","text":"Do you imagine we will box up our terrestrial waste heat and launch it into the sun? The portion people who leave the Earth do not matter, the fate of the portion who remain remains the same. See \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy: Sustainability 101\", Al Bartlett [LINK] Full length video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29657916,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2021-12","story":"When Fossil Fuels Run Out, What Then? (It's 2060) (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Hubbert's peak oil predictions and oil discovery rates","quote":"It's mostly about oil, but he cites the work of M Hubbert","text":"See this video from about 1999 - Arithmetic, Population and Energy by Dr Albert Bartlett, about this timestamp[1] for one example. It's mostly about oil, but he cites the work of M Hubbert which was from ~1956 and predicting peak oil production in the USA around 1980 and running out maybe in 2100. Not the \"running out soon\" that you claim. He also suggests the drops in production which delay the Hubbert peak correspond to OPEC oil price rises. Then see his updated talk from 2011 link[2] which includes \" what we're taking out each year is something like three times what we're discovering each year \". Wikipedia critiques the Hubbert curve[4] saying \" new extraction methods began reversing this trend beginning in the mid-2000s decade, with production reaching 10.07 million b/d in November 2017 – the highest monthly level of crude oil production in U.S. history \". The US Energy Information Authority (EIA) explains[3] \" much of the increase in 2009 was associated with deepwater developments in the Federal Gulf of Mexico \" and \" Operators drilling at the Bakken and other shale formations are combining horizontal wells and hydraulic fracturing \". That is, turning to resources which are harder and more expensive to extract, resources which return fewer barrels of usble oil per barrel of oil of energy put in to extract them than easier to access resources. In the first talk he says \" We have very good ways now of estimating how much oil remains undiscovered. Now every so often someone"},{"id":21218109,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-10","story":"Some corals regrow after 'fatal' warming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Also Al Bartlett: \"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function.","text":"Specifically: \"In 2016, a report published by the UK All-Party Parliamentary Group on Limits to Growth concluded that \"there is unsettling evidence that society is still following the 'standard run' of the original study – in which overshoot leads to an eventual collapse of production and living standards\".[45] The report also points out that some issues not fully addressed in the original 1972 report, such as climate change, present additional challenges for human development.\" Also Al Bartlett: \"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function.\" Transcript: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO] Previously mentioned: [LINK]"},{"id":20723834,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-08","story":"The Population Bust","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Al Bartlett's thesis: \"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function","text":"Also about that Asimov's article: [LINK] Also, Al Bartlett's thesis: \"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function\" [VIDEO] The transcript: [LINK]"},{"id":20552490,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-07","story":"The human body is already close to thermal limits in many regions","stance":"reference","why":"Bacteria in a bottle illustration of exponential growth","quote":"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function","text":"I'd like to quote Al Bartlett: His thesis: \" The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function \" An example from the rest of the talk: \"Bacteria grow by doubling. One bacterium divides to become two, the two divide to become 4, the 4 become 8, 16 and so on. Suppose we had bacteria that doubled in number this way every minute. Suppose we put one of these bacteria into an empty bottle at 11:00 in the morning, and then observe that the bottle is full at 12:00 noon. There's our case of just ordinary steady growth : it has a doubling time of one minute, it’s in the finite environment of one bottle. I want to ask you three questions. Number one: at what time was the bottle half full? Well, would you believe 11:59, one minute before 12:00? Because they double in number every minute. And the second question: if you were an average bacterium in that bottle, at what time would you first realize you were running of space? Well, let’s just look at the last minutes in the bottle. At 12:00 noon, it’s full; one minute before, it’s half full; 2 minutes before, it’s a quarter full; then an 1/8th; then a 1/16th. Let me ask you, at 5 minutes before 12:00, when the bottle is only 3% full and is 97% open space just yearning for development, how many of you would realize there’s a problem? \" [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":16185415,"author":"Sacho","month":"2018-01","story":"The cost of games","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates exponential growth hitting resource limits suddenly","quote":"This is repeatedly exemplified in Professor Allen Bartlett's talk \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy","text":"But this is the problem of exponential growth - you can sustain it without even noticing the detrimental effects, until suddenly you can't. The resource usage of all the doubling time periods until the last one would have used up only 50% of the resources available(this could be millions of years, depending on the exponent), and then suddenly the last one would use up all the resources left. This is repeatedly exemplified in Professor Allen Bartlett's talk \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy\"(video link at specific point: [VIDEO] transcript: [LINK] )"},{"id":14414607,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2017-05","story":"China Can’t Sustain Its Debt-Fueled Binge, Moody’s Says","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the insanity of fixed-rate growth indefinitely","quote":"This video more or less shows the insanity of trying to grow at a fixed rate","text":"All the fears around growth is from people not understanding the difference between absolute growth and relative growth. Which is better growth of 1000 or 3% ? the developed world cannot grow at high percentage points because they're so large. This video more or less shows the insanity of trying to grow at a fixed rate (say being an \"emerging market\" at 6% per year) for an indefinite timespan. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13554870,"author":"acqq","month":"2017-02","story":"To Live Your Best Life, Do Mathematics","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function","text":"\"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":13637165,"author":"espeed","month":"2017-02","story":"Big Picture of Calculus (2010) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Bartlett's famous lecture on exponential function","quote":"That's the thesis of Dr. Bartlett's famous lecture \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy\"","text":"\"The greatest shortcoming of the human race is our inability to understand the exponential function.\" - Dr. Albert A. Bartlett That's the thesis of Dr. Bartlett's famous lecture \"Arithmetic, Population and Energy\": [VIDEO] Calculus is foundational for understanding our rapidly changing, data-driven world. It gives us the ability to see. You can apply calculus anytime you have a quantity that's changing over time, and you want to see where things are going, how things are changing, and when something is going to happen based on its rate of change -- e.g. stocks, startup growth metrics, how an idea is spreading, mass-market adoption, etc -- anytime you have time-series data and you want to see what it means -- make sense out of it, and understand its implications. See [LINK] And Calculus gives you the ability to calculate how lifelong learning can impact your growth over time -- the significance it can have throughout your life/career -- and realize it's never too late to learn anything ;-) In the words of Prof John Ousterhout: \"A little bit of slope makes up for a lot of y-intercept.\" [LINK]"}],"r":[["UcN7SGGoCNI",1],["gSPNRu4ZPvE",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["G0R09YzyuCI",1],["o0VrZPBskpg",1],["5WPB2u8EzL8",1],["qPb_0JZ6-Rc",1],["X-iSQQgOd1A",1],["DkMwYxfSYNQ",1],["BwGsr3SzCZc",1],["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1]]},"ExwqNreocpg":{"wy":"HN admires it as a creative hack and cites it as the inspiration for later tiny-game and QR-code projects.","q":{"x":"I admire this type of content where the creator take some concepts we [all] use and understand","i":32271501,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32271501,"author":"redbell","month":"2022-07","story":"Embedding an EXE inside a .REG file with automatic execution","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Creative combination of familiar concepts into something new","quote":"I admire this type of content where the creator take some concepts we [all] use and understand","text":"I admire this type of content where the creator take some concepts we [all] use and understand then combine them in a specific, but a new way, add some tweaks to get some never-seen results. Here is another experience that, as a Windows developer, blows my mind for a while. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25252602,"author":"fath0m","month":"2020-11","story":"Flappy Bird in 228 bytes","stance":"rec","why":"Snake game fitting inside a QR code","quote":"Quite an interesting watch.","text":"Using all the browser functionality feels like cheating. There is a good YouTube video, where a person made a snake game that fits inside a QR Code (albeit a larger version that we're used to, which can hold up to 2953 bytes). You can check it out here: [VIDEO] Quite an interesting watch."},{"id":43732217,"author":"redbell","month":"2025-04","story":"Show HN: I made a Doom-like game fit inside a QR code","stance":"reference","why":"Inspiration for fitting a game in a QR code","quote":"I was heavily inspired by this work","text":"Really cool project! TIL about 'data:' URLs—while I was familiar with the 'data:' URI scheme and had used it before, I didn’t realize it could be used as a full URL. Funny enough, I had been thinking about building something similar that fits entirely within a QR code, but I held off because I mistakenly thought it would require an HTTP(s) link. I was heavily inspired by this work: Can you fit a whole game into a QR code?( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":43734015,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2025-04","story":"Show HN: I made a Doom-like game fit inside a QR code","stance":"reference","why":"Storing arbitrary binary data in QR codes","quote":"","text":"QR codes have various encoding modes: numeric, alphanumeric, 8 bit and kanji. The most common is alphanumeric encoding and the densest is 8 bit encoding which just stores binary data. The QR code standards seem to be a little ambiguous on the meaning and purpose of the 8 bit encoding. I got the impression they added it to support alternative character encodings. Still, it's a mode that \"represents an 8-bit byte value directly\". > The default interpretation for QR Code is ECI 000020 representing the JIS8 and Shift JIS character sets. > 8.3.4 8-bit Byte Mode > The 8-bit byte mode handles the 8-bit Latin/Kana character set in accordance with JIS X 0201 (character values 00HEX to FFHEX). > In this mode data is encoded at a density of 8 bits/character. > 8.4.4 8-bit Byte Mode > In this mode, one 8 bit codeword directly represents the JIS8 character value of the input data character as shown in Table 6, i.e. a density of 8 bits/character. > In ECIs other than the default ECI, it represents an 8-bit byte value directly. In any case, it is possible to use QR codes to store arbitrary binary data. The qrencode tool can do this natively. Decoder support is more tricky, they tend to assume all QR codes contain text. I had to send patches to zbar to help it decode QR codes with binary data in them because it was passing the data through iconv and mangling the output. I also had to add options to the zbar tools to make them decode exactly one QR code I just wanted to print out 4096 bit RSA"},{"id":38852408,"author":"askiiart","month":"2024-01","story":"Building a self-contained game in C# under 2 kilobytes","stance":"reference","why":"Game in a QR code limited to 2,953 bytes","quote":"there's Can you fit a whole game into a QR code? by MattKC, which is limited to about 3 kilobytes (2,953 bytes)","text":"In a similar vein, there's Can you fit a whole game into a QR code? by MattKC, which is limited to about 3 kilobytes (2,953 bytes) [VIDEO]"},{"id":31154457,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the most stable form of digital storage?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of video games stored in QR codes","quote":"You can, for example, store video games in a QR code","text":"Paper. It's proven technology: written records last hundreds of years. You can easily encode arbitrary data as 8 bit mode QR codes and print them out. This gives you machine readability and error correction. Data density is not that good but it's very effective, especially for small amounts of data. I wrote some binary decoding patches for ZBar for this exact use case. You can, for example, store video games in a QR code: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24287479,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2020-08","story":"US indictments of piracy group members throw top-tier piracy world into chaos","stance":"reference","why":"Game in a QR code built on commenter's patch","quote":"If so, then the patch he submitted builds upon my work!","text":"Video games in QR codes? That's interesting. Do you have the link to the video? Is it this one? [VIDEO] [LINK] If so, then the patch he submitted builds upon my work! He fixed a line feed conversion bug that was present in Windows. I use Linux so I did not experience this issue. I remember seeing that patch get merged but I didn't know about the whole video game context. This is amazing. I wrote the patch because I was trying to print a 4096 bit RSA secret key as a QR code. Awesome to see it getting used for something even more awesome. Here are the links to my contributions and related pages: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] ZBar used to automatically convert binary data to text when decoding 8 bit QR codes, mangling the data in the process. My patch adds the option to disable that, enabling the data to be extracted without corruption. I also added the one shot feature to the ZBar tools to make it easier to use in automated scripts that expect exactly one output: [LINK] Looks like we have a real world application for tiny ELFs now! [LINK]"}],"r":[["WXuK6gekU1Y",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1]]},"UN8bJb8biZU":{"wy":"HN recommends it whenever legal threats against critics or open-source authors come up, often as the best accessible primer on SLAPP suits.","q":{"x":"In my opinion, it's the best one they've ever done with an absolutely magnificent ending","i":26904112,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26904112,"author":"input_sh","month":"2021-04","story":"EFF sues Proctorio on behalf of student falsely DMCA'd","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best Oliver episode on SLAPP suits with a great ending","quote":"In my opinion, it's the best one they've ever done with an absolutely magnificent ending","text":"SLAPP suits (acronym for Strategic Lawsuits Against Public Participation) are designed to shut up critics, plain and simple. They're not designed to be won, they're designed to be an annoyance to those that are being sued, dragged on as much as possible, and incur as many legal fees for the defendant as possible. The end goal is simply for other people's self-censorship to kick in. As in, when other people want to criticise Proctorio, there's a chance they're gonna stumble upon this lawsuit and decide against speaking up. Highly recommend this John Oliver video. In my opinion, it's the best one they've ever done with an absolutely magnificent ending: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24656899,"author":"notpiika","month":"2020-10","story":"Bob Murray, Who Fought Regulations as a Coal Operator, Files for Lung Benefits","stance":"rec","why":"Last Week Tonight coverage of Murray","quote":"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight episodes on Murray are great watches.","text":"If you haven't already seen them, John Oliver's Last Week Tonight episodes on Murray are great watches. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27428089,"author":"munk-a","month":"2021-06","story":"Replit used legal threats to kill my open-source project","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver on his lawsuit from Murray Energy","quote":"John Oliver is actually a really good source for this sort of point","text":"John Oliver is actually a really good source for this sort of point as he dedicated an episode[1] to the trials and tribulations of his lawsuit against Murray Energy - something he described as a SLAPP suit which I think is a fair viewpoint. The replit case is especially interesting since it could be a lot more personal (because the dev behind it previously worked for them and people hold grudges) and also because it might actually be a lot more valid then a lot of other frivolous C&Ds we see (since they were a former employee and it would be impossible to claim they were unaware of how replit worked at a very basic level). I think this lawsuit is a lot riskier than most and replit certainly does have a leg to stand on in open court. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24898220,"author":"luc4sdreyer","month":"2020-10","story":"Coal baron Robert Murray has died","stance":"reference","why":"Follow-up episode after Murray's lawsuit","quote":"","text":"And after he was sued by Robert Murray for the above episode, the follow up episode [1] won an Emmy award [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["aw6RsUhw1Q8",1]]},"ewsGmhAjjjI":{"wy":"HN points to it as a vivid first-person description of aphantasia and an example of an aphantasic artist.","q":{"x":"this video is fantastic for describing the feeling of aphantasia","i":20268525,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20268525,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2019-06","story":"Aphantasia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic depiction of the feeling of aphantasia","quote":"this video is fantastic for describing the feeling of aphantasia","text":"A little slow, but this video is fantastic for describing the feeling of aphantasia and it's sort of doubly fascinating that the incredibly talented artist who made it suffers from it (as I'm the flip side, great at visualizing and horrid at creation) [VIDEO]"},{"id":18805716,"author":"cybersol","month":"2019-01","story":"The blind mind: No sensory visual imagery in aphantasia","stance":"rec","why":"Describes aphantasia and includes a test","quote":"Here is another description of it, and a test in the first part","text":"So I have aphantasia, and I first realized when talking to someone about mental images and memories. They have photorealistic pictures in their mind, or even video scenes like a movie. At my best I have something like an expressionist painting with words and concepts arranged spatially on blobs of color representing the object. More typically, just blackness. Here is another description of it, and a test in the first part: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37804347,"author":"frabcus","month":"2023-10","story":"My Brain Doesn’t Picture Things","stance":"reference","why":"Artist with aphantasia explains her drawing workflow","quote":"There are some professional artists who are aphantasic, e.g. this YouTube video talks about it","text":"It's hard! There are some professional artists who are aphantasic, e.g. this YouTube video talks about it [VIDEO] IIRC, the artist does lots of sketches, and looks at them to see what looks right. And also uses computer models to get angles of what she's drawing which she struggles with otherwise."}],"r":[["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["ooOL4T-BAg0",1],["2AecAXinars",1],["fwCz8Ris79g",1],["U_X5uR7VC4M",1],["HmL2l-bcuUQ",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["o4MwTvtyrUQ",1]]},"iIpPuJ_r8Xg":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear overview of military logistics, often as a contrast with civilian infrastructure efforts.","q":{"x":"Wendover Productions has a great video about the insane amount of logistics","i":34957420,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34957420,"author":"rtkwe","month":"2023-02","story":"Consultants, The real reason it costs so much to build new subways in America","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insane scale of logistics so US never waits on materiel","quote":"Wendover Productions has a great video about the insane amount of logistics","text":"The US could cut back and still compete in the West Pacific/South China see but it would have to focus there very heavily to the detriment of the existing implied obligations to NATO. Part of the US's politically dominant position post WW2 is that we've signed ourselves up to be anywhere in the world fighting a large scale conflict inside of a month with huge stockpiles of everything you need to fight all over the world, including one floating one that can backup the standard land based prepositioned stocks. [0] Granted a lot of that was also enabled by being the only major economy not significantly bombed through the war but that advantage was heavily invested in since then. [0] Wendover Productions has a great video about the insane amount of logistics dedicated to the goal of never having to wait for materiel practically anywhere in the world. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33476977,"author":"rsj_hn","month":"2022-11","story":"The U.S. Air Force Just Admitted the F-35 Stealth Fighter Has Failed","stance":"rec","why":"Nice summary of US military logistics and global prepositioning","quote":"A nice summary of US military logistics is here","text":"I'm really worried that cheap, abundant and reliable is not something our defense industry is capable of. - As a result of deindustrialization, we can't ramp up production of critical components. We make up for that by prepositioning supply depots around the world, and these depots allow a brigade or so to fight for a few weeks. But once that runs out, things get hairy. See this article: [LINK] which talks about \"surge\" capability after a four year ramp up time and also here: [LINK] - We have little sea lift capacity to move gear around to the hot spot even if we were able to produce it in large quantities. [LINK] see also [LINK] A nice summary of US military logistics is here: [VIDEO] - US gear has a reputation of being expensive to maintain and breaking down easily, even though it is generally best in class. - We critically rely on air dominance Basically we've optimized our military for global police actions by pre-positioning small amounts of troops and supplies around the world, rather than maintaining an industrial base with a large amount of slack and the ability to quickly ramp up production to support a sustained conflict. At some point an adversary is going to call our bluff with a major sustained conflict - and sufficient AA defenses to deny us air dominance. Then people will realize how hollowed out our defense industry has become, and they'll look at the huge amount we spend on defense and how little we are getting in return for that investment."},{"id":44040957,"author":"theshrike79","month":"2025-05","story":"Finland announces migration of its rail network to international gauge","stance":"reference","why":"Shows bonkers scale of US military logistics","quote":"Compare this to the completely bonkers logistics of the US Military","text":"Compare this to the completely bonkers logistics of the US Military: [VIDEO] Even Unicef has a massive logistics center in Denmark with pallets of stuff categorised and ready to be sent for any emergency: [LINK]"}],"r":[["Gksxu-yeWcU",1],["AdKNCBrkZQ4",1],["g3Ud6mHdhlQ",1],["hpAMbpQ8J7g",1]]},"oFtjKbXKqbg":{"wy":"Recommended in solo-developer threads for its insights on shipping small projects with simple stacks and AI tools.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend checking out this podcast","i":41550056,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41550056,"author":"nmrtn","month":"2024-09","story":"Ideas to make money as solo dev","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insights on solo developers leveraging AI tools for bigger projects.","quote":"I highly recommend checking out this podcast","text":"I highly recommend checking out this podcast with Peter Levels : [VIDEO] With the rise of AI and automation tools, solo developers now have more 'brain' and 'production power' at their disposal, allowing them to tackle more complex projects without needing a big team. It might give you some insights into how to approach your project and how to leverage AI tools to enhance your development process. Definitely worth a listen!"},{"id":43063729,"author":"jll29","month":"2025-02","story":"My Life in Weeks","stance":"rec","why":"Long interview featuring life-in-weeks spreadsheet reminding you not to waste time.","quote":"after initial annoyance I'm glad I watched it all.","text":"In this Lex Fiedman video, they talk about how life is short, and a visualization as a spreadsheet is shown that makes one very concerned: Pieter Levels: Programming, Viral AI Startups, and Digital Nomad Life | Lex Fridman Podcast #440 [VIDEO] [ironically, the podcast is very long - over 3 hours - speaking of not wasting time, but after initial annoyance I'm glad I watched it all.] The positive insight that can come out of that is use every week and don't waste it, and and if you can, move your long-term dreams closer to the here and now. I made a version of that spreadsheet for \"the rest of my life\" to hand it into my office as a reminder. (Even if I will live until 90, it fits my laptop screen without scrolling...)"},{"id":46701443,"author":"nomilk","month":"2026-01","story":"OpenAI is rolling out age prediction","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This could have the unintended consequence of encouraging under-agers to ask more 'adult' questions in order to try to trick it into thinking they're an adult. Analogous to the city that wanted to get rid of rats, so offered a bounty for every dead rat, and to the surprise of nobody except policy makers, the city ended up with more rats, not less. (lesson: they thought they were incentivising less rats, but unintentionally incentivised more) The padding in OpenAI's statement is easy to see through: > The model looks at a combination of behavioral and account-level signals, including how long an account has existed, typical times of day when someone is active, usage patterns over time, and a user’s stated age. (the only real signal here is 'usage patterns' - AKA the content of conversations - the other variables are obfuscation to soften the idea that OpenAI will be pouring over users' private conversations to figure out if they're over/under age.). Worth also noting 'neutered' AI models tend to be less useful. Example: Older Stable Diffusion models were preferred over newer, neutered models: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41383247,"author":"mlamina","month":"2024-08","story":"Show HN: I made a background noise generator for coders","stance":"reference","why":"Pieter Levels discusses what he listens to while programming.","quote":"talking about what they listen to while programming","text":"I got inspired by a conversation between Pieter Levels and Lex Friedman ( [VIDEO] ), talking about what they listen to while programming. Let me know what you think!"},{"id":41399474,"author":"napoleoncomplex","month":"2024-08","story":"Marketing to Engineers (2001)","stance":"reference","why":"Levels explains sticking to PHP and jQuery and framework hype as marketing.","quote":"roughly 2 minutes of relevant answer","text":"Very much agreed on a lot of the points there, and on that note, how new frameworks market to developers is probably a great lesson in that. Pieter Levels (of nomadlist.com and similar fame) recently talked about it on a podcast, how he basically sticks to PHP and jQuery, and how often he sees developers jumping on a new framework, not realizing it's likely a marketing tactic that's pulling them in. Time-stamped to that part of the podcast, roughly 2 minutes of relevant answer: [VIDEO] The part that feels most like the advice above: \"And same thing what happens with nutrition and fitness or something, same thing happens in developing. They pay this influencer to promote this stuff, use it, make stuff with it, make demo products with it, and then a lot of people are like, “Wow, use this.” And I started noticing this, because when I would ship my stuff, people would ask me, “What are you using?” I would say, “Just PHP, jQuery. Why does it matter?” And people would start attacking me like, “Why are you not using this new technology, this new framework, this new thing?”"}]},"21EiKfQYZXc":{"wy":"Recommended as a broad, practical view of AI's near-term impact from a leading practitioner, with his argument that worrying about evil AI is premature.","q":{"x":"I really enjoyed this Andrew Ng talk","i":13930332,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14878462,"author":"abhgh","month":"2017-07","story":"Thoughts on OpenAI, reinforcement learning, and killer robots","stance":"rec","why":"Ng argues worrying about evil AI is premature","quote":"Nice talk all around if you can find the time.","text":"> It is hard for me to empathize with Musk’s fixation on evil super-intelligent AGI killer robots in a very distant future. This reminded me of a talk by Andrew Ng I watched recently [1]: \"Worrying about evil AI robots today is a little bit like worrying about overpopulation on the planet Mars ... how do you not care ... and my answer is we haven't landed on the planet yet so I don't know how to work productively on that problem...of course, doing research on anti-evil AI is a positive thing but I do see that there is a massive misallocation of resources.\" Nice talk all around if you can find the time. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13930332,"author":"melling","month":"2017-03","story":"Opening a new chapter of my work in AI","stance":"rec","why":"Ng talk on evil AI, radiologists, basic income, trolley problem","quote":"I really enjoyed this Andrew Ng talk","text":"I really enjoyed this Andrew Ng talk: [VIDEO] Some excerpts: Anti-Evil AI drives funding: [VIDEO] Radiologist will be impacted: [VIDEO] Against Basic Income: [VIDEO] Trolley Problem (who dies): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19106163,"author":"jasode","month":"2019-02","story":"Should I Machine-Learn? (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Context for Ng's 1-second heuristic","quote":"deep link at 12m20s for context and 14m05s is the \"1 sec heuristic\"","text":">Andrew Ng once tweeted about a 1-second heuristic [..] And was summarily derided for it. Read the replies to his tweet to see some of the suggested counter examples. Fyi to add some extra context to that \"1 sec heuristic\"... I've seen several Andrew Ng presentations over the last several years and in the live talks, he has more of a lead up to that \"1 second\" idea[0]. He was trying to help project managers at Baidu think about the possibilities of new AI products to build. Unfortunately, the extreme brevity of that tweet removes all that surrounding context that so it makes him look like an AI crackpot instead of an AI realist. (E.g. The top tweet reply from Pedro Domingos seems to be based on the limitations of current AI but Andrew Ng was trying to convey the idea to PMs of what new AI to build.) [0] deep link at 12m20s for context and 14m05s is the \"1 sec heuristic\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":15030188,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2017-08","story":"Andrew Ng is raising a $150M AI Fund","stance":"reference","why":"Ng's broad views in a Stanford lecture","quote":"he gave a lecture on his broad views in a Stanford lecture","text":"not sure if there's a transcript anywhere but he gave a lecture on his broad views in a Stanford lecture [VIDEO]"},{"id":13561185,"author":"melling","month":"2017-02","story":"Andrew Ng: Artificial Intelligence Is the New Electricity","stance":"reference","why":"Excerpt on evil AI worries from Ng's talk","quote":"","text":"The problem with worrying about the \"Evil AI\": [VIDEO] Radiologist will be impacted: [VIDEO] Against Basic Income: [VIDEO] Trolley Problem (who dies): [VIDEO]"},{"id":13669276,"author":"melling","month":"2017-02","story":"Robots Rule at Swiss Factories as Strong Franc and Wages Bite","stance":"reference","why":"Ng on basic income and re-education","quote":"Andrew Ng discusses \"basic income\" where people need to learn","text":"Why should humans work in factories doing mindless jobs? We need to prepare people to re-educate themselves as technology advances. Andrew Ng discusses \"basic income\" where people need to learn: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OyFJWRnt_AY",1],["Wp-WiNXH6hI",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["0QNiZfSsPc0",1],["pqzcCfUglws",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"8rS3fTbC7TE":{"wy":"HN users cite it as the explanation of the tricks around the IPO and the index-inclusion rule changes.","q":{"x":"Informative and entertaining.","i":47605658,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47605658,"author":"superjan","month":"2026-04","story":"SpaceX Files to Go Public","stance":"rec","why":"Informative and entertaining discussion of the SpaceX IPO.","quote":"Informative and entertaining.","text":"Patrick Boyle (fund manager, professor, youtuber) recently discussed this IPO on his channel. Informative and entertaining. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47389233,"author":"johnbarron","month":"2026-03","story":"SpaceX IPO Scandal","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the tricks around the SpaceX IPO.","quote":"At 25:48 you have the explanation for the (multiple) tricks","text":"At 25:48 you have the explanation for the (multiple) tricks: [VIDEO] This Musk guy makes Ponzi look like the Pope.You almost have to admire it the way you admire a raccoon breaking into a triple locked trash can at 2 AM. You are furious. You know you should be furious. But part of you is at the window wondering \"how did he do that?\". As you will see below, this raccoon is getting the trash can manufacturer to remove the lock for him first. This is how retail investors are about to get played by the SpaceX IPO: First they only release 5% to 10% to create an artificially inflated price. Its called the low float strategy... Also...the Nasdaq 100 inclusion is supposed to be earned. You list, trade for up to a year at least, prove you are stable and then maybe you might be selected for inclusion. That rule protects the millions of people whose retirement money is in index funds. But Musk told Nasdaq \"fast-track me or I list on NYSE... so the Nasdaq invented a \"Fast Entry\" rule out of thin air....15 trading days and you are in. They openly admitted it was designed for SpaceX. S&P is now considering the same thing for the S&P 500, which has around $24 trillion in assets tracking it. Why does this matter? The second SpaceX hits these indexes, every passive fund is forced to buy, your 401k, your Vanguard fund, your target date fund. All buying SpaceX at whatever inflated price it opens at, with zero public track record. Nobody asks you. With the index inclusion and the implication of "},{"id":48385292,"author":"tcp_handshaker","month":"2026-06","story":"Anthopic, OpenAI Should Not Be Allowed to IPO, Says Ed Zitron [video]","stance":"reference","why":"One of several SpaceX IPO critiques listed.","quote":"SpaceX IPO Scandal\" -","text":"[LINK] \"SpaceX IPO retail offering is worrying\" - [LINK] ( This guy will never be invited back to CNBC...interview is hysterically funny...and accurate... ) \"SpaceX IPO: Nice Try Though\" - [LINK] \"SpaceX IPO Scandal\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":48385458,"author":"tcp_handshaker","month":"2026-06","story":"Elon Musk is expected to become the first trillionaire","stance":"reference","why":"One of several SpaceX IPO critiques listed.","quote":"SpaceX IPO Scandal\" -","text":"[LINK] \"SpaceX IPO retail offering is worrying\" - [LINK] ( This guy will never be invited back to CNBC...interview is hysterically funny...and accurate... ) \"SpaceX IPO: Nice Try Though\" - [LINK] \"SpaceX IPO Scandal\" - [VIDEO] \"Anthopic, OpenAI Should Not Be Allowed to IPO, Says Ed Zitron\" - [LINK]"},{"id":48412538,"author":"tcp_handshaker","month":"2026-06","story":"SpaceX, Other Mega IPOs Denied Fast Index Entry by S&P","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among sources on the SpaceX IPO scandal.","quote":"SpaceX IPO Scandal\" -","text":"At this moment, there is so so much, publicly available information [1] on the fact the SpaceX IPO is the biggest scandal in the long history of Wallstreet insiders fleecing the \"Johns\". A scandal orchestrated and cheered on by the NASDAQ, as well as Goldman Sachs and JP Morgan the underwriters, that if you spend any money on it, you deserve to be parted with your money. And if you have a 401k...you are forced to buy no questions asked. This will become such a disaster for retail, that hopefully Goldman Sachs and JP Morgan and the NASDAQ too, will spend their next 10 years in court defending action group lawsuits. [1] - [LINK] \"SpaceX IPO retail offering is worrying\" - [LINK] \"SpaceX IPO: Nice Try Though\" - [LINK] \"SpaceX IPO Scandal\" - [VIDEO] \"Anthopic, OpenAI Should Not Be Allowed to IPO, Says Ed Zitron\" - [LINK]"},{"id":48430375,"author":"tim333","month":"2026-06","story":"S&P 500 rejects SpaceX, also blocking entry for OpenAI and Anthropic","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses rule change for SpaceX index entry.","quote":"There's a Patrick Boyle vid talking about it","text":"SpaceX going in the index is fine but I think changing the rule to make it different from other stocks is iffy and was at the request of Musk. There's a Patrick Boyle vid talking about it [VIDEO]"},{"id":47624165,"author":"frm88","month":"2026-04","story":"A $1.75T IPO Would Be Overpaying 30% for SpaceX","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of index exceptions and consequences.","quote":"Patrick Boyle made a video analysing this and pointing to consequences","text":"The S&P500 will make an exception, too. Patrick Boyle made a video analysing this and pointing to consequences [VIDEO]"},{"id":47648247,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2026-04","story":"SpaceX and OpenAI: The Mega IPO Grift [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Related Boyle IPO content cited.","quote":"Also his video \"Investing in Initial Public Offerings (IPOs)\"","text":"Also his video \"Investing in Initial Public Offerings (IPOs)\": > If you can get in on the ground floor of an IPO, that is getting an allocation in the initial share offering before the stock starts trading, there is evidence that you are likely to make a profit; IPOs tend to be underpriced. While this might seem like an obvious way to easy profits, there are a few crucially important things for you to consider. * [LINK] And perhaps the episode of the podcast he co-hosts on IPOs: > We’ve previously compared IPOs to lotteries that are prone to inflated valuations and low returns. Today we welcome “Mr. IPO,” Professor Jay Ritter onto the show for a deeper dive into IPO performance, for his insights into SPACs, and to hear his research into why economic growth doesn’t correlate with stock returns. Early in the episode, Jay unpacks how long-term IPO returns perform against first-day trading. While exploring the role that venture capital plays in tech IPOs, Jay talks about why negative earnings don’t affect tech IPOs in the short-term before sharing how skewness factors tend to impact young companies. Reflecting on how IPOs are usually underpriced, Jay discusses how the interests of companies are not aligned with the interests of IPO underwriters. After looking into IPO allocation, Jay compares the 2020 ‘hot IPO market’ with the internet bubble of the late 90s. Later, we ask Jay about what special-purpose acquisition companies (SPACs) are and why they’ve exploded in recent years. H"},{"id":47393212,"author":"tartoran","month":"2026-03","story":"Nasdaq's Shame","stance":"reference","why":"Patrick Boyle's video on the topic.","quote":"Obligatory video from Patrick Boyle","text":"Obligatory video from Patrick Boyle [VIDEO] Edit: someone posted it on HN, there's already a thread for it : [LINK]"}],"r":[["c1UBJbfR-H0",1]]},"9dKiLclupUM":{"wy":"HN cites it when arguing that different risks follow different distributions and should be judged by growth rates and tails rather than current counts.","q":{"x":"Watch this to understand that we're talking about two different distributions","i":15468346,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":26076541,"author":"nmca","month":"2021-02","story":"Covid-19 and the political economy of mass hysteria [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Taleb on comparing risks by growth rates","quote":"I think Taleb has the best summary of this, for all his flaws","text":"No it isn't, in this case. I think Taleb has the best summary of this, for all his flaws: [VIDEO] In short, risk due to coconuts vs sharks is a valid comparison because we have good priors about growth rates, and they are similar. Coconuts vs covid is a terrible, terrible comparison because again we have good priors on growth but they are mind-bogglingly different functions of time."},{"id":15468346,"author":"s3nnyy","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: UK Startups – Are You Thinking of Leaving the UK Because of Brexit?","stance":"rec","why":"Taleb on why terrorism and ladder falls follow different distributions","quote":"Watch this to understand that we're talking about two different distributions","text":"I will give an alternative explanation why people fear terrorism: Falling from a ladder also kills more people than terrorism, right, but this is not what people are afraid of. Watch this to understand that we're talking about two different distributions: [VIDEO] Terrorism SUDDENLY increases the odds of you being killed by it. And that is what - rightfully - scares people. Example: Before 9/11 the chances of being killed by terrorism was let's say 0.000000.....1%. After 9/11 the chance SUDDENLY increased a hundredfold. That change in probability never ever happened in the distributions of car-accidents, being shot in the US or falling ladders."},{"id":28460535,"author":"nmca","month":"2021-09","story":"20 years after 9/11: Will we ever stop taking our shoes off at airports?","stance":"reference","why":"Taleb's point on forecasted growth rate versus current rate","quote":"I think Taleb has a point on this topic","text":"I think Taleb has a point on this topic - the concern is about a forecasted growth rate, not about the current rate. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16763439,"author":"21","month":"2018-04","story":"Tesla crash in September showed similarities to fatal Mountain View accident","stance":"reference","why":"Taleb argues Ebola and terrorism are a different risk class","quote":"would say that you are the one which is being very bad at reasoning with probabilities","text":"Funny, Nassim Taleb, a worldwide (if controversial) risk probability expert, would say that you are the one which is being very bad at reasoning with probabilities, because you are putting Ebola and terrorism in the same class of risk as falling off a ladder: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["r0plv_nIzsQ",1],["lqJUIqZNzP8",1],["gcS04BI2sbk",1]]},"CGRtyxEpoGg":{"wy":"Commenters cite it to explain expert blindness and the difficulty of solving problems from inside your own cultural context.","q":{"x":"explains a general problem (that of trying to solve problems that are more prevalent elsewhere in the world, from within your own cultural context)","i":41910707,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42991272,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-02","story":"Obscure islands I find interesting","stance":"rec","why":"Explains OLPC failure; whole video relevant.","quote":"OLPC is discussed from ~9 minutes in, but the whole thing is relevant.","text":"It was this line of thinking which lead to the One Laptop Per Child failure. See \"Why do Western Designs fail in Developing Countries\" - [VIDEO] OLPC is discussed from ~9 minutes in, but the whole thing is relevant. OLPC started when Seymour Papert and Nicholas Negroponte went to Senegal to teach people to code, and found that there was no good reason to learn to code if you live in rural Senegal and people were not interested. They decided they knew better and doubled down, making the OLPC project, and when presenting their laptop to the world the African delegation at the conference objected to spending money on laptops instead of infrastructure, schools, water. Negroponte decided the target users didn't know better than him what they needed, so he ignored them. \"One of the things people told me is, look Nicholas you can't just give a kid a laptop and walk away. Yes you can\". he said. And he did. And almost none of the kids did any coding on the laptops. [LINK]"},{"id":41910707,"author":"zahlman","month":"2024-10","story":"Egypt declared malaria-free after 100-year effort","stance":"rec","why":"Explains solving problems across cultural contexts, with malaria nets.","quote":"explains a general problem (that of trying to solve problems that are more prevalent elsewhere in the world, from within your own cultural context)","text":">If you have enough funding, Malaria is preventable. It requires more than funding to solve the problem. Sorry that my source is a YouTube video, but [VIDEO] explains a general problem (that of trying to solve problems that are more prevalent elsewhere in the world, from within your own cultural context) and gives malaria as an example. People in malaria-afflicted countries, given free insecticide-treated nets, will often try to use them for fishing - not caring about the effect the insecticide will have on the haul. It's not due to ignorance or a lack of understanding, but due to a value judgment: people who have lived with malaria for generations don't see it as being as big of a problem, while poor people (on a global scale - not like in the US where \"the poor\" can afford some really impressive things) are always concerned with food supply."},{"id":41085304,"author":"xk3","month":"2024-07","story":"In the Beginning Was the Command Line (1999)","stance":"reference","why":"Expert blindness in design.","quote":"This is \"expert blindness\".","text":"> On the command line, the context is obvious. You are in the context While I share the opinion that the command line is _the one true way_ of computing, I don't think this is really all that true. Computers are alien. GUIs are alien. CLIs are alien. Everything is learned. Everything is experience. Everything is culture. Learning, experience, and culture blind us from the experience of the novice. This is \"expert blindness\". Why Western Designs Fail in Developing Countries [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"CPLdltN7wgE":{"wy":"Recommended as the author's own quick intro to lazygit, a TUI that makes git workflows more natural.","q":{"x":"There is a nice video from the author that introduces a lot of its features.","i":33638392,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47331757,"author":"haskman","month":"2026-03","story":"Rebasing in Magit","stance":"rec","why":"Lazygit TUI that works standalone and embeds well in vim.","quote":"it's a TUI program that's usable independently, that also happens to embed really well within vim.","text":"Vim/Neovim users should look at LazyGIT. [VIDEO] The best part - it's a TUI program that's usable independently, that also happens to embed really well within vim."},{"id":33614731,"author":"kobalsky","month":"2022-11","story":"Sapling: A new source control system with Git-compatible client","stance":"rec","why":"Lazygit stages individual lines among other features.","quote":"which allows you to stage individual lines among other features.","text":"> git add -p allows me to add chunks easily and create commits your workflow resembles mine so I'm in the obligation of mentioning [LINK] which allows you to stage individual lines among other features. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33638392,"author":"peterfication","month":"2022-11","story":"Sapling: A new source control system with Git-compatible client","stance":"rec","why":"Author's intro video covering many lazygit features.","quote":"There is a nice video from the author that introduces a lot of its features.","text":"Using lazygit has improved my usage of Git so much! There is a nice video from the author that introduces a lot of its features. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41895936,"author":"rakoo","month":"2024-10","story":"Jujutsu (jj), a Git compatible VCS","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates more natural workflows on top of git.","quote":"lazygit has shown it is possible to have more natural workflows on top of git","text":"If anything, lazygit has shown it is possible to have more natural workflows on top of git as demonstrated by [VIDEO] , so it is indeed possible"}]},"XopSDJq6w8E":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in threads on China's housing glut and construction quality as visual evidence of poor, empty new buildings.","q":{"x":"This video from last year was pretty eye opening to me.","i":20466721,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20466721,"author":"ajflores1604","month":"2019-07","story":"China’s State-Driven Growth Model Is Running Out of Gas","stance":"rec","why":"Uninhabited new ghost cities crumbling.","quote":"This video from last year was pretty eye opening to me.","text":"This video from last year was pretty eye opening to me. Shows uninhabited \"new\" ghost cities completely crumbling. There's no underlying value to the property bubble going on over there and the scale seems mind blowing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17686937,"author":"ballenf","month":"2018-08","story":"Always-on Alibaba office app fuels backlash among Chinese workers","stance":"rec","why":"Covers new construction problems in China.","quote":"ADV China youtube channel has covered this with some great video of new construction problems in China","text":"ADV China youtube channel has covered this with some great video of new construction problems in China (not implying the problem is only there): [VIDEO]"},{"id":34661785,"author":"onlypositive","month":"2023-02","story":"Scalability Is Overrated","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A large number of them aren't and they're crumbling apart after only a couple of years being built. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28496861,"author":"jollybean","month":"2021-09","story":"Societies that treat women badly are poorer and less stable","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm sorry that you're not aware of the reality of total lack of quality in many products and services in China, which is partly due to a function of lower costing, but also due to a general lack of industrial focus on quality, and particularly a lack of standards. And then gaslight others for pointing out the facts ... I work for a company that sells an electrical device, which has considerable standards applied in Europe and North America, and basically none in China. The competitive products to ours in China are essentially dangerous. They will start fires. We manufacture in China, and depending on the manufacturer, can usually make no assumptions about quality, other than it will be the lowest possible unless there is considerable oversight. Unbelievable, 'shocking' hacks and cutting corners, which we view as a matter of quality, they view as a matter of cost reduction. With the bigger factories that do business with big established brands, that's less of a problem because they're consistently building for markets with higher standards and requirements. For some thoughts on Food Safety [1][2] In China, in the year 2000, only 50% of the population had 'basic sanitation' services i.e. toilets. That's changed a lot, but there are still wide swaths of population literally without basic sanitation. The number is effectively 100% in almost every other semi-developed country. [3]. Even in 2015, 5% of the country still does not have access to basic water. [4] Chinese construction "},{"id":28597021,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2021-09","story":"China Evergrande contagion concerns rile global markets","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Bit of a hot take. While there are some buildings in the US where substandard construction are going to catch up to the building and the owners, shoddy construction is endemic in China (or buildings are left unfinished and eventually need to be retired before completion). [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":25282660,"author":"justaman","month":"2020-12","story":"How the CCP Does Job Promotions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here is video of a Chinese ghost city. It was made by a bloggers that were forced to leave the country for fear of their lives. [VIDEO] > At every level, there is total abdication of responsibility. The whole system incentivizes this. Exactly like the USSR and Chernobyl."},{"id":19991580,"author":"pas","month":"2019-05","story":"Study Pinpoints Source of Banned Gas That Saps Ozone Layer: Eastern China","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"State socialism/communism. Everything is provided, decided, controlled, influenced, reviewed, rethought by and via the state (in some representation, the party, the government, the local government, some group or other, an official, someone with connections, the police, or even just a faceless automated system/website/machine). This reminds people that everything is temporary, effort is futile, etc. This makes people simply not care much about common stuff. (Environment, roads, streets, houses, etc.) Source, I'm from a post-soviet country, and we're dealing with huge swaths of the population who are still very much thinking like this. See also: - [LINK] (why everything is falling apart) - [VIDEO] (what about new buildings, quality, maintenance?) - [LINK] (about powerlessness, apathy)"},{"id":19638207,"author":"gthtjtkt","month":"2019-04","story":"‘China’s Manhattan’ Borrowed Heavily, but the People Have Yet to Arrive","stance":"reference","why":"Recent example of ghost towns and poor building quality.","quote":"","text":"Yeah, I watch a few YouTube channels of Americans in China and the ghost towns are still everywhere. Their buildings in general also seems to be of extremely poor quality, but it's one of those topics that's socially unacceptable to discuss. Here's a recent example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19653372,"author":"reaperducer","month":"2019-04","story":"Construction has kept rent in Chongqing, China to $75 a month","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Chinese buildings deteriorating after three years.","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure why you're being downvoted. The construction quality in mainland China is terrible. I've witnessed a lot of it, myself, but this video was posted on HN a couple of days ago: [VIDEO] As bad as that is, it only scratches the surface because they didn't actually go inside any of the buildings. It would be unimaginable for a building outside of the Chinese bubble to literally start collapsing upon itself after standing on its own for only three years."},{"id":19172010,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2019-02","story":"China's housing glut casts pall over the economy","stance":"reference","why":"Shows 20-story concrete buildings collapsing within a decade.","quote":"","text":"These are concrete structures, 20 stories tall, that are collapsing in less than a decade. This isn't a case of lack of maintenance, but poor construction trying to goose GDP. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18937707,"author":"FooHentai","month":"2019-01","story":"A Fifth of China’s Homes Are Empty (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Follow-up showing new Chinese buildings deteriorating.","quote":"tl;dr yes.","text":"\"Immediately above us towered what could have been the headquarters of a bank — forty floors of office space, wrapped in a shell of mirrored panels. In its un-maintained state however, these reflective scales were falling away in great swathes, to expose the bare concrete beneath. Not even finished yet, and already it needed a makeover.\" [LINK] \"the farmers that own the land here get the cheapest materials, and make these pop up out of nowhere\": [LINK] Follow-up video \"Are new chinese buildings really falling down?\": [VIDEO] tl;dr yes. Note at 2:30 \"We saw this place being built three years ago\"."}],"r":[["-6rWqJhDv7M",1],["4KuVvb9DTaU",1]]},"ZV-AFnCkRLY":{"wy":"HN holds it up as a vivid example of focused game-jam productivity, and it has been used to demystify programming for students and inspire tools for recording coding sessions.","q":{"x":"show them Notch's time-lapse in the background on projector","i":8834389,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":8834389,"author":"wallflower","month":"2015-01","story":"Ask HN: I'm going to present technology jobs to 400 16yo,what should I tell them?","stance":"rec","why":"Time-lapse of making a game to demystify creation for students.","quote":"show them Notch's time-lapse in the background on projector","text":"First, congratulations! I would demystify the process of creation. Show them your wireframes, your pencil sketches, talk about the business v. creative push and pull. Make it interactive - break them into groups of 2-3 each for 5 min and show them Notch's time-lapse in the background on projector while you have them work on exercises in ideation - what would their game be? what would the levels, characters, and challenges be. They don't have to present - just get them comfortable with the first step of creation - putting it on 'scratch' paper. If you have time, deconstruct Flappy Bird. The scrolling left to right background v. making the bird move. How the object detection is greatly simplified when you have only one coordinate to detect (y-bird and y-pipeTop/y-pipeBottom). How adding gravity to the bird (downward velocity) allows you to fly the bird. \"Making Metagun\" by Notch [VIDEO] \"Creating Prelude of the Chambered\" by Notch [LINK]"},{"id":8224577,"author":"chops","month":"2014-08","story":"Notch programming a Doom-like in Dart","stance":"rec","why":"Notch's Ludum Dare timelapse inspired tooling for rendering coding sessions as videos.","quote":"was inspiration for a series of scripts I now use for clients","text":"Notch's Ludum Dare video for Metagun ( [VIDEO] ) was inspiration for a series of scripts I now use for clients and my own entertainment purposes (for some loose definition of \"entertainment\"). It's a series of shell scripts I use that screencap videos of coding and set them to music (so, for example, rendering a 48-hour coding competition to a 5-minute song, or as I more typically do for clients, render the development process down to a few minutes for them to watch in fast-time how their development was done). It's called watchmecode ( [LINK] ) and I just have to do ./make-av-video.sh /path/to/video.mpg /path/to/song.mp3 And it does the rest, and outputs it to \"done.mp4\" The result is something that looks like this: [LINK] (disclaimer, I've plugged this before on HN: [LINK] )"},{"id":3611235,"author":"thebigshane","month":"2012-02","story":"Hackathons are nonsense","stance":"reference","why":"Notch live-coding a game for Ludum Dare.","quote":"Watching `notch` live-code is also very popular.","text":"Watching `notch` live-code is also very popular. [1]: [VIDEO] Making Metagun [2]: [LINK] Making MiniCraft [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":1783016,"author":"hjalle","month":"2010-10","story":"Productivity Tips for Programmers","stance":"reference","why":"Notch time-lapse of a 48-hour game jam showing total dedication.","quote":"There you got productivity by Notch","text":"So basically, premature optimization sucks. Don't search for a solution of a problem that doesn't exist. If you spend lots of time writing the same code: optimize that. If you dont do that, dont optimize it. I think lots of people belive that its some kind of magic that made Sergey, Larry and all the other successful programmers successful. [VIDEO] <-- There you got productivity by Notch (Minecraft creator for those who dont know). Its like three seconds of twitter, 30 minutes for food and some sleep, for a total of 48h. Actually that makes me take back the idea of the magical thing, since the magic seems to be complete dedication to the task you are supposed to do."}],"r":[["4oAB83Z1ydE",1],["ybcvlxivscw",1],["X2wLP0izeJE",1],["KcfFJ6pNEZk",1]]},"b1NTaVQPt1E":{"wy":"HN recommends it for specifics on how Truffle and Graal achieve strong dynamic-language performance.","q":{"x":"Chris Seaton gave this talk recently about JRuby+Truffle and some specifics about how it optimises so well compared to other Ruby implementations.","i":12123037,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13088161,"author":"BenoitP","month":"2016-12","story":"The Ruby+OMR JIT","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Truffle+Graal giving very good Ruby results.","quote":"I hear the Truffle+Graal combination is getting very good results about Ruby.","text":"I hear the Truffle+Graal combination is getting very good results about Ruby. Here is video about it (pointed to the missing-method example): [1] They're hitting 35x in some benchmarks [2]. Most benefits being in workloads where Ruby has to continuously talk to C; the result-passing part gets compiled away. It is available in Java 9, if I'm not mistaken. LLVM IR bytecode ingestion is in preparations too [3]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":12123037,"author":"matthewrudy","month":"2016-07","story":"Graal and Truffle could accelerate programming language design","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how JRuby+Truffle optimises so well compared to other Rubies.","quote":"Chris Seaton gave this talk recently about JRuby+Truffle and some specifics about how it optimises so well compared to other Ruby implementations.","text":"Chris Seaton gave this talk recently about JRuby+Truffle and some specifics about how it optimises so well compared to other Ruby implementations. [VIDEO] Previous discussion [LINK]"},{"id":19539461,"author":"chrisseaton","month":"2019-04","story":"PHP 8 to Add a JIT","stance":"reference","why":"The author's own lecture on optimising dynamic languages.","quote":"I gave a lecture about this","text":"> I understand that a JIT engine cant do this, due to speed. Most serious JIT engines do! For example V8, LuaJIT, Graal, C2. > But would be surprised that a LLVM compiler with a custom-designed heuristic for specific optimizations cant manage this. Rubinius for example tried writing custom optimisations like you're describing and didn't get very far. LLVM is not designed for these kind of optimisations - it understands memory reads and writes, not high-level semantic information about arrays and objects. In practice, it just doesn't seem to work. As I say I think it's a bare minimum for making a dynamic language genuinely fast. I gave a lecture about this [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["wjZofJX0v4M",1],["60z_hpEAtD8",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1]]},"bLk1cjZ4ll0":{"wy":"Recommended as a passionate take on good appliance design and cited in toaster threads.","q":{"x":"This one about Sunbeam toasters from 50+ years ago is particularly great","i":31109515,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36945973,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-07","story":"A fridge from 70 years ago has better features than the fridge I own now","stance":"rec","why":"Passionate take on good toaster design","quote":"a guy who is ranting/passionate about technology","text":"One of the YouTube channels that I watch from time to time is a guy who is ranting/passionate about technology. The Antique Toaster that's Better than Yours - [LINK] and How to design an actually good toaster with lessons from the 1940's - [VIDEO] It's not a fridge... but similar design thoughts. There is a recent video about a fridge... This goofy fridge has a really clever design. It's also kinda terrible. - [LINK]"},{"id":31109515,"author":"BeefWellington","month":"2022-04","story":"Companies are buying washing machines to take the chips out and repurpose them","stance":"rec","why":"Sunbeam toaster design from 50 years ago","quote":"This one about Sunbeam toasters from 50+ years ago is particularly great","text":"Indeed, and in some cases the experiences have gotten worse. There's a Youtube channel named Technology Connections and he has several videos on this topic that I recommend. This one about Sunbeam toasters from 50+ years ago is particularly great: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39015133,"author":"evanjrowley","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: Should I try to manufacture toasters?","stance":"reference","why":"One of several toaster design videos by the reviewer","quote":"","text":"Try doing a Kickstarter. Over the years, I've spent way more money than I should have on Kickstarter projects that are highly specific and non-refundable. Both of these characteristics will likely be important to leverage as you are starting out this venture. Kickstarter targeted advertising through Facebook and Instagram seem to be very good at getting your product in front of people willing to buy it - even if it's an expensive American-made toaster. Once you have a prototype, you may want to consider getting a review by Technology Connections. He's done multiple on toaster design novelty: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39108140,"author":"em-bee","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: Should I try to manufacture toasters?","stance":"reference","why":"Dropped as relevant to the toaster manufacturing question","quote":"","text":"i am just going to drop this here: How to design an actually good toaster with lessons from the 1940's From Technology Connections: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1]]},"coGpmA4saEk":{"wy":"HN brings it up in discussions of originality, invention, copying and attribution, including AI art.","q":{"x":"You will enjoy this documentary (Everything a Remix).","i":10532931,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10532931,"author":"icegreentea","month":"2015-11","story":"StarCraft: The past, present and future","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on how creative work copies and remixes","quote":"You will enjoy this documentary (Everything a Remix).","text":"You will enjoy this documentary (Everything a Remix). [VIDEO] Hardly 'scientific'... but how do you quantify copying."},{"id":6285919,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2013-08","story":"Researcher controls colleague’s motions in first human brain-to-brain interface","stance":"rec","why":"New inventions come from combining existing ideas","quote":"If you have some time to kill, I would check out this video called","text":"> doesn't seem to have broken any new ground, only mashed together two existing technologies I'd argue that new inventions are created by mashing existing technologies, often emerging or bleeding edge, and creating something new. Henry Ford didn't invent the combustion engine, transmission, suspension, etc, but he did mash these things together into something new. What we are seeing in the video and research is something new and pretty exciting if you take it a couple steps further. Think about replaying these signals, building a play book, etc. ps. If you have some time to kill, I would check out this video called, \"Everything Is A Remix\", which talks about this idea @ [VIDEO]"},{"id":42893268,"author":"zxvkhkxvdvbdxz","month":"2025-01","story":"GenAI Art Is the Least Imaginative Use of AI Imaginable","stance":"reference","why":"Everything is a remix of prior expression","quote":"one could argue that everything is a remix of pre-existing expressions.","text":"Indeed, one could argue that everything is a remix of pre-existing expressions. This, of course does not rhyme well with current copyright laws. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8959384,"author":"visakanv","month":"2015-01","story":"Everyone’s stealing jokes online. Why doesn’t anyone care?","stance":"reference","why":"Remix culture and attribution of creative work","quote":"","text":"Yep, you're right. As a writer, marketer, creator and consumer of content, I enjoy thinking about things like this. I've witnessed my work being plagiarized and edited before. I personally accept it as just the way reality is– some people are lazy about attribution, and other people like feeling talented and special when they diliberately share others' content without attribution or remixing. The answer to \"Why doesn't anyone care\"- it all boils down to incentive structures. \"Parody accounts\" can steal with impunity. \"Casual randos\" can steal with impunity. - If you're an artist, and faceless, nameless folks plagiarize your work, going after them actually makes you look insecure or clueless. (Metallica and Napster come to mind.) - If you get plagiarized by someone Bigger, Badder and More Powerful, then that's a news story in itself- you're the underdog, and everybody loves to root for the underdog. The best response to plagiarism, in my opinion, is to make more art. Some artists get very offended by this- they feel that they are entitled to all the attribution to all their work forever and ever. And that would be a beautiful world, I guess, and maybe discussions like these help to educate people. I also can imagine a world where- when you make a joke that's been made before (online), or you post an image that's been posted before, a sort of reverse Google / reverse image search will reveal to readers where the origin of the remix is. For anybody who is curious about this sort"}],"r":[["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["0qKp-0h9P18",1],["F9cO3-MLHOM",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["2qv3fcXW1mg",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1]]},"eDJrQBwJpqk":{"wy":"HN points to it as a companion to Don't Talk to the Police for knowing your rights during stops.","q":{"x":"This video is also useful,","i":1371193,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":2959287,"author":"username123","month":"2011-09","story":"Police Assisted Apple Investigators in Search of SF Man's Home","stance":"rec","why":"Know your rights when police ask to search","quote":"KNOW your rights.","text":"My take: KNOW your rights. Nothing stinks here. As one of the videos below states: If police is entitled to a search then they will just break in and do a search. If they ASK for consent, and even more, if they threaten, they KNOW that they are not entitled. Keep the door shut and don't given up your rights. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":1371193,"author":"dotBen","month":"2010-05","story":"Don't Talk to the Cops","stance":"rec","why":"Useful advice on handling a police stop","quote":"This video is also useful,","text":"I've seen these two videos a long time ago and they are GREAT advice. It's actually scary the kind of stuff the cop says he has done and other cops will do to you during questioning. In the UK, where I'm originally from, the police are not allowed to lie or ask you to do anything outside of your rights and so it is total wake up call for anyone who is not used to the way the police work here in the US. This video is also useful, [VIDEO] However, a friend who I talked to about this did make a counter perspective... that sometimes it is worth talking to the cops in the right circumstances. For example, if you are pulled over cos you just ran a stop sign and you have nothing illegal on you/in the car/etc then it is better just to talk to the cop and get the ticket dealt with. If you start playing the 'not going to say anything' and 'no you can't look in the car' then you are opening yourself up for a long painful experience and potentially arrest if they decide to fuck back with you and they can pin some suspicion for something on you (eg you are driving through an area that happens to be associated with drug sale, you have a LeatherMan tool you and the cop can say the knife was being carried for other intent, etc) Cops are naturally authoritarian and can choose to make your life hell if they think you are trying to be superior or above them."},{"id":15828676,"author":"tomohawk","month":"2017-12","story":"How police used a traffic stop to take $91,800 from an innocent man","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how to behave during a traffic stop","quote":"When pulled over, never answer questions. You don't have to.","text":"The best way to avoid civil asset forfeiture in a traffic stop is to just not ever answer any nosy questions the officer may ask. When pulled over, never answer questions. You don't have to. If the police officer asks you to remain once they've written the ticket, ask if you're being detained or if you're free to go. Once they've written the ticket, the stop is over, but aggressive police will try to fool people into thinking the stop is not over. The longer they can keep you there, the more pressure they can apply. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-FENubmZGj8",1]]},"ett_8wLJ87U":{"wy":"Recommended as a gripping account of scientific fraud and the history of cloning research.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend watching this video [1].","i":37731342,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38828013,"author":"mbaye","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: What are the YouTube channel worth to follow in 2024?","stance":"rec","why":"Documentaries about daring scientific fraud.","quote":"Documentaries about daring scientific fraud","text":"Bobby Broccoli: [LINK] Documentaries about daring scientific fraud such as \"The man who tried to fake an element\" [LINK] or \"The man who faked human cloning\" [VIDEO] Or the epic tale of political intrigue in \"The $21,000,000,000 hole in Texas\" [LINK] about the The Superconducting Super Collider that never was."},{"id":37731342,"author":"xmprt","month":"2023-10","story":"Don't worry about global population collapse","stance":"rec","why":"History of human cloning research.","quote":"I'd recommend watching this video [1].","text":"Wars don't just appear out of thin air. There were a lot of incentives for countries to engage in war. Cloning doesn't really benefit anyone and doing cloning requires a ton of funding and grants and ethics boards reviews. If you're interested in the history of human cloning (and where that field of research is trending towards), I'd recommend watching this video [1]. Full human cloning is somewhat pointless however the research has lead to innovations in stem cell research which does have real benefits for things like organ transplants. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36765231,"author":"ouraf","month":"2023-07","story":"Johnson and Johnson sues researchers who linked talc to cancer","stance":"reference","why":"Case of cloning fraud in South Korea.","quote":"There's also a case related with cloning (which includes stem cell research) and massive fraud in South Korea 20 or so years ago.","text":"It is as bad in medicine, if not worse. Recently a very influential paper about alzheimer was pulled[1] because of almost everything you said, but for biology instead: data fabrication, overestimating numbers, etc. There's also a case related with cloning (which includes stem cell research) and massive fraud in South Korea 20 or so years ago.[2] It's a complicated matter: research is a beacon of light on top of a mountain of failed experiments. And not even the government will fund a mountain of failures if your shining discovery at the end can't bring returns to society (or shareholders) in greater value than what you took. And the pharmaceutical industry never settled for small results. And, unlike ML, it's much harder to test things as an outsider (you can hate the hype, but openAI, hugging face and all these \"open\" language models makes it much easier to learn, test, tweak and improve things without a master's degree in data engineering.). It's a grimy area to research even as a pastime. There's a lot of recorded practices both in research and production that makes you question how low can a person go for money 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36917369,"author":"evrimoztamur","month":"2023-07","story":"Argonne National Lab is attempting to replicate LK-99","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on Hwang Woo-suk's faked cloning.","quote":"the next BobbyBroccoli special like the one on Hwang Woo-suk, the man who faked human cloning","text":"...or the next BobbyBroccoli special like the one on Hwang Woo-suk, the man who faked human cloning ( [VIDEO] ). Not claiming the researchers of LK99 are intentionally faking their results, by the way!"}],"r":[["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["4hZxo96x48A",1],["qTBAW-Eh0tM",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["lvTqbM5Dq4Q",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1]]},"fjs2gPa5sD0":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on jobs, automation and hiring when arguing about IQ and employability.","q":{"x":"Jordan Peterson has a very good video on this[1].","i":14241266,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18161385,"author":"collyw","month":"2018-10","story":"Gig-Economy Workers Are the Modern Proletariat","stance":"rec","why":"Clip on IQ and job satisfaction.","quote":"There is an interesting clip from Jordan Peterson on IQ and jobs.","text":"There is an interesting clip from Jordan Peterson on IQ and jobs. Sure intelligent people want something meaningful from their jobs, but at the lower ends people are probably happier with something less challenging. [VIDEO] Personally I wouldn't mind cycling around all day and staying fit, but keeping software running pays a fair bit better."},{"id":14241266,"author":"carsongross","month":"2017-05","story":"Why manufacturing jobs aren't coming back to the U.S","stance":"rec","why":"Peterson on jobs for people with lower IQ.","quote":"Jordan Peterson has a very good video on this[1].","text":"I agree entirely with you on the urgency of figuring out productive jobs for people with IQs on the left side of the bell curve. Jordan Peterson has a very good video on this[1]. I would couple this with a concern that we end (or, dare we dream, reverse) the dysgenic social policies we currently have in place (IQ is 50-80% heritable). In the short run, we need to work to move the production chain back to the united states, and that will generate some manufacturing jobs. I suspect more than the corporate-interest controlled media will say, but it almost doesn't matter if we are playing the long game for our kids. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30424614,"author":"oreally","month":"2022-02","story":"Misidentifying talent","stance":"reference","why":"Psychometric research on job performance for hiring.","quote":"IQ and The Job Market","text":"Well hiring for software engineers should involve fields outside their own while de-emphasizing leetcode. For example, there are psychometric studies comparing personality and job success. Some of JP's lectures talk on this point quite a bit. Workplace Performance, Politics & Faulty Myers-Briggs: [LINK] IQ and The Job Market: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18161425,"author":"choot","month":"2018-10","story":"Gig-Economy Workers Are the Modern Proletariat","stance":"reference","why":"Relationship between job expectations and IQ.","quote":"See this video for relationship between job expectation and IQ","text":"The reason they give is based on self evaluation. People are particularly bad at self evaluation. Keep in mind, most people don't know what their IQ is. And most don't consider themselves to posses less intelligence as they don't know what more intelligence feels like. See this video for relationship between job expectation and IQ: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16147688,"author":"xiphias","month":"2018-01","story":"Stanford CS9: Problem-Solving for the CS Technical Interview","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that employers prize intelligence over experience.","quote":"Google never cared about experience and knowledge as much as intelligence (the ability to learn fast)","text":"Google interviews are mostly IQ tests. There are a basic set of primitives (algorithms and data structures) and you have to think fast and combine them. Google never cared about experience and knowledge as much as intelligence (the ability to learn fast): [VIDEO]"},{"id":16031047,"author":"quantumofmalice","month":"2017-12","story":"Unstacking the deck","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson discusses balanced economy for lower-IQ workers.","quote":"Jordan Peterson discusses this problem in this video","text":"This is a decent article on a true problem, but there are two things I would mention that add context to the situation: Genetics do matter. Adult intelligence appears to be somewhere between 50 and 80% determined by genetics. Thus we should expect over time in a meritocracy for smart kids to come from the same families. This is compounded by assortative mating by IQ, which has become far more widespread in the last forty years. So things are going to look nepotistic (and they always will be to some extent, see my second point below) when, in fact, this is a natural phenomena. So, what? Just let the people who lose the genetic lottery rot? No. Instead, we might want to focus on having a more balanced economy where, rather than everyone striving to become part of the elite, a working class person can have a perfectly reasonable life. Jordan Peterson discusses this problem in this video: [VIDEO] Secondly, it is not immoral for parents to make large investments in their own children's lives. This behavior is no doubt somewhat driven by genetics (nearly everything appears to be at least 40% genetic) but it is also a learned behavior. Government programs that replace parental investment have been catastrophic for minorities in the united states: for example 73% of non-hispanic blacks are born out of wedlock. So delegating parental investment in children to the state, in the name of ending nepotism, may have some very detrimental effects that are difficult to predict. All of this is"},{"id":14867399,"author":"pcnonpc","month":"2017-07","story":"China Is Engineering Genius Babies (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on IQ and employability.","quote":"","text":"To flesh out a large range of implications will take at least an effort worthy of a PhD dissertation. With successful embryo selection, at least we can keep up with the increasingly higher minimum IQ needed to function well in advanced society and avoid a large underclass of unemployable people. Jordan Peterson - IQ and The Job Market [VIDEO]"},{"id":14871906,"author":"pcnonpc","month":"2017-07","story":"First Human Embryos Edited in U.S","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on IQ and employment.","quote":"","text":"Modern advanced society already requires a high minimum intelligence for people to function in especially to hold a respectable job or be a respectable member of a community. The minimum is increasing when more automation is used for most kinds of automation. If someone's children will likely have a lower than this minimum intelligence, which you can infer from parent's IQ and other measures, will you encourage or prohibit them to use either embryo selection or genetic engineering? What are the impact on the child's life for each option? Is the 'distaste' more important than giving a better opportunity for the newborns? Also, what can you do? You cannot put regulations on China or other countries. On this issue, they will not agree on international agreement either. And there will be rich people who fly to other countries to have the procedures done. This will cause even more inequality than allowing it domestically. Jordan Peterson IQ and Job Market [VIDEO]"},{"id":14172700,"author":"carsongross","month":"2017-04","story":"America is regressing into a developing nation for most people","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson on IQ's importance in modern job market.","quote":"Jordan Peterson has discussed how important IQ is in the modern economy","text":"Yep. Jordan Peterson has discussed how important IQ is in the modern economy and how both the left and the right are in denial about it. While the left thinks anyone can be anything, the right thinks lazy people just need to work harder. Both are wrong. [VIDEO] Difficult problem."}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["vmgxtcbc4iU",1],["LPjzfGChGlE",1]]},"wK5v1Nw98xs":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a detailed critical counterpoint to Helion's claims, for those who want more than the hype.","q":{"x":"explains that 95% recovery efficiency is not the whole story","i":34590586,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37679644,"author":"nabakin","month":"2023-09","story":"Nucor and Helion to Develop 500 MW Fusion Power Plant by 2030","stance":"rec","why":"Additional resources on Helion debates.","quote":"Additional resources for people who want to read or watch more on the subject","text":"Additional resources for people who want to read or watch more on the subject: [VIDEO] [LINK] /u/ValAslanyan is Improbable Matter and he gets into some interesting debates about Helion [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":34590586,"author":"fpoling","month":"2023-01","story":"When Will Fusion Energy Light Our Homes?","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed critique explaining Helion recovery efficiency is not the whole story.","quote":"explains that 95% recovery efficiency is not the whole story","text":"The new video by the same guy who created the original criticism [VIDEO] starting from 2:03:00 explains that 95% recovery efficiency is not the whole story."},{"id":35901481,"author":"jnsaff2","month":"2023-05","story":"Helion’s fusion power purchase agreement with Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Q&A mentioned is likely this [0]. Do you have any hints which section would cover the upsides? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34590718,"author":"fpoling","month":"2023-01","story":"When Will Fusion Energy Light Our Homes?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"describes how Helion will get a lot of D+T neutrons from the secondary reactions","text":"The new video by the same guy [VIDEO] from 32 min describes how Helion will get a lot of D+T neutrons from the secondary reactions despite all their efforts. Even without that the neutron flux from D+D reaction by Helion own admission will be enough to do a lot of damage within days of operation based on experience with slower fission neutrons."}],"r":[["aKLntZcp27M",1]]},"ypn3Fe_PLts":{"wy":"Linked whenever Yellowstone or caldera unrest comes up, as the debunking of civilization-ending eruption fears.","q":{"x":"See this video for more information","i":35705588,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37683858,"author":"sparcpile","month":"2023-09","story":"What's Going on at Italy's Campi Flegrei Caldera?","stance":"rec","why":"Compares Yellowstone caldera uplift to Campi Flegrei.","quote":"There is a video by Soup Emporium on Youtube that compares the uplift of the Yellowstone caldera to Campi Flegrei's.","text":"There is a video by Soup Emporium on Youtube that compares the uplift of the Yellowstone caldera to Campi Flegrei's. The video is queued up to where Campi Flegrei is discussed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35704695,"author":"sickofparadox","month":"2023-04","story":"Could we stop Yellowstone from erupting with a giant geothermal power plant?","stance":"rec","why":"Volcanologists are not worried about Yellowstone erupting soon.","quote":"Volcanologists are not even worried about Yellowstone erupting anytime soon","text":"Firstly, Law of Headlines - the answer is no. But secondly, Volcanologists are not even worried about Yellowstone erupting anytime soon: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35705588,"author":"coltonv","month":"2023-04","story":"Could we stop Yellowstone from erupting with a giant geothermal power plant?","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the supervolcano doom myth.","quote":"See this video for more information","text":"Just so everyone knows, the whole \"Yellowstone is a supervolcano that could erupt any minute and cause cataclysmic effects on the planet\" is just not true. It was made up for TV \"documentaries\". Yellowstone does not pose any real threat to society at large. Volcanologists are practically unanimous in that belief. See this video for more information: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41225330,"author":"_uhtu","month":"2024-08","story":"Paris Agreement thresholds crossed","stance":"reference","why":"No real prospect of a Yellowstone event.","quote":"There is no real prospect of a \"Yellowstone event\"","text":"There is no real prospect of a \"Yellowstone event\": [VIDEO] We could effectively geoengineer one by pumping things into the upper atmosphere ourselves, but there's a lot of questions about that and we have no idea if this would help more than it hurt."},{"id":41057444,"author":"_uhtu","month":"2024-07","story":"Hydrothermal explosion at Yellowstone National Park","stance":"reference","why":"Yellowstone will not end civilization.","quote":"Yellowstone will not explode and end civilization","text":"Yellowstone will not explode and end civilization (or American civilization for that matter) in many many lifetimes: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cfSIC8jwbQs",1]]},"OO18F4aKGzQ":{"wy":"Shared in surveillance and civil-liberties threads as a powerful rap critique of the US.","q":{"x":"if you think the US is screwed up, you have to listen to this: it's gold!","i":6435559,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6435559,"author":"contingencies","month":"2013-09","story":"Metadata Equals Surveillance","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Powerful rap critique of the US","quote":"if you think the US is screwed up, you have to listen to this: it's gold!","text":"Welcome to the United Snakes Land of the thief home of the slave Grand imperial guard where the dollar is sacred and proud ... The Cold Continent latch key child Ran away one day and started acting foul King of where the wild things are daddy's proud cos the Roman Empire done passed it down Imported and tortured a work force and never healed the wounds or shook the curse off Now the grown up Goliath nation Holdin' open auditions for the part of David ... Only approved questions get answered Now stand your ass up for that national anthem - Brother Ali, Uncle Sam Goddamn ... I usually never listen to rap, but if you think the US is screwed up, you have to listen to this: it's gold! [VIDEO]"},{"id":22555458,"author":"Crosseye_Jack","month":"2020-03","story":"Chelsea Manning Tries to Kill Herself in Jail, Lawyers Say","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’ve always preferred Brother Ali’s wording. “ Welcome to the United Snakes Land of the Thief , Home of the Slave Grand imperial guard where the dollar is sacred and proud.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":8301204,"author":"bobandbob","month":"2014-09","story":"Are You a Terrorist?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as relevant to San Francisco engineers","quote":"I find this extremely relevant to software engineers practicing in the fairy city of San Francisco, CA","text":"I find this extremely relevant to software engineers practicing in the fairy city of San Francisco, CA => [VIDEO] We make obscene amounts of cash in comparison the rest of the country and for what - to contribute more to murdering the everliving fuck out of everyone."}],"r":[["ISAKc9gpGjw",1],["XDM3MqHln8U",1],["8Z685OF-PS8",1]]},"PGqCeSjNuTY":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible, interesting talk on molecular dynamics and simulation-based drug discovery.","q":{"x":"There is brilliant video by the hedge fund manager DE Shaw about molecular dynamics simulation. Its very accessible and I found it very interesting","i":44239475,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44239475,"author":"max_","month":"2025-06","story":"A Primer on Molecular Dynamics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accessible, interesting talk by DE Shaw on molecular dynamics simulation.","quote":"There is brilliant video by the hedge fund manager DE Shaw about molecular dynamics simulation. Its very accessible and I found it very interesting","text":"There is brilliant video by the hedge fund manager DE Shaw about molecular dynamics simulation. Its very accessible and I found it very interesting — [VIDEO]"},{"id":39017938,"author":"max_","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: What's a promising area to work on in 2024 -ideally not crowded already?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of drug discovery via molecular dynamics simulation.","quote":"Drug discovery via Molecular Dynamics Simulations [2]","text":"> What do you mean by fundamental AI research? Coming up with new/original models that solve the same exact problems that people are trying to solve with neural networks (computer vision, language translation, image generation etc) hopefully that are better performing & cheaper computationally. > What kind of computational model? I think answering that question would be what the researcher in this field is trying todo. I think a place to start would be exploring abandoned AI strategies like Genetics Algorithms & Markov Logic >What are some practical applications of complex systems? - Modeling Financial Markets [1] - Drug discovery via Molecular Dynamics Simulations [2] - Urban Planning [3] - Modeling Epidemics [4] - Cosmology - Computer Graphics - Much more .... [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] If interested, subscribe to these podcasts. 1: Simplifying Complexity: [LINK] 2: Complexity: [LINK]"},{"id":25260594,"author":"conformist","month":"2020-11","story":"David Shaw vs. Seymour Papert Debate (1995) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"A recent academic talk by Shaw on his bio-informatics research.","quote":"There are also some more recent academic talks of his on youtube, mostly focused on his bio-informatics research.","text":"There are also some more recent academic talks of his on youtube, mostly focused on his bio-informatics research. E.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7059764,"author":"cma","month":"2014-01","story":"AMD launches Kaveri processors aimed at starting a computing revolution","stance":"reference","why":"Visual example of a computational microscope showing how a drug finds its binding site.","quote":"here's a visual example of using a computational microscope to understand how drug actually works and finds its binding site","text":"I'm not giving you what you want, but here's a visual example of using a computational microscope to understand how drug actually works and finds its binding site: [VIDEO] If you can't see a difference between that and say, X-Ray crystallography then well.."}],"r":[["bxe2T-V8XRs",1],["cKxRvEZd3Mw",1]]},"kN0SVBCJqLs":{"wy":"HN commenters use it to compare against later Apple launches, to show how low-key the debut was, and as an example of a great product speech.","q":{"x":"his announcement of the ipod was an incredible speech","i":12640664,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12640664,"author":"hans","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Incredible speech tying together tech, art, music and Apple","quote":"his announcement of the ipod was an incredible speech","text":"o u said steve jobs: really his announcement of the ipod was an incredible speech .. it ties together tech+art+music+apple and his vision appears pretty fresh. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30021927,"author":"Someone","month":"2022-01","story":"The Windows 11 taskbar is an annoying step backward","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs says music before mp3, showing Apple positioned the iPod as a music player","quote":"count how often Steve says music before he says mp3.","text":"> There's a circus about them that permits them to stomp out history And how do you think they would do that? Apple sometimes uses a bit of hyperbole to describe itself ( [LINK] ), but I don’t think Apple ever claimed \"Apple invented the mouse\" or they had \"the first mp3 player\" or \"the first smartphone\". I also have never met anybody who made such claims, only people saying there are people who make such claims. It also would be weird for them to do so. Their marketing differentiates their products from the competition, and has been doing that for years. The competition sells MP3 players, the iPod was a _music_player_ or just the iPod (look at [VIDEO] , and count how often Steve says music before he says mp3. [1]); the iPhone an iPod with touch controls, a revolutionary mobile phone, and a breakthrough internet communications device ( [LINK] ), the iPad just the iPad. [1] There’s a claim in hat video that Apple invented FireWire. I think that is mostly correct. They were the driving force there."},{"id":28528740,"author":"danaris","month":"2021-09","story":"Apple unveils new iPad mini with breakthrough performance in new design","stance":"reference","why":"Citation for the iPod naming lineage","quote":"","text":"This has been the case since 2007 with \"iPhone\"...which mimicked the naming for \"iPod\" since 2001 [0]...which mimicked the naming of \"iMac\" since 1998 [1]. All of these, of course, go back to Steve Jobs' introduction of \"Macintosh\" in 1984 [2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13921063,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2017-03","story":"Apple Store currently down","stance":"reference","why":"Apple was floundering at the iPod launch; the room was small","quote":"It's remarkable how small the room was.","text":"Apple was still floundering when they inro'd the iPod. [VIDEO] It's remarkable how small the room was. But I don't think it was small due to exclusivity."},{"id":5158061,"author":"tolmasky","month":"2013-02","story":"Wozniak: Facts in Steve Jobs movie look 'atrocious'","stance":"reference","why":"Low-key iPod announcement, not an obvious hit at the time","quote":"the iPod announcement was pretty low key","text":"Seems strange to leave out the iPhone product announcement, especially since the iPod announcement was pretty low key ( [VIDEO] ), and was not an obvious hit at the event. Compare that to the iPhone announcement where everyone went wild right then and there, and truly felt like the culmination of Steve's vision of what computing should become (the iPod in retrospect seemed more like a side project that didn't really \"mesh\" with his general philosophy on computing)."},{"id":4877488,"author":"brudgers","month":"2012-12","story":"Stop building apps no one wants","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs introducing the iPod, mentioning trust in the Apple brand","quote":"People trust the Apple brand...iMac, iBook, iPod, \" - Steve Jobs introduces the iPod at the 2001 Music Event.","text":"\"People trust the Apple brand...iMac, iBook, iPod, \" - Steve Jobs introduces the iPod at the 2001 Music Event. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1081735,"author":"shmichael","month":"2010-01","story":"Apple’s iPad Just a Big iPod Touch","stance":"reference","why":"Original iPod launch for comparison with the iPad launch","quote":"Compare the iPad launch video from today with the original iPod launch","text":"The iPod wasn't another MP3 player. It was the MP3 player, featuring the most songs in the least amount of space. For mobile devices, this makes a big difference. Compare the iPad launch video from today with the original iPod launch: [VIDEO] The iPhone was the first phone capable enough and attractive enough for third-party applications. It came two years ahead of competition. The iPad comes after the Kindle, and alongside plans from Microsoft and others to launch their own tablets."}]},"75Ju0eM5T2c":{"wy":"HN quotes it as a source for Knuth's caution against believing something is good just because it is trendy.","q":{"x":"Donald Knuth interviews are so interesting","i":31483485,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31483485,"author":"belter","month":"2022-05","story":"Donald Knuth on work habits, problem solving, and happiness (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Little piece of advice from Knuth's interview playlist.","quote":"Donald Knuth interviews are so interesting","text":"Donald Knuth interviews are so interesting, but would like to particularly highlight this little piece of advice, out of this great playlist: \"Donald Knuth - My advice to young people\": [VIDEO] Complete Playlist - \"Donald Knuth (Computer scientist)\" [97 videos]: [LINK] Also the \"Oral History of Donald Knuth\" from the Computer History Museum is great. \"Oral History of Donald Knuth Part 1\": [LINK] \"Oral History of Donald Knuth Part 2\": [LINK] Plus.. \"Donald Knuth Interview 2006\": [LINK] \"An Interview with Donald Knuth\": [LINK] \"Interview with Donald Knuth\": [LINK] This somewhat \"colourful\" page also tracks a few: [LINK] PS: The story that he told Steve Jobs he was \"Full of shit\" is not true. \"Donald Knuth never told Steve Jobs that he was full of shit\" [LINK]"},{"id":45954875,"author":"sfpotter","month":"2025-11","story":"The Pragmatic Programmer: 20th Anniversary Edition (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Another take on getting good at something.","quote":"Here's another take","text":"Fair! Personally, I'm not sure how you can get good at something if you aren't at least a little in love with it. Here's another take: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9789081,"author":"iamcreasy","month":"2015-06","story":"The Curse of the Pixar Universe","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth's advice not to believe something is good because it is trendy.","quote":"Source","text":"Excellent. It's just like what Donald Knuth said, \"Don't believe something is good just because it's trendy. I'd probably go the other extreme way. If I find too many people adopting a certain idea, I'd probably think its wrong.\" Source : [VIDEO] I've always find those questions that are generally regarded as stupid party amusing. Because I think those questions showing the difference in out thought process, and instead of stigmatizing it there are valuable lessons to be learned here."},{"id":9347598,"author":"soveran","month":"2015-04","story":"On software popularity","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth says to be wary when too many people adopt an idea.","quote":"I took it from this video","text":"An extended version of the quote would be \"Don't just believe that because something is trendy, that it's good. I would go the other extreme where if I find too many people adopting a certain idea I'd probably think it's wrong. Or if my work had become too popular I probably would think I have to change.\" I took it from this video: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Wp7GAKLSGnI",1],["ghtpJnrdgbo",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["QpAhX-gsHMs",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["hMtu7vV_HmY",1],["cbSu2PXOTOc",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1]]},"9nF0K2nJ7N8":{"wy":"HN uses it as a widely seen, fairly balanced real-world appraisal of FSD, both for its impressive moments and for its manual takeovers.","q":{"x":"this MKBHD video from a few days ago was a fair appraisal of the current state of FSD","i":34117251,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34117251,"author":"twoodfin","month":"2022-12","story":"California passes law banning Tesla from calling software FSD","stance":"rec","why":"Fair appraisal of the current state of FSD","quote":"this MKBHD video from a few days ago was a fair appraisal of the current state of FSD","text":"If you don’t have a friend, I thought this MKBHD video from a few days ago was a fair appraisal of the current state of FSD: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36181535,"author":"MangoCoffee","month":"2023-06","story":"The death of self-driving cars is greatly exaggerated","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter impressed by Tesla FSD demo","quote":"marques brownlee FSD demo","text":"I'm impressed by this comparison between Tesla FSD vs Waymo. whether Tesla is level 2 or 4 autonomous. I'm very impressed with Tesla's FSD. edit: Tesla FSD reach the destination first by taking the high way while Waymo go the local route and far longer to reach the same destination. [LINK] marques brownlee FSD demo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35530534,"author":"memish","month":"2023-04","story":"Elizabeth Holmes loses bid to stay out of prison","stance":"reference","why":"Shows FSD driving the reviewer to his studio","quote":"Marques Brownlee has a video from last year where it drove him to his studio.","text":"It demonstrably has FSD capability. Marques Brownlee has a video from last year where it drove him to his studio. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34009015,"author":"priomsrb","month":"2022-12","story":"Who knew the first AI battles would be fought by artists?","stance":"reference","why":"MKBHD took manual control three times in a 20 minute drive","quote":"a famous youtuber having to take manual control 3 times in a 20 min drive to work","text":"I was of the impression that Tesla's self driving is still not fully reliable yet. For example a recent video shows a famous youtuber having to take manual control 3 times in a 20 min drive to work [0]. He mentioned how stressful it was compared to normal driving as well. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RIvL2ONhFBI",1],["suRDUFpsHus",1]]},"GFMyMxMYDNk":{"wy":"Cited as a compact account of how editing shaped the original Star Wars, though one commenter notes a rebuttal video disputes parts of it.","q":{"x":"A good video.","i":21247940,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21247940,"author":"jerf","month":"2019-10","story":"In praise of dissenters: It pays to encourage a variety of opinions","stance":"rec","why":"New material of interest from a film school perspective.","quote":"A good video.","text":"How Star Wars Was Saved In The Edit: [VIDEO] A good video. I believe the content is largely pulled from a book on the topic, but this is definitely a case where video helps, and if you haven't read or even heard of the book (like me), it's new stuff, not just a rehashing of the same old Star Wars stuff, and interesting from a film school point of view, not just a Star Wars point of view. I'll also just echo the grandparent post and say that you can see this pattern in a lot of artists: Heinlein, probably JK Rowling (her later books definitely were getting flabby and she's had no comparable success since the original series), all kinds. Hollywood has an interesting pattern where a director makes a huge hit, and they kind of have a defined structure for letting them make a \"passion project\" before putting them back under some editorial control. The passion projects usually do fairly poorly. I'm not sure what happens if one of them becomes a big hit. That would be interesting to see."},{"id":28593172,"author":"cpcallen","month":"2021-09","story":"Project 4K77","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Lucas's unrestrained tendencies; a rebuttal video disputes its picture.","quote":"I would have agreed with you until I saw _How Star Wars was Saved in the Edit_","text":"I would have agreed with you until I saw _How Star Wars was Saved in the Edit_ ( [VIDEO] ). Maybe George Lucas had _always_ been a teenager with no restraints? EDIT: Of course in the process of looking up the URL for HSWwSitE, I discovered _How \"How Star Wars was saved in the edit\" was saved in the edit (sort of, but not really)_ ( [LINK] ) which is a really rather thorough (if laborious) rebuttal of the overall picture the original video sought to paint, and and quite justifiably takes the original video's creators to task for extremely selective reporting (and in several cases fairly woeful research). But boy does it go on at some length (nearly 2h!). If only someone would save _How \"How Star Wars was saved in the edit\" was saved in the edit (sort of, but not really)_ in the edit."},{"id":26513673,"author":"seanicus","month":"2021-03","story":"John le Carré's crucial, covert collaborator","stance":"reference","why":"An accounting of changes made in the Star Wars edit.","quote":"An accounting of changes made in the edit of Star Wars","text":"These accounts about Marcia are deserved but downplay the contributions of Paul Hirsch and Richard Chew. The three of them absolutely saved Star Wars in the editing room. The lack of Lawrence Kasdan is the reason that every piece of dialogue in the prequels is incredibly awkward (but apparently extremely meme-able). An accounting of changes made in the edit of Star Wars: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18874010,"author":"PetitPrince","month":"2019-01","story":"Recreating the Death Star Trench Run Scene with Lego","stance":"reference","why":"Explains editing changes that added pacing and drama.","quote":"this part was added during the editing of the movie for the sake of pacing and drama","text":">(like the Death Star waiting to orbit Yavin before firing at its moon.) Trivia: this part was added during the editing of the movie for the sake of pacing and drama, see the last part of [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"HM23sjhtk4Q":{"wy":"Cited in self-driving threads as the source of Tesla's vision-only stance, usually to discuss or push back on it.","q":{"x":"I think Elon Musk makes some good points why Lidar","i":24908993,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24908993,"author":"Ecstatify","month":"2020-10","story":"Waymo and Daimler Trucks partner on the development of fully autonomous trucks","stance":"rec","why":"Musk argues cameras beat lidar for autonomous driving","quote":"I think Elon Musk makes some good points why Lidar","text":"I think Elon Musk makes some good points why Lidar isn’t the best choice. [VIDEO] Waymo taxi looks incredible but how can they scale it."},{"id":40440872,"author":"EcommerceFlow","month":"2024-05","story":"On Self-driving, Waymo is playing chess while Tesla plays checkers","stance":"reference","why":"Presents the vision-only strategy of Musk and Karpathy","quote":"Elon and Andrej Karpathy's strategy","text":"Anyone who owns a Tesla and has used the new V12.3 understands why Elon is so bullish about the Robo-Taxi's. He's said they've internally seen a 5x-10x reduction in disengagements on 12.4 (already trained, out soon), and that 12.5 is even better. Their GPU stack by end of the year will also be 8x the size it was earlier this year. Combine all this together, and the \"feedback loop\" with millions of people using FSD, it's looking like the \"vision only\" strategy will work. My question is what happens to all other car markers if it turns out Elon and Andrej Karpathy's strategy ( [VIDEO] ) is the correct one?"},{"id":27813584,"author":"belter","month":"2021-07","story":"Scenarios in which Tesla FSD Beta 9.0 fails","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's anti-LIDAR stance cited in discussion of sensors and situational awareness","quote":"Note that in no any other way I would consider Tesla an example to follow","text":"Its an interesting video but its an edited video. The company website mentions the system was able to negotiate the challenges of the driving conditions, looks like an euphemism... Very few details, no scientific publications I could find on their Asimov system on a quick search, not sure if this is a technological breakthrough or a fine tuning of existing processes and methods. Because if its a fine tuning of current algorithms not sure how long they can push this. They are relying on Lidar (and other sensors) but even as of last year, it seems most teams already realized Lidar would not be the solution for Snow, and were now pushing Ground Penetrating Radar ( sounds expensive...) \"Autonomous Cars Struggle in Snow, but MIT Has a Solution for That\" [LINK] Even Tesla, already realized its not just a sensor problem, solving self-driving needs a higher level algo than can put the different sensors, in context of situational awareness. Note that in no any other way I would consider Tesla an example to follow ;-) And do not think they are any closer to getting a working system. Some of the statements show at least a second level understanding of what is required. Sensors are a means to it. Its about situational awareness but also inference. \"LIDAR is a fool’s errand… and anyone relying on LIDAR is doomed. — Elon Musk\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":25901609,"author":"neillyons","month":"2021-01","story":"Waymo CEO dismisses Tesla self-driving plan: “This is not how it works”","stance":"reference","why":"Musk explains his reasoning on cameras versus LIDAR for autonomy","quote":"he said that the Dragon spacecraft has LIDAR and he spearheaded that effort","text":"Elon talked about LIDAR at the autonomy investor day. Basically he said that the Dragon spacecraft has LIDAR and he spearheaded that effort. He also said that LIDAR and cameras both use the same wavelength, if you are going to add a sensor it makes more sense to add one which is outside of the visible wavelength. Even though I know nothing about driverless cars, the reasoning from Elon makes sense. [VIDEO]"}]},"OmZOZjHjT5E":{"wy":"Cited in passing to support the half-serious argument that Idiocracy's world is a utopia.","m":[{"id":11650036,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-05","story":"Economist Removed from Plane for Algebra","stance":"rec","why":"Better explanation of stupidity combined with ambition.","quote":"A better explanation, that highlights how it isn't just \"stupidity\", but the combination of stupidity and ambition .","text":"A better explanation, that highlights how it isn't just \"stupidity\", but the combination of stupidity and ambition . [VIDEO]"},{"id":18069643,"author":"smegger001","month":"2018-09","story":"Millennials Are Causing the U.S. Divorce Rate to Plummet","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for argument that Idiocracy is a utopia.","quote":"There is even a argument that Idiocracy is a utopia.","text":"I would trade President Dwayne Elizondo Mountain Dew Herbert Camacho for Trump any day. At least Mr Dwayne tried to appoint the smartest people available (the protagonist). There is even a argument that Idiocracy is a utopia. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16447408,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2018-02","story":"Crazy new Swedish bill makes sharing music and TV as bad a “crime” as manslaughter","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that the Idiocracy world is a utopia.","quote":"The world of Idiocracy is a utopia.","text":"The world of Idiocracy is a utopia. They're not only willing but eager to appoint the smartest man in the world to high office. President Camacho steps aside as soon as he realises that there is someone better qualified to be president. The movie shows a world that is deeply dysfunctional, but also devoid of any signs of bigotry, hatred or totalitarianism. There are no death camps, no migrations of starving people, no persecuted minority being scapegoated for the food crisis. We should envy those happy idiots. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"TnzcwTyr6cE":{"wy":"HN links it as a clear explainer of gene drive when mosquito genetic engineering comes up.","q":{"x":"one of my favourite Youtube channels","i":12659929,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12659929,"author":"smnscu","month":"2016-10","story":"We’re trying to stop bad mosquitoes by raising and releasing good ones","stance":"rec","why":"Similar video on using CRISPR to change mosquito genomes.","quote":"one of my favourite Youtube channels","text":"Similar (using crispr to change the mosquitoes' genome) video from Kurzgesagt, one of my favourite Youtube channels: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43470185,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2025-03","story":"Gene drive modified mosquitoes offer new tool for malaria elimination efforts","stance":"reference","why":"Background on gene drive mosquitoes circulating for years.","quote":"The idea of using gene drive on mosquitoes has been circulating for at least 8 years.","text":"The idea of using gene drive on mosquitoes has been circulating for at least 8 years. [1] In that time, something like 4 million people have died of malaria, many millions more have been infected, and it is significantly impacting the economic growth of Africa. What will it take for us to pull the trigger on this? 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31104859,"author":"chasil","month":"2022-04","story":"Results from first US trial of genetically modified mosquitoes","stance":"reference","why":"Explains gene drive as a targeted approach against a species.","quote":"","text":"The introduction of an invasive species can have an overall beneficial effect if carefully evaluated, and there is a lot that can go wrong. [LINK] It appears that a \"gene drive\" was introduced into the Florida Keys in this case, and that is much more targeted and specific to a species that it is intended to destroy. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["FxKtZmQgxrI",1]]},"XgFbqSYdNK4":{"wy":"Cited as an easy-to-understand framing of trusted computing and TPMs as tools that can treat users as threats.","q":{"x":"Here it's put in easy to understand terms","i":7967261,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7967261,"author":"shmerl","month":"2014-06","story":"Netflix Could Be Classified As a 'Cybersecurity Threat' Under New CISPA Rules","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why DRM and trusted computing treat users as threats.","quote":"Here it's put in easy to understand terms","text":"It's really simple. Trust is a mutual relation. I.e. since DRM always views users as potential criminals, it's natural always to view DRM as potential malware (and thus a security threat). Here it's put in easy to understand terms: [VIDEO] Practice proves that point spectacularly, because DRM always has an overreaching unethical nature (since it's an overreaching preemptive policing which uses the logic of presumption of guilt). As the Sony exec voiced the core idea behind DRM: > We will develop technology that transcends the individual user. We will firewall Napster at source - we will block it at your cable company. We will block it at your phone company. We will block it at your ISP. We will firewall it at your PC... These strategies are being aggressively pursued because there is simply too much at stake. (See [LINK] ). I.e. overreaching nature of DRM straight from the DRM proponents mouth. And it applies to all DRM by its very definition."},{"id":35792310,"author":"IYasha","month":"2023-05","story":"AMD's Firmware TPMs Vulnerable to Hardware Attacks, Defeating Disk Encryption","stance":"reference","why":"A decade-old presentation on TPM.","quote":"decade old presentation on TPM","text":"Good? It was never intended to do so. ) Also, made me remember (once again) decade old presentation on TPM: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8033768,"author":"shmerl","month":"2014-07","story":"How to prevent screenshots in a web browser","stance":"reference","why":"Trusted computing is often about DRM, conflicting with security.","quote":"Actual usage of that term is more than often happens to be about DRM","text":"> \"Digital Rights Management\" -- you know, words mean something. They mean what they were designed for. And that is excessive preemptive policing. About \"trusted execution\" you are wrong. Original meaning of trusted execution is exactly about security, not about DRM. Actual usage of that term is more than often happens to be about DRM (which is conflicting with security actually, because DRM can be viewed as a security risk). See [VIDEO] If you constantly mix up security, authentication, trusted execution with DRM, I think you should consider your own level of knowing what you are talking about. I explained everything above."},{"id":32235164,"author":"squiffsquiff","month":"2022-07","story":"The Dangers of Microsoft Pluton","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Not so fantastic for personal devices. Basically this","text":"Sure it's fantastic in a corporate environment. Not so fantastic for personal devices. Basically this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["92vuuZt7wak",1],["W6aKQ-eBFk0",1],["fpbOEoRrHyU",1]]},"_6sh097Dk5k":{"wy":"HN cites it for first-hand ARM history, including Thumb's origin, and for Jaggar's endorsement of RISC-V.","q":{"x":"Dave Jaggar has some interesting comments on the history around DEC, StrongARM and the Austin ARM team.","i":23670097,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23670097,"author":"klelatti","month":"2020-06","story":"Was Acorn's RISC OS an under-appreciated pearl of OS design?","stance":"rec","why":"Jaggar discusses ARM history around DEC, StrongARM, and Austin team.","quote":"Dave Jaggar has some interesting comments on the history around DEC, StrongARM and the Austin ARM team.","text":"Agreed plus Thumb which got ARM into Nokia mobile phones. Dave Jaggar has some interesting comments on the history around DEC, StrongARM and the Austin ARM team. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32975018,"author":"laurencerowe","month":"2022-09","story":"A history of ARM, part 1: Building the first chip","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Apple choosing ARM for the Newton.","quote":"","text":"Apple's investment in ARM in November 1990 (when ARM was spun out of Acorn as a joint venture between Acorn, Apple, and VLSI) was a result of Apple's decision to select ARM for the Newton sometime in 1990 since Acorn and Apple competed in the UK education market [1]. [1] [VIDEO] , \"A History of The ARM Microprocessor\""},{"id":27070995,"author":"brucehoult","month":"2021-05","story":"ARM vs. RISC-V Vector Extensions","stance":"reference","why":"ARM architect endorses RISC-V instruction set.","quote":"Dave Jaggar who developed the ARM7TDMI, Thumb, Thumb2.","text":"Here’s the opinion of probably THE most important ARM engineer of the 1990s and 2000s, Dave Jaggar who developed the ARM7TDMI, Thumb, Thumb2. [VIDEO] Check at 51:30 where he says (bringing up the topic of RISC-V himself) “I would Google RISC-V and find out all about it. They’ve done a fine instruction set, a fine job […] it’s the state of the art now for 32-bit general purpose instruction sets. And it’s got the 16-bit compressed stuff. So, yeah, learning about that, you’re learning from the best.”"},{"id":25995752,"author":"brucehoult","month":"2021-02","story":"RISC-V isn't as interesting as you think","stance":"reference","why":"Supports RISC-V argument with industry context.","quote":"","text":"There's really a lot of severely misinformed opinion there. \"Much of the hype of RISC-V is hoping for laptop/desktop/server class silicon.\" Really? RISC-V is pitched mostly against the likes of ARM, which seems to be doing fine without laptop/desktop/server class silicon. Several RISC-V vendors are already shipping Cortex A53/A55 class CPUs, which are used as the LITTLE cores in mobile devices -- and even still the main cores in lower end mobile. Several RISC-V vendors have formally announced A72 (SiFive U84) or A73 (Alibaba C910) class cores. Alibaba is apparently using these internally already, and boards for general sale are expected this year. SiFive's U84 will probably be shipping in around 12 months from now. ARM is a few steps ahead with A75 and A76, but those are just incremental developments and RISC-V is catching up fast. It seems the article was written before the HiFive Unmatched was announced -- but I don't think it was written before the U74 CPU cores in the Unmatched were announced in October 2018, so I guess the author either wasn't paying attention, or else don't understand the standard 2 to 2.5 years from announcement of a core to shipping products using an SoC with that core. There is nothing surprising about the HiFive Unmatched. Was the article also written before Apple announced its switch to ARM64 architecture and their M1 chip? The M1 uses ARM's 64 bit instruction set, but is far ahead of anything from ARM or its other licencees in performance. If some"},{"id":26002959,"author":"seedless-sensat","month":"2021-02","story":"RISC-V isn’t as interesting as you think","stance":"reference","why":"Key ARM architect endorsing RISC-V as state of the art.","quote":"Here is Dave Jaggar, a key ARM architect, endorsing RISC-V as state of the art in 2019","text":"> RISC-V seems like it hasn’t learned anything from CPUs designed after 1991. Never mind what RISC-V says about itself. Here is Dave Jaggar, a key ARM architect, endorsing RISC-V as state of the art in 2019: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":26006391,"author":"brucehoult","month":"2021-02","story":"RISC-V isn’t as interesting as you think","stance":"reference","why":"Supports RISC-V argument with industry context.","quote":"","text":"There's really a lot of severely misinformed opinion there. \"Much of the hype of RISC-V is hoping for laptop/desktop/server class silicon.\" Really? RISC-V is pitched mostly against the likes of ARM, which seems to be doing fine without laptop/desktop/server class silicon. Several RISC-V vendors are already shipping Cortex A53/A55 class CPUs, which are used as the LITTLE cores in mobile devices -- and even still the main cores in lower end mobile. Several RISC-V vendors have formally announced A72 (SiFive U84) or A73 (Alibaba C910) class cores. Alibaba is apparently using these internally already, and boards for general sale are expected this year. SiFive's U84 will probably be shipping in around 12 months from now. ARM is a few steps ahead with A75 and A76, but those are just incremental developments and RISC-V is catching up fast. It seems the article was written before the HiFive Unmatched was announced -- but I don't think it was written before the U74 CPU cores in the Unmatched were announced in October 2018, so I guess the author either wasn't paying attention, or else don't understand the standard 2 to 2.5 years from announcement of a core to shipping products using an SoC with that core. There is nothing surprising about the HiFive Unmatched. Was the article also written before Apple announced its switch to ARM64 architecture and their M1 chip? The M1 uses ARM's 64 bit instruction set, but is far ahead of anything from ARM or its other licencees in performance. If some"},{"id":25676300,"author":"brucehoult","month":"2021-01","story":"Show HN: v8-riscv — Port of JavaScript V8 engine to RISC-V","stance":"reference","why":"ARM architect endorses RISC-V at 51:30.","quote":"Dave Jaggar who developed the ARM7TDMI, Thumb, Thumb2.","text":"That's the opinion of one ARM engineer I've never heard of. Here's the opinion of probably THE most important ARM engineer of the 1990s and 2000s, Dave Jaggar who developed the ARM7TDMI, Thumb, Thumb2. [VIDEO] Check at 51:30 where he says \"Are there any ARM snipers? No ... I would Google RISC-V and find out all about it. They've done a fine instruction set, a fine job [...] it's the state of the art now for 32-bit general purpose instruction sets. And it's got the 16-bit compressed stuff. So, yeah, learning about that, you're learning from the best.\""},{"id":25500437,"author":"brucehoult","month":"2020-12","story":"Apple M1 foreshadows Rise of RISC-V","stance":"reference","why":"ARM architect endorses RISC-V at 51:30.","quote":"Dave Jaggar who developed the ARM7TDMI, Thumb, Thumb2.","text":"Who says RISC-V is mediocre? Here's the opinion of probably THE most important ARM engineer of the 1990s and 2000s, Dave Jaggar who developed the ARM7TDMI, Thumb, Thumb2. [VIDEO] Check at 51:30 where he says \"Are there any ARM snipers? No .. I would Google RISC-V and find out all about it. They've done a fine instruction set, a fine job [...] it's the state of the art now for 32-bit general purpose instruction sets. And it's got the 16-bit compressed stuff. So, yeah, learning about that, you're learning from the best.\""},{"id":25204574,"author":"brucehoult","month":"2020-11","story":"ESP32-C3 WiFi and BLE RISC-V processor is pin-to-pin compatible with ESP8266","stance":"reference","why":"ARM engineer praising RISC-V.","quote":"says RISC-V is great.","text":"That's one opinion. Another ARM engineer who contributed one heck of a lot more to ARM's success (designed ARM7TDMI, Thumb, Thumb2) says RISC-V is great. See at 51 minutes in this [VIDEO]"},{"id":23675641,"author":"klelatti","month":"2020-06","story":"Was Acorn's RISC OS an under-appreciated pearl of OS design?","stance":"reference","why":"Jaggar explains Thumb's origin in Nintendo ROM size constraints.","quote":"Dave Jaggar makes it clear in this video that it was to keep code within smaller Nintendo ROM size constraints.","text":"Surprising but true! Dave Jaggar makes it clear in this video that it was to keep code within smaller Nintendo ROM size constraints. Seven year gap between ARM / Nintendo discussions and GBA appearance! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["yaonVYOTSsk",1]]},"c6SUOeAqOjU":{"wy":"HN readers treat it as a contrarian counterpoint to the usual PARC mythology, though one commenter noted a few apparent mistakes in it.","q":{"x":"Nelson's history is a breath of fresh air.","i":7547786,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7547786,"author":"tod222","month":"2014-04","story":"Alan Kay at Demo: The Future Doesn't Have to Be Incremental","stance":"rec","why":"Nelson lists limitations of PARC's vision, offering a fresher history.","quote":"Nelson's history is a breath of fresh air.","text":"Thank you. Nelson does a great job enumerating the limitations of PARC's vision and the capabilities absent from PARC's work. After being exposed to PARC's self-congratulatory self-promotion for decades, Nelson's history is a breath of fresh air. This is not to devalue what PARC accomplished, but that its accomplishments must be taken in perspective. It All Went Wrong at Xerox PARC is also on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38588967,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2023-12","story":"1968-12-09 the mother of all demos","stance":"reference","why":"Recalled as related Ted Nelson video.","quote":"a Ted Nelson video subtitled 'It all went wrong at Xerox PARC'","text":"This comment jogged my memory of a Ted Nelson video subtitled 'It all went wrong at Xerox PARC' : [VIDEO]"},{"id":7128684,"author":"leoc","month":"2014-01","story":"Ted Nelson: It All Went Wrong at Xerox PARC","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I noted a couple of apparent mistakes","text":"It's Ted Nelson rattling through some of his computer-history notes on camera. So it's good material, but it's much better presented in his enjoyable and reasonably-priced book /Geeks Bearing Gifts/ [LINK] . I noted a couple of apparent mistakes: at [VIDEO] Nelson says that Xerox paid Jobs to look at the PARC work, while AFAIK Apple in fact paid Xerox, in pre-IPO Apple stock. He also suggested that Xerox tried to bring in Jobs, when it seems to have been Apple who made the approach (at the instigation of Jef Raskin at Apple). [VIDEO] is misleading. PARC was more-or-less forbidden to buy a DEC PDP-10, which would have reflected badly on the competing machines from Xerox's new acquisition Scientific Data Systems, so PARC built a PDP-10 clone (the MAXC) in-house for their own use. That had very little to do with why they later designed and built the Altos: there was nothing remotely similar to an Alto out there that they could have bought (Nelson knows this)."}]},"rENyyRwxpHo":{"wy":"Linked as a quick visual intro to Laniakea and large-scale cosmic structure.","q":{"x":"There's also a short video on this","i":36686344,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":30164399,"author":"ok_computer","month":"2022-02","story":"Nearly 1k mysterious strands revealed in Milky Way’s center","stance":"rec","why":"Videos from the authors of the Laniakea paper.","quote":"I like these two from the authors of the Laniakea super cluster paper in 2014 Nature","text":"I like these two from the authors of the Laniakea super cluster paper in 2014 Nature: [VIDEO] Contributing Author: [LINK]"},{"id":36686344,"author":"pmlnr","month":"2023-07","story":"Laniakea Supercluster","stance":"reference","why":"Short video on the Laniakea supercluster.","quote":"There's also a short video on this","text":"There's also a short video on this: [VIDEO] Plus there's that scene from Stargate Universe: - Are those stars? - No, those are galaxies."},{"id":30731011,"author":"jerf","month":"2022-03","story":"First images from James Webb telescope exceed expectations","stance":"reference","why":"Close illustration of large-scale structure of the universe.","quote":"This isn't the exact sort of video I'm looking for, but it's close enough to what I'm talking about","text":"As well as what ISL said, if the universe is toroidial it seems like it's larger than our visible horizon. While images of an individual galaxy might be hard to tell if they are repeated, we can see enough of the structure of the universe that if our visible horizon was much larger than the real value of the universe and there was wrapping going on, we'd see it. This isn't the exact sort of video I'm looking for, but it's close enough to what I'm talking about: [VIDEO] Everywhere we look we see distinct structures. It is not a bad thought, though. It may well do some sort of wrapping around, just at a larger scale than we can see. It's an open problem."}]},"yW2LpFkVfYk":{"wy":"Cited in voting-related threads as a favorite example of street interviews on voter ID, though it is a partisan satirical piece.","m":[{"id":29997806,"author":"300bps","month":"2022-01","story":"IRS Will Soon Require Selfies for Online Access","stance":"rec","why":"Street interviews on voter ID laws in Berkeley and Harlem.","quote":"My favorite video on this topic","text":"My favorite video on this topic: [VIDEO] They first go to Berkeley, California and ask a bunch of white people their thoughts on voter idea laws. \"They're racist, african americans don't know where the DMV is, don't have access to the Internet\" type of answers. Then they go to Harlem and ask people's thoughts on it."},{"id":33565419,"author":"TechBro8615","month":"2022-11","story":"Voting from Antarctica","stance":"reference","why":"Street interview video on voter ID.","quote":"famously exposed as ridiculous in this","text":"\"Black people can't get IDs\" is a racist trope promoted by the Democrats, famously exposed as ridiculous in this \"on the street\" video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30741182,"author":"brigandish","month":"2022-03","story":"Masks on planes requirement dropped by some UK airlines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> At least some of them is about proxy and postal voting. That has no relation to IDs. Firstly, that’s not true. As quoted above: > The vast majority of convictions have involved postal or proxy ballots, often in conjunction with attempts to manipulate the electoral registers by registering bogus electors or adding electors to the register at empty properties. That requires processes that are slack about identity . The requirement to prove/provide identity at certain points in the process would render these problems moot. If you wish to focus solely on in-person voting then say so, but that’s not the same as there being no relationship. Secondly, as with any conspiracy theory, you would have to: - show me how you knew their intentions without recourse to mind reading And with restpect to this particular case: - show how needing ID would reduce (legitimate) votes cast (I hope it’s not something like this[1]) - explain why increased security is a bad thing (taking into account the ease and low cost of this solution) It would be just as easy to claim that those who are against such measures wish to benefit from the fraud. That wouldn’t be possible without mind reading and I lack that ability. However, it does correlate , those against better election finance protections tend to benefit from them (Conservatives and Labour). Those against better proxy vote, postal vote and in-person voting fraud tend to benefit from it (Labour). In either case I don’t care, to improve the legitima"},{"id":30099888,"author":"300bps","month":"2022-01","story":"Poll: Is the leetcode grind necessary to land a high paying remote job?","stance":"reference","why":"Street interviews comparing reactions to voter ID laws.","quote":"Your comment reminds me of the video where they ask white people in Berkeley, CA if voter id laws are racist.","text":"Your comment reminds me of the video where they ask white people in Berkeley, CA if voter id laws are racist. They say yes of course they’re racist because African Americans don’t have id as often, don’t have as much access to the internet, don’t know where the DMV is, etc. Then they go to Harlem to ask people what they think of those comments. [VIDEO] As for your claims themselves, In 3 months I studied 350 hours to pass the hardest test in finance while I was married, had three young children and worked full time. Nobody has time to do the things required to advance your career. You have to make the time. For years, I also recruited and mentored students from an inner city bootcamp near me where the students spend 80-90 hours per week for 12 weeks learning software engineering. The vast majority of students are minority and there is fierce competition to hire graduates by local companies."},{"id":28576393,"author":"lugged","month":"2021-09","story":"U.S. court upholds dismissal of lawsuit against NSA on 'state secrets' grounds","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"How are voters being suppressed? That link was... lacking in detail. Yes it says voters rights are suppressed, but doesn't actually explain why or how. Do you even know? Because I've heard this racist story before. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23660508,"author":"nec4b","month":"2020-06","story":"NASA names headquarters after first Black female engineer Mary W. Jackson","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well at the and of the day you still have to make a choice who deserves what. Since you don't want meritocracy and argue for a compassionate ideological framework you still haven't provided how people are ranked in your system. How does a Russian immigrant Igor who is white, has a bad accent, hardly speaks the language compares to Karim, a successful black business man? All I see is a group of white people arguing with an another group of white people for power and one is using minorities just so that it can call the other white group racist. No where have i seen minorities ranking themselves in the historical injustice ladder. You don't even realize how patronizing you are. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20760109,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2019-08","story":"16M Americans will vote on hackable paperless machines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Easily gamed without strong (physical presense) voter ID requirements. EDIT: Mail in voting is skimmed with little to no risk. Oops, lost 1% of that zip code... [VIDEO]"},{"id":20761215,"author":"drak0n1c","month":"2019-08","story":"16M Americans will vote on hackable paperless machines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Assuming that people can't get IDs based on their demographic is also a form of discrimination. Weak ID requirements disenfranchise voters of all backgrounds by enabling the possibility of diluting their votes with fraudulent registrations and ballots. Los Angeles County had more registered voters than they have age 18+ citizens and recently were forced by lawsuit to purge 5 million invalid registrations from their rolls. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"-i0hat7pdpk":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an early official Go talk, including Rob Pike's remarks that Go aims to be useful rather than theoretically exciting.","m":[{"id":4517076,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2012-09","story":"Will Go be the new go-to programming language?","stance":"reference","why":"Entry in a personal list of Go talks.","quote":"Writing Web Apps in Go","text":"The \"Talks and Videos\" page ( [LINK] ) is outdated as well. Here's my personal list: Why Learn Go? [LINK] Short interview with Rob Pike, OSCON 2012 Get Started with Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand A Tour of Go [LINK] Russ Cox Go Concurrency Patterns [LINK] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2012 Goroutines, Channels Meet the Go Team [LINK] Panel (Pike, Thompson, Griesemer, Gerrand, Fitzpatrick, Simons), Google I/O 2012 Computing Map Tiles with Go on App Engine [LINK] Chris Broadfoot, Andrew Gerrand, Google I/O 2012 Go in Production [LINK] Gustavo Niemeyer, Google I/O 2012 Go: Code that grows with grace [LINK] (Contains some audio glitches) Andrew Gerrand Interview with Rob Pike [LINK] Rob Pike, GOTO Conference Lexical Scanning in Go [LINK] Rob Pike Cuddle, A Go AppEngine Demo [LINK] Andrew Gerrand Building Integrated Apps on Google's Cloud Platform [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, Google Developer Day Japan 2011 Real World Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, I/O BootCamp 2011 Go: it's that simple?! [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, OSDC 2011 Practical Go Programming [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, FOSDEM 2011 Writing Web Apps in Go [VIDEO] Andrew Gerrand, Rob Pike, Google I/O 2011 Go Programming [LINK] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2010 Another Go at Language Design [LINK] Rob Pike, Stanford University Public Static Void [LINK] Rob Pike, OSCON 2010 The Go Programming Language [LINK] Rob Pike, Google Tech Talk"},{"id":2747420,"author":"singular","month":"2011-07","story":"Go Rocks - How Can We Avoid Something This Bad In The Future?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Rob Pike's remark on Go being useful, not theoretically exciting.","quote":"","text":"God what an annoying troll. It's a very new language, and things are being improved + fixed all the time, I would be very surprised indeed if tail-call optimisation + the ever so vital self-recursive lambda issue weren't fixed at a later date. To treat these things as permanently a part of the language is disingenuous at best. Rob Pike (part of the core team) has said that what puts a lot of PL people off is that it is not theoretically exciting, it's just designed to be very useful [1]. The funny thing about go is that it seems like an uninteresting language when you first begin, but as you write more code you begin to appreciate its simplicity and orthogonality and realise that these are extremely nice qualities. Language design is as much about saying no to things as it is to saying yes to things. On a related note, I don't like the implication that there is now a scientific method to language design which obviates the need for practical considerations - that strikes me as class A ivory tower bullshit. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2585203,"author":"codesink","month":"2011-05","story":"C'mon, stop writing Java for Android","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the video mentioned, Q&A at end.","quote":"the video I mentioned is at","text":"They managed to optimize their golang.org homepage to get the position #5 on the word \"go\". They must be good at SEO. BTW the video I mentioned is at [VIDEO] (at the end during Q&A)"}]},"12OSBJwogFc":{"wy":"Pointed to for nice demonstrations of bendable glass, though commenters note it feels like an infomercial.","q":{"x":"For nice demos, look at","i":19471241,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":35602035,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-04","story":"Prince Rupert's Drop vs Molten Glas","stance":"reference","why":"Corning-sponsored MythBusters glass video, infomercial feel but neat.","quote":"they have the feel of infomercials - it isn't subtle at all... they're still neat","text":"In the \"yet another PRD video\" ... though one of the earlier ones... The Mythbusters did a pair of videos for Corning eight years ago (and yes, they have the feel of infomercials - it isn't subtle at all... they're still neat): The Glass Age, Part 1: Flexible, Bendable Glass - [VIDEO] The Glass Age, Part 2: Strong, Durable Glass - [LINK]"},{"id":19471241,"author":"Someone","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: Do we need foldable screens or it it just another fad?","stance":"reference","why":"Nice demos of bendable glass.","quote":"For nice demos, look at","text":"There’s no reason it can’t be glass. Glass can be made highly flexible: [LINK] says a bend radius of 15 times the diameter of the glass typically is a safe assumption. That factor 15 doesn’t directly apply to plates of glass, and still might give too high a radius, but this is a hotly researched field. For nice demos, look at [VIDEO] (And of course, there’s [LINK] , invented about 2,000 years ago."},{"id":20783599,"author":"krispbyte","month":"2019-08","story":"Show HN: SponsorBlock – Skip sponsorship segments of YouTube videos","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Would this block the whole video about the Glass Age[0] by the MythBusters since the whole thing was sponsored? :)","text":"Would this block the whole video about the Glass Age[0] by the MythBusters since the whole thing was sponsored? :) [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"7GhAuZH6phs":{"wy":"Used as a concrete example of split-key instruments and an audible demonstration of the D-sharp versus E-flat difference.","q":{"x":"can you hear the difference in the video linked in the article","i":32626570,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32626570,"author":"PeterisP","month":"2022-08","story":"Why are D-sharp and E-flat considered to be two different notes?","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates audible difference between D-sharp and E-flat.","quote":"can you hear the difference in the video linked in the article","text":"No, and yes. The difference is obvious even those who don't distinguish pitches much (can you hear the difference in the video linked in the article - [VIDEO] ) because the 'wrong' one played in a scale or a chord sounds really, really horrible; that's why we have all the work done on 'tempered' tuning to reduce the gap so that everything is just slightly off."},{"id":30365846,"author":"rdlw","month":"2022-02","story":"Music theory for nerds (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"keyboard instruments with split flat/sharp keys [4]","text":"Except for voice [0], any fretless stringed instrument [1], any fretted string instrument with separate frets for sharps and flats [2], keyboard instruments that allow expression [3], keyboard instruments with split flat/sharp keys [4], and electronic instruments that allow unorthodox tunings without huge hassle [5]... I'm sure I'm missing some, but other than that you're totally right ;) [0] [LINK] , [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":21290939,"author":"mastazi","month":"2019-10","story":"Scala: A software tool for microtonic, macrotonic and other alternative tunings","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a meantone organ with split sharps.","quote":"This is an example of meantone organ with split sharps","text":"The list of export formats is really impressive. It's interesting to note that Hauptwerk (a pipe organ software emulator) is among the supported targets; some early organs used historical temperaments which sound different compared to equal temperament and some of those had split keys [1][2]. [1] This is an example of meantone organ with split sharps [VIDEO] [2] This is an explanation of Meantone and split keys [LINK]"}]},"ELCq63652ig":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a good example of how Google treats browsers that are not Chrome and of the browser-monopoly concern.","m":[{"id":34507147,"author":"AndroidKitKat","month":"2023-01","story":"Apple attempting to stop investigation into its practices involving Browsers","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Google behavior toward non-Chrome browsers","quote":"this video [1] is a good example of some of the behavior Google uses when they find out you're not using Chrome.","text":"While being 3 years old, this video [1] is a good example of some of the behavior Google uses when they find out you're not using Chrome. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24062086,"author":"mendyberger","month":"2020-08","story":"Changes to SameSite Cookie Behavior – A Call to Action for Web Developers","stance":"reference","why":"Example complaint of Google services degrading on other browsers","quote":"Just to point out one of the many such complaints","text":"No, people don't choose them because they like Chrome, people choose them because Google makes sure that their services don't work well in other browsers! Just to point out one of the many such complaints [VIDEO]"},{"id":20295555,"author":"nkkollaw","month":"2019-06","story":"Google’s new reCAPTCHA has a dark side","stance":"reference","why":"Video on Google building a browser monopoly","quote":"Tech Altar even had a video about it the other day","text":"Google is trying to kill the competition by purposely introducing weird bugs here and there, taking advantage of the fact that they own the most visited sites on the web. They've been doing it for a while now, Tech Altar even had a video about it the other day: [VIDEO] Along with the censorship and privacy issues, I guess it's time for them to change their payoff, \"don't be evil\"."}]},"FHwnrYm0mNc":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that AI can solve interview-style algorithm puzzles, in threads questioning the value of Leetcode.","m":[{"id":29805104,"author":"usrbinbash","month":"2022-01","story":"Leetcode has taught me that I'm a bad engineer","stance":"reference","why":"Copilot solving Leetcode problems as rebuttal.","quote":"","text":"> But if there wasn't any art to these problems, then after 400+ problems wouldn't you have figured out the robotic algorithm to solve them? [VIDEO]"},{"id":29413225,"author":"thomasahle","month":"2021-12","story":"Solving Advent of Code Puzzles with GitHub Copilot: Day 1","stance":"reference","why":"Copilot solving harder Leetcode problems.","quote":"it did manage to solve some of the harder leetcode questions here","text":"It's a question of whether copilot can be \"creative\". I'm mit sure, but it did manage to solve some of the harder leetcode questions here [VIDEO] and I'm curious how it will fare on the later advent of code problems."},{"id":28005055,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2021-07","story":"FSF-calls for white papers on philosophical and legal questions around Copilot","stance":"reference","why":"Copilot solving Leetcode interview questions.","quote":"i actually like it that copilot is better than me at solving interview questions.","text":"i actually like it that copilot is better than me at solving interview questions. [VIDEO] I for one welcome our robot overlords. i wonder if they could retrain the model on BSD or MIT licensed code only; How much of the open source code is licensed as GPL vs more permissive licenses, does anyone know? Interesting that they want to charge for the use of co-pilot, I guess that we will see this business model more in the future."}]},"FY8SwIvxj8o":{"wy":"Cited as a demonstration of how samey pop music is, in threads about chord progressions and homogeneous music.","q":{"x":"This brilliant mashup of 6 different formulaic pop country songs would imply that there is a very low bar for some music listeners.","i":45898084,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43558859,"author":"nyrikki","month":"2025-04","story":"Shared DNA in Music","stance":"reference","why":"Mashup showing how similar pop country songs are","quote":"Sir Mashalot had a few videos a while ago.","text":"The same applies to risk-adverse, high profit genres and/or producer heavy ones like pop country. Obviously zeitgeist and even conventions and music theory play a role. Sir Mashalot had a few videos a while ago. [VIDEO] The Blues Traveler trolling with the song Hook using the melody from Pachelbel's Canon with lyrics explicitly calling this out wasn't noticed by almost anyone. [LINK] Obviously Pop heavily samples too, E.G. Tom Tom Club Genius of Love being used by Mariah Carey's Fantasy, but it goes beyond sampling. The Rolling Stones pulling from artists like Fred McDowell and Led Zeppelin settled copyright suits on Lemon Song, Whole Lotta Love, Bring it on Home and Dazed and Confused etc... The invention of the Fairlight Sampler may have lead to groups like the Pet Shop Boys sampling dozens of other works, but as the Stones and Led Zeppelin show it wasn't unique. Well I guess the Pet Shop Boys were inspired by Grandmaster Flash, and if you listen to the original recording of West End Girls you can hear James Brown samples...not sure if that counts as \"hip-hop\" in that case or not. [LINK] As a bad guitar player, I can understand why even Rock and Roll pretty much grew out of artists copying R&B/Gospel artists like Sister Rosetta Tharpe, but inspiration vs copying is a fuzzy line. But basically the tooling changed, not the methods which have complex and intertwined causes, motivations, and effects."},{"id":16145818,"author":"soundwave106","month":"2018-01","story":"Lana Del Rey, Radiohead, and the Difficulty of Making Original Music","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The progression is I / III / IV / iv. \"Get Free\"'s verses (not chorus) is in this progression in B. \"Creep\" is in G. Not the most common progression, but a Google on it led to Reddit where a few other examples came up -- part of the chorus of David Bowie's \"Space Oddity\" (\"this is ground control to Major Tom\") or ELO's \"Tightrope\". One problem with \"Creep\" is that it is a simple four chord loop, unlike the above songs. Nothing wrong with a four bar chord loop, but four chord loops alone really aren't unique enough to be copyrightable (MHO). Four chord loops are a staple of basic rock and roll (garage rock and punk etc.). Because the scope of triad movement is limited in these styles, the chords you are playing (and even to some degree the melodies that come from this) have probably been done before a long time ago. Think for instance what would happen if the \"12 bar blues\" progression or the \"doo wop progression\" (I-vi-IV-V) which dominates 1950s music was placed under lock and key. Inevitably bands in four chord loop mode step on each other and produce similar sounding songs. When I played in a cover band, we played with this sort of thing to deliver \"two songs at once\" moments. For example, Puddle of Mudd's \"She Hates Me\" has the exact same chords (and pacing!) as Suicidal Tendencies' \"I Saw Your Mommy\" -- both are Emaj Amaj F#maj Bmaj. The chorus of the Kingsmen's \"Louie Louie\" and the Troggs' \"Wild Thing\" (I-IV-V-IV in Amaj, somewhat similar rhythm) are also similar enoug"},{"id":14668644,"author":"iheartmemcache","month":"2017-06","story":"Generating music with expressive timing and dynamics","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"It's sort of convincingly improvisatory.\" I'm not sure if it was the dynamics specifically, but it was clear to me that A was human. Within 30 seconds I was so sure, I hit pause and loaded the answer to see if I was right (I was, and I'm likely the worst pianist on these forums and only a casual fan of music that falls into the 'classical' genre.) Here's[0] a fabulous physics paper that analyses the 16th notes by a studio drummer widely considered one of the best in his field. IIRC, the paper mentions he couldn't record with a click because it'd throw him off. That being said, the quality of the recording didn't suffer (his 2nd take of the track was more than good enough for the rest of the musicians to record against). So his own 'internal metronome' was more than good enough. The interesting thing wasn't that his syncopation was incompatible with a click track, but rather the skew which evolved throughout each phrase had a mathematical model that fit well against it. The study compared his recording against a corpus of user submissions of the same track and all of these drummers universally followed a similar set of dynamics. So presumably all humans (or at least, all western drummers who elected to submit their recordings) have that same skew intrinsically. It's interesting whether it's a byproduct of culture (like an accent) or a feature intrinsic to humans. In fact, that itself would be an interesting study -- compare the patterns of a traditionally schooled western j"},{"id":11964922,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2016-06","story":"Led Zeppelin did not steal 'Stairway' riff, jurors say","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how many songs share the same chord progression","quote":"And yes, I-V-vi-IV is interesting.","text":"Musical tastes have gotten simpler in general. There remain the musicians who push the envelope, but they just aren't as popular. Its fine: the typical audience just doesn't have the ear to appreciate certain riffs. Simplicity breeds familiarity which breeds comfort. With that said, I still hear great songs on occasion, you just don't really hear it from Top 40s radio stations anymore. EDIT: I don't mean to say that modern music is bad. Its just simple. The vast vast majority of 2010s Music is I-V-vi-IV turnaround with one additional chord in there to make things a little bit different. [LINK] In the 80s and 90s, a bit of musical experimentation was done. In the 2000s and 2010, electronica is definitely pushing the envelope but your typical top 40 songs are sticking to the basics. And yes, I-V-vi-IV is interesting. [VIDEO] Its so interesting that everybody is doing it. I mean, its not \"brain-dead\" simple, but any person trained in music theory can hear the ridiculous amount of similarity between these riffs. But taking the pattern that literally every other country song has used and throwing just slightly different lyrics on top of it isn't original."},{"id":10030549,"author":"TheOtherHobbes","month":"2015-08","story":"Classical music generation with recurrent neural networks","stance":"reference","why":"Example of pop sameness via mashup","quote":"","text":"Pop has actually ossified over the last ten years or so. Dubstep is more than fifteen years old now, and the Paul Van Dyk album I've just listened to sounds a lot like the last Paul Van Dyk album a few years ago. Then you get this: [LINK] and this: [VIDEO] and the now famous this: [LINK] and you start to wonder how much entropy there is to analyse. Modern classical has gone the way of modern art. It's now basically a marketing exercise. The musical experience is secondary. But then I expect creative AIs to be better at marketing too..."},{"id":8876942,"author":"im3w1l","month":"2015-01","story":"Why Do All Records Sound the Same?","stance":"reference","why":"The original six-song country mashup","quote":"Original mashup","text":"That seems to be some random guy just reposting. Didn't know youtube had \"blogspam\" before Original mashup: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45898084,"author":"noman-land","month":"2025-11","story":"AI-Generated Country Song Is Topping Billboard Chart. That Should Infuriate Us","stance":"critical","why":"Mashup of six formulaic country songs shows low bar for listeners","quote":"This brilliant mashup of 6 different formulaic pop country songs would imply that there is a very low bar for some music listeners.","text":"This brilliant mashup of 6 different formulaic pop country songs would imply that there is a very low bar for some music listeners. [VIDEO]"}]},"FcW9kUUTfxY":{"wy":"Linked as the go-to footage of a linear aerospike engine actually running whenever aerospike nozzles come up.","q":{"x":"here's a video of a linear aerospike test","i":8679484,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":44396569,"author":"cubefox","month":"2025-06","story":"A Review of Aerospike Nozzles: Current Trends in Aerospace Applications","stance":"reference","why":"Ground test of an aerospike engine","quote":"","text":"A reusable lower stage with powered landing also had never been flown to orbit at that time. And in contrast to aerospike engines, which had been tested before on the ground [1], you can't do ground testing with rocket stage landings. I think SpaceX didn't try to develop aerospike nozzles because the advantages probably aren't that large compared to the mixed nozzle design they are currently using. They also reused the same ceramic heat shield material developed for the space shuttle instead of developing something new. Compare that to the cancelled \"VentureStar\": It would have used both linear aerospike engines and a new metallic thermal protection system (TPS) instead of a ceramic one. I remember an interview where Musk answered the question of why they aren't doing aerospikes or metallic heat shields etc, that there are many ways to skin a cat. They are only doing one thing that they think will work, which is not necessarily the best possible solution, but potentially faster or cheaper to develop. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26519739,"author":"api","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"reference","why":"Example of NASA aerospike work that never became operational","quote":"Linear aerospike engines are pretty neat too","text":"I love SpaceX, but I don't find them quite as miraculously impressive as some people. SpaceX is \"just\" doing what NASA did in their glory days: rapidly iterating, innovating, and then carrying those innovations all the way through to real world flight. That last part is key. In industry we call that \"shipping.\" NASA didn't stop innovating per se. In the 1980s and 1990s they worked out their own version of vertical take-off and landing and actually test flew it: [LINK] Linear aerospike engines are pretty neat too: [VIDEO] Problem is absolutely none of this stuff ever got close to operational. It kept getting cancelled in the prototype stage. If the DC-X had been developed into a fully reusable first stage, we'd have had something like Falcon 9 by the year 2000 and might be on Mars by now."},{"id":8679484,"author":"ridgeguy","month":"2014-12","story":"Firefly Space Systems charges full-speed toward low Earth orbit","stance":"reference","why":"Test fire of a linear aerospike engine","quote":"here's a video of a linear aerospike test","text":"Lots of work done on aerospike engines. Google IYF. FYI, here's a video of a linear aerospike test: [VIDEO]"}]},"I9lquok4Pdk":{"wy":"HN links it as a reference for the history and distribution of nuclear tests, and occasionally as an example of audio data presentation.","q":{"x":"There is a timeline form 1945 to 1998 of all the nuclear detonations recorded","i":2347711,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":21190221,"author":"Angostura","month":"2019-10","story":"Supreme Court allows blind people to sue retailers if websites aren't accessible","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an audio approach to data.","quote":"","text":"Just pondering whether there is a system that converts line graphs into series of rising and falling tones, while a voice reads out the x axis markers. If you have multiple series, you could have different sounds running in parallel - sin wave, square wave or perhaps musical instruments each denoting a different series. That might actually be usable if there was an automated system for creating them on the fly. There's also this kind of approach [VIDEO]"},{"id":2347711,"author":"simonsarris","month":"2011-03","story":"Was Greece Just Nuked?","stance":"reference","why":"Timeline of recorded nuclear detonations by place and nation.","quote":"There is a timeline form 1945 to 1998 of all the nuclear detonations recorded","text":"There is a timeline form 1945 to 1998 of all the nuclear detonations recorded, where they took place and what nation did it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7615517,"author":"Retric","month":"2014-04","story":"Did Israel steal bomb-grade uranium from the United States?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The last 30 seconds of that video mention it but there way off base.","text":"The last 30 seconds of that video mention it but there way off base. Humanity has done a lot of above ground nuclear testing and for the most part unless your vary close it's simply a slight increase in cancer risk. [VIDEO]"}]},"L1UgdoP2aeg":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of automated cloud labs and AI already running physical experiments.","q":{"x":"the more we can remove the human aspect and inherent ambiguity of science, the better for reproducibility","i":25668767,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39751841,"author":"fennecbutt","month":"2024-03","story":"Google Scholar search: \"certainly, here is\" -chatgpt -llm","stance":"reference","why":"AI already running physical experiments.","quote":"But there is already ai running physical experiments","text":"Which will be so amazing. But there is already ai running physical experiments: [VIDEO] But it has been done for at lest a decade or so I think"},{"id":32323953,"author":"strateospeter","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (August 2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Video about Strateos's automated cloud lab.","quote":"Check out this Seeker video to learn more","text":"Strateos | Senior Software Engineer (Ruby on Rails) | Remote within US* | Full-Time Strateos is a life sciences company that uses remote control and automated cloud labs. Our mission is to create new knowledge driven by data, computation, artificial intelligence, automation software, and high-throughput robotics with the goal of fundamentally advancing the life sciences. Check out this Seeker video to learn more: [VIDEO] We are looking for a backend Senior Software Engineer fluent in Rails and experienced with Java. VISA sponsorship is available. *Employees can work remotely from CA, WA, NY, TX, OR, AZ, IL, NC, MA, DE, OH, VT, MN, VA. Feel free to apply via email (peter.bui@strateos.com) or directly on our website. [LINK]"},{"id":25668767,"author":"davnn","month":"2021-01","story":"Groundhog: Addressing the Threat That R Poses to Reproducible Research","stance":"reference","why":"Examples of automating science to improve reproducibility.","quote":"the more we can remove the human aspect and inherent ambiguity of science, the better for reproducibility","text":"The fun thing is that there are approaches that want to go beyond this kind of methodological description of a scientific process to code [0, 1]. In general I would say that the more we can remove the human aspect and inherent ambiguity of science, the better for reproducibility. See [2] for a couple of examples. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"NIhCuzxNvv0":{"wy":"Used as a critical reference to argue gravity energy storage is impractical, not as a general recommendation.","q":{"x":"Several physical and technological factors make Energy Vault completely impractical.","i":25654918,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31750774,"author":"ncmncm","month":"2022-06","story":"If it isn't going to work, just shut it down","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Softbank invested. What else do you need to know?","text":"[LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] Softbank invested. What else do you need to know? By contrast, this is not stupid: [LINK] They might still not execute well, but the idea is sound. What is different? They use a single weight, up to thousands of tons. It is suspended in a disused mineshaft, up to a kilometer deep, protected from weather. The one weight just goes up and down, i.e. it has just two moving parts, the weight and the winch. Winches are extra-mature technology. They are talking about sealing the shaft and compressing hydrogen into it. And, maybe storing heat there, besides. Both ideas are also simple, but they don't have to work for the product to be viable; if they do work, they add value. My impression is they added those so their solution could seem more \"innovative\": the weight in the shaft, by itself, is a sure thing , so probably missed out on some long-shot seed subsidies. Funding in the energy sector is not well aligned. They will probably need to figure ways to get their cost lower. Batteries are approaching $200/MWh, with people expecting that to hit $100. Certain chemistries (\"Form\") are quoting $20, but are probably over-optimistic. 50 years operation with no degradation could beat a nominally cheaper system that needs the expensive part replaced regularly."},{"id":26922901,"author":"blueblisters","month":"2021-04","story":"Gravity batteries try to beat chemical ones with winches, weights, mine shafts","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Wasn’t Energy Vault debunked as being too impractical?","text":"Wasn’t Energy Vault debunked as being too impractical? Here: [VIDEO] These guys “solve” the wind and control problem by digging a 300m deep pipe for the weights."},{"id":25654918,"author":"sthnblllII","month":"2021-01","story":"Gravity Energy Storage: Alternative to batteries for grid storage","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Several physical and technological factors make Energy Vault completely impractical.","text":"Several physical and technological factors make Energy Vault completely impractical. Dr Philip Mason explains them here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25655016,"author":"aaron695","month":"2021-01","story":"Gravity Energy Storage: Alternative to batteries for grid storage","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This idea is nuts, just watch if you don't understand why.","text":"This idea is nuts, just watch [VIDEO] if you don't understand why. Does no one do fundamentals at school anymore, water is around the same density as concrete (~2.4 times smaller) except you can just use a simple pump. The reason they use concrete in these designs is because if they used water people would say it's stupid. That's literally the reason. It's the same for mineshafts. These concepts are introduced to confuse you. \"But pumped hydro requires some very specific geography—two big reservoirs of water\" So does concrete. Expect you are building the geography and it'll be around the same size if it stores around the same energy."},{"id":24619365,"author":"dimnsionofsound","month":"2020-09","story":"Nuclear power: Are we too anxious about the risks of radiation?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here’s a video discussing the drawbacks","text":"I’m not sure this idea is all that they’re marketing it as. Here’s a video discussing the drawbacks [0], the guy doing the video can be kind of abrasive at times but worth watching/skimming as a counterpoint. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21310828,"author":"aaron695","month":"2019-10","story":"UK's disused mine shafts plan to store renewable energy","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is a joke Here's an example video to explain if you can't see why","text":"This is a joke Here's an example video to explain if you can't see why - [VIDEO] [edit] I know it's a waste trying to talk to the death cult, but surely you can see 12,000 tonnes is just 23 * 23 * 23 meters cube of water. You could pump this up a hill, with a simple pump and a simple pipe into a hole No need for $$$ af mining engineers to work in a mine continuously. In a complex engineering task where pieces are moving everywhere. Fumes can kill. Noisy. You get you have to move the 500 tonne concretes block when it gets to the bottom (and top).... all this machinery needs people."}]},"NYJ2w82WifU":{"wy":"HN uses it only as a joke reference in unrelated threads.","m":[{"id":46416979,"author":"lifestyleguru","month":"2025-12","story":"No it's not a Battleship","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"So Americans are now about to replace words in dictionary with word \"Trump\" or it will only be used as a prefix? [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44346162,"author":"darkerside","month":"2025-06","story":"What is the most realistic submarine movie? (2019)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Both are like the term aladeen.","text":"Both are like the term aladeen. They mean something and its exact opposite. You can only determine from context. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26204693,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2021-02","story":"Disrespectful “dummy” not allowed in gRPC codebase","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The scene shows a physician asking a patient","text":"These remind me of \"General Aladeen\" replacing both the words \"positive\" and \"negative\" by \"Aladeen\"[0]. The scene shows a physician asking a patient: Physician: \"Do you want the Aladeen news or the Aladeen news?\" Patient: \"Aladeen\" Physician: \"You are HIV Aladeen\" There are some words being removed because they are perceived as insulting or disrespectful, and that perception appears to stem from the fact these words refer to a \"negative\" property. Say, \"moron\", \"idiot\", \"stupid\", \"dumb\" as opposed to \"smart\", \"sharp\", \"shrewd\", \"brilliant\", etc. That these words may be hurtful is not the argument. Wouldn't removing words that have antonyms destabilize language by creating a \"linguistic void\"? Some words refer to an \"absence\" of something or the opposite of something. \"Darkness\", \"cold\", \"unstable\", \"evil\". This either creates a sort of \"spectrum\" of which they are the ends, or a dichotomy with mutual exclusion. These can be deemed as disrespectful as well. Would the language become impractical when amputated from words invented to communicate \"lack of something\" or opposition to something. For example, if somehow the word \"cold\" became perceived as disrespectful, how would you describe an entity with that property? Not hot? There are insults that carry their own weight, like racist or homophobic slurs, for example. They don't need or have an opposite/antonym. I was on a forum and someone, knowing where I am from, has used the expression \"sand n-word\". Now, the moderators dee"}]},"Puo6Vgcbxps":{"wy":"HN mostly mentions it as an example of an extreme maker project, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":37040362,"author":"swighton","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the coolest physical thing you've made?","stance":"reference","why":"Own video about an explosive-powered baseball bat build.","quote":"I made a baseball bat that uses explosives to add a bunch of energy to your swing","text":"I made a baseball bat that uses explosives to add a bunch of energy to your swing and hit a baseball really far. It uses the explosive blanks normally used to shoot nails into concrete (e.g. ramset). It worked way better than I expected, didn't have a kick, and turned out to be just a really neat thing to look at while sipping a frosty beverage. I also made a video about it if you're interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36909662,"author":"wlesieutre","month":"2023-07","story":"Knife Throwing Machine Part 1 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Lists the creator's other projects including the gunpowder home run bat.","quote":"gunpowder-powered home run bat","text":"He has other videos like a pumpkin carving robot, gunpowder-powered home run bat, and a self-aiming pool cue [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30155610,"author":"evan_","month":"2022-01","story":"The New York Times buys Wordle","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fine. It’s easy if the batter has one of these: [VIDEO] And we assume that the motorcycle is already started and warmed up and the base path is paved etc. etc. Also the batter has a gun."}]},"d6sVWEu9HWU":{"wy":"Pointed to as an early example of LLM-powered NPC dialog in games.","m":[{"id":44160867,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2025-06","story":"Show HN: Penny-1.7B Irish Penny Journal style transfer","stance":"reference","why":"Example of LLM-driven NPC dialog in a game","quote":"Not sure what all they used for this [0]","text":"Now I'm just imagining a video game with characters each having their own fine tune applied on top for their dialog. I'm guessing you could use some relatively small models. In each case you would be feeding all the context to the model (player name, current relevant quests, summary of previous interactions, etc). Though maybe fine tuning/training isn't even needed and a good enough prompt will work (Not sure what all they used for this [0]). I'm excited for the first AAA game that tries this. Anyone that has played a RPG-style game knows that after a few times going into a city (or a couple play-throughs) the dialog feels repetitive. I love the idea of Skyrim but with better dialog. You could either run the models on the user's computer or maybe just run it on the backend so you can block certain generations (wrong/misleading/\"unsafe\") and just ship updated dialog lists to the client occasionally. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37154620,"author":"kridsdale3","month":"2023-08","story":"OpenAI Acquires Global Illumination","stance":"reference","why":"Skyrim mod demo with LLM-driven NPC dialog","quote":"I saw a demo of a Skyrim mod on Youtube [1]","text":"I saw a demo of a Skyrim mod on Youtube [1], where they wired the dialog screen with NPCs in the town of Windhelm up with the OpenAI API. Each character had all their Bethesda-written dialog fed to the LLM as a starting prompt, so each one communicated with the player within the bounds of their authored identity, and knew what that character was written to know. The human player used freeform in-game text-to-LLM as input to talk to the people in the town square. The latency was large, but not more than a few seconds. He was able to walk up to someone and say things like \"Where do you live? Who is your father?\" .... latency pause ... \"Oh me? I live in the Cloud District. It's where all of us Battleborn clan live, of course!\" The real kicker of this demo was that they passed the LLM-generated response dialog in to a deep-fake model trained on each character's original voice actors (since the corpus in Skyrim is vast, this ended up quite accurate). All of this was a major generational shift for gaming as a whole. I had just witnessed the first time a video game player could actually TALK to an NPC, in a fully unstructured and unscripted fashion, while each character remained as in-character as the original game's authors intended. So maybe this purchase by OpenAI is about something like that. Building NPCs for Biomes.gg that you can actually talk to. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35807318,"author":"trafficante","month":"2023-05","story":"Google, Microsoft CEOs Called to AI Meeting at White House","stance":"reference","why":"Skyrim NPCs powered by AI conversation","quote":"this is the biggest leap for video games since Half Life 1.","text":"Real-time convincing-sounding TTS is extremely close - I’d be surprised if we don’t get an elevenlabs equivalent to gpt-turbo before the end of summer. I spent an hour or three over the weekend messing around in Skyrim VR with a mod that does player speech recognition, pipes it out to GPT with identifier tags to give scene context, sends GPT output to elevenlabs (optional), and then the mod integrates it into Skyrim mouth rigging etc. Yes, there’s an extremely obvious lag before you get a response, but it’s on the order of seconds even though this is an early Skyrim mod with a ton of moving parts interacting. And the result is…astounding. As someone said in a comment on the below linked video: this is the biggest leap for video games since Half Life 1. [VIDEO]"}]},"gJJH7a2J9O8":{"wy":"Linked as the authoritative background on how Elixir's gradual set-theoretic type system works.","q":{"x":"watch the video presentation by the author[1]","i":38409954,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48398251,"author":"mechanicum","month":"2026-06","story":"Elixir v1.20 released: now a gradually typed language","stance":"reference","why":"One of several presentations on set-theoretic types","quote":"","text":"Short answer: “a type system centered on the use of set-theoretic types (unions, intersections, negations) that satisfy the commutativity and distributivity properties of the corresponding set-theoretic operations”. Long answer, well, there are blog posts[0], the Design Principles of the Elixir Type System paper[1] and related presentations[2, 3, 4] that talk about it at length. Giuseppe Castagna’s site has many more related papers: [LINK] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":38409954,"author":"pzmarzly","month":"2023-11","story":"Elixir at Ramp","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation by the author on Elixir's set-theoretic typing","quote":"watch the video presentation by the author[1]","text":"> The hottest news in the Elixir community is that it is currently experimenting with set theoretic type / gradual typing. Some french PHD is working on it To expand on it: you can read the proposal[0] or watch the video presentation by the author[1]. It is a very similar system to Typescript, with (at the first glance) only minor differences: \"any\" becomes \"term\", primitive constants are not singleton types (so you cannot write programs with type system alone... yet), and I expect some edge cases around generics will be handled differently (especially since you cannot specify types yourself in function calls, i.e there is no myFunction<MyType1, MyType2>()). On the other side, there are some things that the proposal seems to be doing better than Typescript. It handles type assertions without the need for \"function isX(maybeX): asserts maybeX is X { ... }\" and since pattern matching plays important role in Elixir, I expect it will have a lot of polish around that. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36320627,"author":"josevalim","month":"2023-06","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Talk giving context on set-theoretic types for Elixir","quote":"You can find more context in Guillaume’s talk","text":"We were 100% not expecting this to show up on HN front page. :) In any case, the title is accurate in that it is only a prototype and it was built by Guillaume to explore how Elixir idioms map to set-theoretic types. The UX and syntax do not reflect how the type system would look in Elixir itself (if we get a type system at all). You can find more context in Guillaume’s talk: [VIDEO] The initial research announcement: [LINK] And the paper as a deeper, more accurate, and complete resource: [LINK]"}]},"jAdG-iTilWU":{"wy":"Brought up as a joke in threads about doping and athletic performance.","m":[{"id":33111929,"author":"AtlasBarfed","month":"2022-10","story":"AI’s Grandmaster Status Overshadows Chess Scandal","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"RIP Phil Hartman, the greatest SNL actor of all time.","text":"\"He's really going to feel that in the morning when the painkillers wear off\" as the weightlifter (fake) tears his arms out from the socket. [VIDEO] RIP Phil Hartman, the greatest SNL actor of all time."},{"id":31597501,"author":"nradov","month":"2022-06","story":"East German Doping Victims Die 10 to 12 Years Earlier (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Most performance-enhancing drugs are already legal for prescription use, and in some circumstances can even be used by competitive athletes with an approved therapeutic use exception. But there's really nothing to be gained by allowing every athlete to use such drugs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19188265,"author":"mrob","month":"2019-02","story":"Muscle memory discovery ends 'use it or lose it' dogma","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"There's a relevant SNL sketch","text":"Because these drugs have risks, and if they are allowed then every athlete has to take as much as possible or they won't be competitive. It's a classic \"race to the bottom\" scenario, where everybody would be better off without the drugs, but they're forced to expose themselves to avoidable heath risk because that's what everybody else is doing. There's a relevant SNL sketch: [VIDEO]"}]},"jVeagFmmwA0":{"wy":"Cited in hydrogen debates as evidence that pressurized hydrogen tanks do not necessarily explode when punctured.","q":{"x":"The bullet pierced the tank, but it didn't go kaboom. The hydrogen just leaked out.","i":34637254,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39015186,"author":"spookie","month":"2024-01","story":"Hydrogen Cools Well, but Safety Is Crucial","stance":"reference","why":"Shows fibers and gas dispersion in Toyota's test.","quote":"you can see the fibers and the gas dispersion","text":"While I do not argue the explosive nature of it, safety can be improved by embracing how fast it disperses. Think Toyota went that path with interleaved fibers in their containers' outer enclosures, to augment that effect, so it isn't sufficient for an explosion to occur. [VIDEO] (Edit: added Toyota's test where they shot an hydrogen tank, you can see the fibers and the gas dispersion)"},{"id":37164674,"author":"esjeon","month":"2023-08","story":"Israeli Researchers Produce Green Hydrogen with 90% Efficiency","stance":"reference","why":"Toyota shot hydrogen tank to show it disperses.","quote":"Toyota literally shot their hydrogen tank with .50 cal[1].","text":"A catch is that hydrogen does become much safer in high-pressure tanks, because, when such tanks are breached, hydrogen escapes so fast that it can't react with oxygen fast enough to burst into flame. To prove this, Toyota literally shot their hydrogen tank with .50 cal[1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34637254,"author":"freetime2","month":"2023-02","story":"Seawater split to produce green hydrogen with nearly 100% efficiency","stance":"reference","why":"Toyota shot a Mirai tank with .50 cal; it only leaked.","quote":"The bullet pierced the tank, but it didn't go kaboom. The hydrogen just leaked out.","text":"Here's a video released by Toyota where they shot a hydrogen tank used in the Mirai with a 50-caliber bullet [1]. The bullet pierced the tank, but it didn't go kaboom. The hydrogen just leaked out. Now personally I think that a hydrogen leak could also be very dangerous. But so can a gasoline leak or a lithium ion battery if exposed to the air. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27966886,"author":"Clubber","month":"2021-07","story":"Toyota is quietly pushing Congress to slow the shift to electric vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Bullet breaching hydrogen tank without explosion.","quote":"Here's a video of someone breaching a full hydrogen fuel cell with a bullet. No explosion because it comes out too fast.","text":"Here's a video of someone breaching a full hydrogen fuel cell with a bullet. No explosion because it comes out too fast. [VIDEO]"}]},"pZwvrxVavnQ":{"wy":"Mostly invoked as an analogy or joke, including in discussions of opt-in consent and voluntary transactions.","m":[{"id":40079963,"author":"thegrimmest","month":"2024-04","story":"This is a teenager","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> anti-authoritarians are also anti-liberal The words are overloaded, but liberal and authoritarian are on opposite ends of the same axis on the political compass. Classical liberalism stands on the ideas of individualism and laissez-faire economics. Today these ideas are also called \"libertarian\" but the core desire is expressed in the Latin root word. I want to be free - as in without a master, elected or otherwise, who governs my life. > I find them overly vague I'd be glad to elaborate. I'm positing that it's more important our interactions be consensual than value-maximizing. Would you kill one unwilling person to save a million? a billion? I would not. > China has done quite well China is doing well because it is able to benefit from innovations generated outside its borders. It's not able to generate important innovations on its own. This was also the main problem in the USSR. The Soviets just didn't have as ready of access to innovations born of a liberal society. Where China does use its centralized authority, the results are often catastrophic, eg. its one-child policy or COVID response. > Liberalism also concentrates power Some parts of this paragraph are more true than others. There is nothing in classical liberal thought about eg. patent or copyright law. Also you're broadening the definition of power that I put forward: > Your freedom is a function of your relationship to those who can use legitimate force against you - of how much power is held over you Corpora"},{"id":37081906,"author":"thegrimmest","month":"2023-08","story":"Seattle becomes first in U.S. to protect gig workers from sudden 'deactivation'","stance":"reference","why":"Uses consent analogy to argue about voluntary transactions.","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] [2:49] I agree it's not an absolute, immutable concept, but I think it's a hell of a lot clearer than \"good\" or \"right\" or \"fair\". What makes consent is the fact that either party can opt out at any time for any/no reason. It's consensual because we are decision-making agents, and in spite of privation we can still choose to refuse to transact. Consent is violated by force or direct threat of force, not by the circumstances the transacting parties may find themselves in. If you're very horny can you still refuse to consent to sex? If you happen to be dying of thirst and I offer you a glass of water, can you refuse? You can. Are there may reasons why you would? I don't imagine so. Does it follow there there are no circumstances under which anyone would refuse? It does not."},{"id":47548457,"author":"chuckadams","month":"2026-03","story":"If you don't opt out by Apr 24 GitHub will train on your private repos","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Something like \"tea and consent","text":"Something like \"tea and consent\": [VIDEO] Yeah, it ain't sex, but it does still come down to basic respect."},{"id":28276577,"author":"mrfusion","month":"2021-08","story":"FDA grants full approval to Pfizer-BioNTech’s Covid shot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Terrible headline. Amazing they can do casually mention forcing medical treatment on people. What happened to consent? [VIDEO]"}]},"rweblFwt-BM":{"wy":"Mostly debated or criticized; one commenter found his points fair, while others questioned his climate views.","m":[{"id":24044714,"author":"theicfire","month":"2020-08","story":"Thomas Sowell interview","stance":"reference","why":"Short interview of Sowell's skepticism of climate science.","quote":"The points he makes are quite fair.","text":"Thomas Sowell is a critic of the climate change/environmental science initiatives, and it's one of the reasons I heavily question his other views. Here's a 3 min video of him talking about it: [VIDEO] The points he makes are quite fair. The science isn't perfect, yes. Often, people try to hide that the science isn't perfect, too. But the impact is roughly the product of the magnitude of the impact and the probability of the event. In this case, the magnitude is enormous and the probability is less than 100% but seemingly quite large. Dr. Sowell seems to simply argue that climate reports suggest that the probability is higher than it truly is. But if a report suggest 95%, and Dr. Sowell thinks it's more like 40%, the core issue is still enormous. I would want him to talk about why he thinks the huge effects of anthropogenic climate change are such a vanishingly small probability to the point that we shouldn't do anything about it. Here's another article by him: [LINK]"},{"id":23547118,"author":"seppin","month":"2020-06","story":"For black CEOs in Silicon Valley, humiliation is a part of doing business","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Why are you encouraging people to listen to him?","text":"I found this [VIDEO] He calls climate science a \"racket\". Why are you encouraging people to listen to him?"},{"id":13868766,"author":"the_gastropod","month":"2017-03","story":"Why Are the Highly Educated So Liberal? (2016)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"do you find his take on climate to be logical and reasonable? I do not.","text":"To be honest, I'd never heard of Thomas Sowell. So I looked him up. One of the first things I find is this interview: [VIDEO] I'm sure he has some very compelling writings. But do you find his take on climate to be logical and reasonable? I do not."}]},"sFBOQzSk14c":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke or as an early example of satire about gender identity in threads on those topics.","m":[{"id":17364302,"author":"coldtea","month":"2018-06","story":"The Death of Postmodernism and Beyond (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Monty Python skit cited as old satire of gender identities.","quote":"the Monty Python made fun of \"gender identities\" for example in the late 70s","text":"> It's the priests and priestesses of postmodernism that gets people's backs up isn't it, not so much the concept(s) itself. Yes, that. Especially since the concept itself was never meant to be something one can opt to follow or not follow -- just a description of the reality we're in. It's not that we're \"postmodernists\" that is, but that we live in a \"postmodern\" world -- and so we can't be anything aside from that. As for Peterson, I see him as something orthogonal from postmodernism. He is someone that talks from a traditional values standpoint in an era where different norms are becoming the mainstream. He doesn't say anything outrageous, in the sense that what he says would be common acceptable wisdom 30 or 50 years ago (and for tons of people still is). The whole outrage is not because he's saying something inherently outrageous, but because he dares to publicly stray from the mainstream while still being mainstream. Somebody could say the same things in a 1950 classroom or the president could say them in 1970s and nobody would bat an eyelash -- even among the leftist and more progressives. Heck, the Monty Python made fun of \"gender identities\" for example in the late 70s, and those bunch was and is as progressive as they come: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35961524,"author":"eggy","month":"2023-05","story":"Why are so many young Americans adopting fake British accents?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Eerily relevant skit from The Life of Brian","text":"Born and raised in Brooklyn, and old enough to remember when the Brooklyn accent was a detriment on job interviews, yet I've bumped into a bunch of people who sound almost like they're from Brooklyn, but they have only lived there for a few years from the midwest or other areas. Southerners tend to maintain their charming lilts. There are enough transplants, that it is not because they are being inundated with the accent, so I wonder what gives? We used to mimic Monty Python episodes and movies when I grew up in 60s and 70s in Brooklyn. Hilarious stuff. Eerily relevant skit from The Life of Brian: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33142562,"author":"Maursault","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: Are EGPUs for M1/M2 MacBook's on Linux Possible?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's tough. Mac Pro for you, if it must be Mac hardware. But why on earth Mac docker server? Obviously not for professional purposes. For play, use any x86 box you like, just because you want speed doesn't mean speed is necessary. If it must be Apple Silicon, then you can run as many virtual instances of macOS as you like on my GNU/XNU ARMac \"Linux\" (not actually Linux) release, and as many Docker Desktop for Apple Silicon as virtual instances of macOS. But if Docker had any decency, they'd release the source code to you, so you could build it native for Apple Silicon XNU. Have you considered working for Docker? Would make it a lot easier to get the source code, or just move through the ranks and make it a Docker initiative. Or suppose we agree while you can't actually run docker server on Apple Silicon, because the code doesn't exist, which is no one's fault, not even Apple's, but that you can still have the right to run docker server on Apple Silicon. We shall fight your oppressors for your right to run docker server on Apple Silicon because it is symbolic of our struggle against oppression.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28876420,"author":"1cvmask","month":"2021-10","story":"Fecal Transplants May Reverse Brain Aging in Mice","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the skit where they talk about fractious Judean (insert Israeli) politics and men giving birth","text":"That was the first thing that came to my mind when I saw the headline. Some of their shows are often seen as absurd and then they become mainstream reality years later. Sort of like some of the old Monty Python works. For example there is the skit where they talk about fractious Judean (insert Israeli) politics and men giving birth: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12929802,"author":"adamcharnock","month":"2016-11","story":"Address to the American Psychological Association on Men (2007)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’m not going to address much of the article directly, but I would like to address some of the undertones I’m perceiving in both the article and the comments here. Additionally, I don’t hear this kind of thing said around here much… Firstly: Equality is not a zero-sum game, nor does it mean treating everyone equally (yeah). To address the first component of this – the effects of feminism for women do not need result in a net-reduction of freedoms and happiness for men. Patriarchy[1] screws both men and women, each in different ways. Patriarchy is why for a long time I felt compelled to be the strong supportive male and hide my weaknesses, despite the unhappiness these roles caused me. In other men it can manifest differently, perhaps undirected anger at the world, but not really knowing why. Or perhaps a numb feeling that you’re just doing what you’re ‘supposed to do’. I strongly feel that feminism and masculism (?) stand side-by-side. They are different entities fighting mostly the same enemies. Sometimes they will need to have talks and make compromises with each other, but it can still be a cooperation. I get the impression that much of the male disquiet with feminism is coming for a place of, ‘yes, but what about me’. And this is totally reasonable. However, the answer is not to criticise feminism. If you’re envious of your neighbour’s new car, the rational response is not to attack it with a baseball bat. Rather it is either a) deal with your feelings and be happy for yo"},{"id":4269885,"author":"batista","month":"2012-07","story":"The real reason Marissa Mayer left Google: She had to","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Interesting. Citation please. I don't like giving citations, because we have this thing called Google, now. But here it is: \"The NICHD study of Early child care was designed to assess the long term outcomes of non parental care giving. Non Parental care giving involved both relatives (kinship care) and non relatives (Day care). The NICHD study was based on the ecological theory of Uri Bronfenbrenner (1979) [29]. Analysis of the effects of family and child care revealed that the characteristics of the family and the nature and quality of the mothers relationship with the child was a significantly better predictor of childrens outcome.\" [LINK] Also: [LINK] Merely quoting the easiest sources, there are lots of studies to be found online. > I believe there are a certain percentage of women who either don't willingly breast-feed or are incapable. How does it affect their babies? Are their studies? [LINK] (Correlation != causation of course, but the other causation for the effects here would be higher quality mother time, which also furthers the point) > Call it wishful thinking, and it's not about \"we are exactly the same or interchangeable\". I am willing to be corrected, but I think a man by himself is capable of raising a child in a normal way(sans breast milk). It still takes a woman to give birth to a child. And while a \"man is capable of raising a child in a normal way\", that doesn't say much. Also an orphanage is capable of raising a child -- or \"the streets\", as it happen"},{"id":4270035,"author":"irahul","month":"2012-07","story":"The real reason Marissa Mayer left Google: She had to","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I don't like giving citations, because we have this thing called Google, now. But here it is: This thing called Google returns results from the web, and any crackpot can have a page indexed. You are not required to, but the onus of citing is on you when you are making a claim which isn't known to the demographic in question. > That said, the implication that a mother being close to a new born child is completely equal to a father being close to a new born child is PC ideology. >> The NICHD study of Early child care was designed to assess the long term outcomes of non parental care giving What relevance you non-parental care giving has to do with a father raising a child? [LINK] > Attachment theory describes the dynamics of long-term relationships between humans. Its most important tenet is that an infant needs to develop a relationship with at least one primary caregiver for social and emotional development to occur normally. Why are you quoting \"maternal deprivation\" when it is superseded by \"attachment theory\"? Attachment theory talks about \"at least one parent\" and your claims are mother is more important. > [LINK] >> (Correlation != causation of course, but the other causation for the effects here would be higher quality mother time, which also furthers the point) You are conveniently ignoring the studies which didn't find causation or correlation. > Call it wishful thinking, and it's not about \"we are exactly the same or interchangeable\". I am willing to be corrected, "}]},"sforhbLiwLA":{"wy":"Mostly quoted as a joke or a snapshot of Microsoft's dated marketing style rather than recommended.","m":[{"id":48857075,"author":"HPsquared","month":"2026-07","story":"Train SIM Created by Just One Person Is Being Called the Best Ever Made","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Order today! PO box 286 DOS... Except in Nebraska!","text":"I just think of Steve Ballmer's ad for Windows 1.0 (1986) \"Order today! PO box 286 DOS... Except in Nebraska!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":3145705,"author":"coldarchon","month":"2011-10","story":"The Volkswagen Group is using IE6 as standard for displaying web pages","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"AND - CAN YOU BELIEVE IT, REVERSI!","text":"Somebody please explain to me what new browsers have that a car manufacturing corporation needs for its daily business. Flash games? WebGL? Or a bit lower, popup-blocker and google search field? HELLO? Obviously this entry is for unexperienced people of the IT, which neglect the fact that your stuff is only safe as long someone hasn't found it's weakness. [VIDEO] AND - CAN YOU BELIEVE IT, REVERSI!"},{"id":4887908,"author":"l0c0b0x","month":"2012-12","story":"Microsoft's DroidRage Twitter campaign goes painfully wrong","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You'd think they would have grown out of this pathetic style a long time ago","text":"I guess am not the only one that thinks Microsoft's marketing strategy is and has always been pure garbage. You'd think they would have grown out of this pathetic style a long time ago: [VIDEO]"}]},"vbnySBqioB0":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it only as an early example of a politically motivated drone-style attack on Kasparov.","m":[{"id":6410117,"author":"krrrh","month":"2013-09","story":"German chancellor’s drone “attack” shows the threat of weaponized UAVs","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier drone incident targeting Kasparov","quote":"There was a similar politically-motivated drone \"attack\" against Gary Kasparov 5 years ago","text":"There was a similar politically-motivated drone \"attack\" against Gary Kasparov 5 years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5151187,"author":"simias","month":"2013-02","story":"OpenSource Nano Quadcopter Kit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Those things are not exactly silent you know. Nor are they difficult to stop/intercept. Case in point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4400290,"author":"eps","month":"2012-08","story":"Garry Kasparov Arrested By Russian Police ","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"He's been ridiculed and ignored by the very same regime for as long as he was a supposed thorn in its side. Here's one example - [VIDEO] - a flying penis incident. All in all, Kasparov has an incredible brain that, regrettably, peaked in its performance and application when he was a chess player. I left Russia over 10 years ago (meaning that I've spent most of my adult life in the Western world), and yet even when I was there it was bloody obvious that Kasparov was on the US payroll. The fact that a smart and practical person like him continued on the same path only cements my impression."}]},"vidzkYnaf6Y":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference to the politician's fallacy, to mock reasoning of the form 'something must be done, this is something, so do it'.","m":[{"id":47182908,"author":"cjs_ac","month":"2026-02","story":"New California law requires age verification for all OS accounts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Someone has fallen victim to Politician's Logic","text":"Ignoring all the tedious 'no, you're a bad person for having different priorities and beliefs to me' comments that this will inevitably inspire, I have to ask: why does the operating system need to be involved in this? The intended target of the regulation seems to be app stores. Someone has fallen victim to Politician's Logic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42928028,"author":"lolinder","month":"2025-02","story":"Open Euro LLM: Open LLMs for Transparent AI in Europe","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Your formulation sounds like politician's logic","text":"> legislation is kind of the point of a government in the first place I would personally consider legislation to be but one means to an end, with the point of a (democratic) government actually being to ensure stability and prosperity for its citizens. In that framework, \"leading with legislation\" doesn't make any sense—you can lead with results, but the legislation is not itself a result! Lead with development or lead with standard of living or lead with civil rights, but don't lead with legislation. Your formulation sounds like politician's logic: \"something must be done, this is something, therefore we must do it\". Legislation as an end in itself. Very interesting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13507022,"author":"braythwayt","month":"2017-01","story":"Refugees Detained at U.S. Airports, Prompting Legal Challenges","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The politician's fallacy","text":"The politician's fallacy: \"Something must be done. This is something. Therefore we must do it. But doing the wrong thing is worse than doing nothing.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"wsixsRI-Sz4":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a dated source for Musk's past claims, such as autonomous driving being essentially solved, and his rocket reuse analogies.","m":[{"id":35864202,"author":"csydas","month":"2023-05","story":"People are trying to claim real videos are deepfakes. The courts are not amused","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is already done, at least with many modern courts. Substantiating your evidence has to happen via a pretty specific process, and there is an entire process for entering stuff into evidence with rules regarding chain of custody, validation, etc. When it comes to evidence, it's not a specific claim against anyone, it's an item that is being introduced to support a claim. That should be validated and checked before it even gets to the court room. If either counsel believes it to be fraudulent, they need to review it and make the claim. From the article, this was not really the case; instead, a video of a conference with many attendees and participants showed Musk making the claim in question.[0] Tesla's (or Musk's? Not sure) lawyers made a very weak statement: \"[Musk], like many public figures, is the subject of many ‘deepfake’ videos and audio recordings that purport to show him saying and doing things he never actually said or did,” Tesla said\" This is not really an argument towards anything about the video; it doesn't outright say it's a deepfake (likely because it's not), it doesn't provide any evidence to a claim that the video in question is a deepfake, it does nothing to establish who would have produced this deepfake, it doesn't question the YouTube channel owners, Recode, on if they are ready to stand by their statement, etc. In this instance, the lawyers defending Tesla/Musk are trying to muddy the waters; they aren't bold enough to say Recode made a deepfake, the"},{"id":18610315,"author":"Lanzaa","month":"2018-12","story":"SpaceX Launches 19th Rocket in a Year, a Company Record","stance":"reference","why":"Musk uses pallet-of-cash analogy for first stage.","quote":"In regards to the first stage (booster stage) at Code Conference 2016","text":"Musk has used the pallet of cash analogy several times. Here is Musk using it in regards to saving the fairing @ ISS Research and Development Conference 2017: [LINK] In regards to the first stage (booster stage) at Code Conference 2016: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12509461,"author":"firasd","month":"2016-09","story":"Elon Musk on How to Build the Future","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think he says something about spending time with his kids, etc in this Recode interview (don't remember clearly though) [VIDEO]"},{"id":11859381,"author":"josephg","month":"2016-06","story":"Electric vehicle battery costs rapidly declining, Tesla cited as leading","stance":"reference","why":"Musk says Tesla could easily extend range.","quote":"Elon Musk claims the same thing about Tesla","text":"Apple could easily make a laptop with better battery - it would just be bigger, heavier and more expensive. Elon Musk claims the same thing about Tesla - that he could easily increase the range on Tesla's cars if they wanted to: [VIDEO] . But with a range of ~300 miles he thinks they're fine."},{"id":19942137,"author":"rohit2412","month":"2019-05","story":"Third fatal Tesla Autopilot crash renews questions about system","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's Elon musk in 2016 proclaiming self driving to be easy and solved.","text":"Citation needed? Coast to coast autonomous drive, cross country summon mode, full self driving on highways from entry to exit, the fsd fake demo video with \"driver is there only for legal reasons\", million robotaxis that earn you 30k$ a year from 2020, lidar is a fools errand etc etc. You need more? Here's Elon musk in 2016 proclaiming self driving to be easy and solved. Showing entire lack of understanding what driving is like and why computers fail at it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18364486,"author":"sabertoothed","month":"2018-11","story":"Elon Musk: The Recode interview","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Kara Swisher is a terrible interviewer.","text":"Kara Swisher is a terrible interviewer. She was terrible here [1], and she was terrible in this interview. You have an hour with Elon Musk and you ask what BFR means again (knowing the answer) and don't know that they attempt to catch fairings? Why does she always look like she did not do her research? Why do I never learn anything new if she interviews? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17185094,"author":"theCricketer","month":"2018-05","story":"Tesla in autopilot mode crashes into parked Laguna Beach police cruiser","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I basically consider autonomous driving to be a solved problem\".","text":"Elon Musk has constantly underestimated the difficulty of autonomous driving. This video ( [VIDEO] ) shows Elon Musk, two years ago, saying the following: \"I basically consider autonomous driving to be a solved problem\". \"A Model S and Model X can drive with greater safety than a person, already. Right now.\" \"We are less than two years away from complete autonomy\"."}]},"yO9VRtrTJwc":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joking reference when threads discuss Amazon warehouse working conditions and surveillance.","m":[{"id":33269293,"author":"oliveshell","month":"2022-10","story":"The Rise of ‘Luxury Surveillance’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(This song may be familiar to South Park viewers.)","text":"For the curious: besides the Pullman towns, there were many other “company towns” [1] in the United States, owned by various companies [2]. Some of the most exploitive ones were owned by mining firms, as immortalized in the famous protest song “Sixteen Tons”, with its refrain “I owe my soul to the company store”. [3] (This song may be familiar to South Park viewers.) [4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31783750,"author":"type0","month":"2022-06","story":"Leaked Amazon memo warns the company is running out of people to hire","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"South Park had a realistic vision on how it is to work at Amazon Fulfillment Center","text":"> many commercials lately about how great it is to work for Amazon. South Park had a realistic vision on how it is to work at Amazon Fulfillment Center [VIDEO]"},{"id":31785401,"author":"philsnow","month":"2022-06","story":"Leaked Amazon memo warns the company is running out of people to hire","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I dunno, it seemed fairly rosy. They could have twisted the knife quite a bit more: there were a couple 3-second interactions where people are buying things [0][1], if they had been denominated in \"hours of service\" rather than USD, with the Company knowing what their hours balance was, and having the balance tick down and go negative... that would have been dark. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29007662,"author":"type0","month":"2021-10","story":"Amazon has patented a system that would put workers in a cage, on top of a robot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Amazon should trademark the term Wage Cage. At least now it's not been implemented yet, but I would imagine it is very unfulfilling to work at Amazon fulfillment center [VIDEO]"}]},"z4AeoAWGJBw":{"wy":"Cited as an example of false copyright claims hitting even popular creators.","m":[{"id":35604526,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2023-04","story":"Nintendo Is Taking Down My Videos [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Someone filed a false copyright claim against TheFatRat on one of his own songs","text":"> YouTube may have taken down the videos but they also allow him (because of the DMCA) to file a counter-claim and make a claim that the videos aren't infringing. Naive of you to think that works. When you counter-claim, YouTube basically asks the original claimant \"Are you sure?\", and of course they're gonna say yes. Exibit A: [VIDEO] - TLDW: Someone filed a false copyright claim against TheFatRat on one of his own songs . TheFatRat counterclaimed, and the dispute was not approved. YouTube does not at any point make a judgement call."},{"id":29929375,"author":"2pEXgD0fZ5cF","month":"2022-01","story":"Music industry is suing youtube-dl hosters","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It even happens to people with songs with millions of views","text":"> the starving artist The \"starving\" or small time artist is an excuse that is useful for corporations when they want to manipulate public opinion. No music label cares even the slightest bit about individual artists or their fair payment, they care about megahits and big sellers. If you want to support your favourite artist that isn't already a millionaire or multi-millionare paying for spotify isn't it. To actually support \"starving artists\" visit their shows, buy some merch (however even this isn't the case anymore for some) or look for independent artists on bandcamp (bandcamp takes 15% of a sold album). If all you do is pay for spotify or something like that you are not supporting a \"starving artist\" any more than someone downloading the songs, maybe even less because sharing the music on the right filesharing forums might at least lead to some of the sales (merch/concert) above. For anyone interested have a look at [LINK] To add to that: Big labels hurt small time artists immensely. If you are an artist on youtube getting hit with a (false) copyright claim by a large label is a constant threat to you. It even happens to people with songs with millions of views [1]. We have reached a point where using certain chords or chord progression can get you stomped by music labels. Music labels are not in any way friends or allys to \"starving artists\", only to millionaire artists. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27004809,"author":"2pEXgD0fZ5cF","month":"2021-05","story":"Piano teacher gets copyright claim for Beethoven’s Moonlight Sonata [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"music artists get their own original music stolen by Youtube who then proceeds to hand it to someone else","text":"> here artists get denied ownership of their own expression of public domain music Hell why stop there, music artists get their own original music stolen by Youtube who then proceeds to hand it to someone else, for anyone interested in a popular example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26087520,"author":"2pEXgD0fZ5cF","month":"2021-02","story":"Police playing music while being filmed, seemingly to trigger copyright filters","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Back in 2018 TheFatRat got his music claimed by some randoms","text":"This is the worst part of this whole copyright mess, defenders of youtube constantly preach the \"They have to do this\", \"they are forced to do this\" etc. defense. However the way Youtube handles dmca and copyright claims means that if you are a smaller musician, a big corporation can just steal your music and profit off it. Good luck if you don't live in the US and don't have the money to take back what is yours. From that point you can basically just hope that you make the news so that a human at Google looks into the mess they created for once. Just leaving a reminder of how infuriation Youtube copyright handling can become even for larger channels: Back in 2018 TheFatRat got his music claimed by some randoms and Youtube just gave it to them on a silver platter [VIDEO] . TheFatRat was a popular youtuber even back then, and even for him the process was hard. Now imagine how that situation would have played out for the average musician. When people talk about \"protecting the musicians\" they typically only mean the richest musicians, the top few % and the largest record labels. No one cares about regular musicians. The same logic also means that dmca claims can be easily weaponized by any entity as long as they choose their targets strategically (or own enough money to tank the situation in which a target fights back, but even then that can create more damage for the target)."},{"id":24574207,"author":"ostravak","month":"2020-09","story":"They're deleting my channel, but they don't know why? [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Composing your own song, producing it and publishing it via YT is enough.","text":"You need not use somebody other's work at all to get into trouble. Composing your own song, producing it and publishing it via YT is enough. [VIDEO] (2018, now resolved)"}]}});
